At the end of the exchanges, the Prague Stock Exchange switched to the negative
After Tuesday morning’s fall, the Prague Stock Exchange rectified its losses.
Transactions with ČEZ shares reached almost half of the usual daily exchanges.
Tuesday, from the beginning of the exchanges, the Prague stock exchange returned to its fall the day before, when it lost almost six percent.
This time, the decline is due to the drop in shares on the Wall Street.
The reaction of the market to the outcome of the vote of the U.S. House of Representatives, which refused to pass the stabilization plan of the U.S. financial sector, is thus also perceptible to us.
Falling Stocks in Asia
The Asian stock exchanges experienced a dramatic fall on Tuesday, even if the clues managed, during the day, to erase some of the losses.
Hang Seng’s Hang Seng index lost four percent during trading, but later it cleared some of the losses and reduced the decline to nearly 2.5 percent.
The Hang Seng China Enterpers Index following the Hongkong Stock Exchange’s movement lost 3.8 per cent, with markets closed in Shanghai.
Shares in the Sydney Stock Exchange lost more than five percent, but we finally managed to reduce losses to 4.3 percent.
According to its index, the Taiwan Stock Exchange lost 3.6 percent.
“Hesitation about rescue action in the U.S. will affect financial markets around the world,” said Joseph Yam, head of Hong Kong’s Monetary Office.
Although Hong Kong is part of China, it establishes its own monetary policy, that is, regardless of China's central bank.
Interest rates in Hong Kong have the same level as in the United States.
Australian Prime Minister Kevin Rudd said U.S. lawmakers would soon have to resume negotiations and pass the proposal to support the financial system.
In other words, there would be a risk that other countries would be affected.
The bloodbath of American stocks
On Monday, the U.S. House of Representatives rejected the plan to support the financial system, to which it should have devoted up to US$700 billion (nearly US$12 bilions from Kč).
Legislators thus deny the call for support of President George Bush’s project.
According to Bush, the project should address the main causes of the financial crisis and help stabilize the entire economy.
U.S. equities went through a bloodbath on Monday, and major equity indices experienced the biggest drop in the last 20 years.
The Dow Jones Index lost nearly seven per cent, having experienced such a decline for the last time in 1987.
The clue already lost during the vote, but when it appeared that the proposal was not adopted, it was in free fall.
Congress ceded: U.S. government can inject US$700 billion into banks
U.S. Congressional leaders and President George W. Bush’s government have agreed on a broader form of the U.S. financial system financial aid agreement.
It will be put to the vote early next week.
U.S. legislators carried out a turning point in talks on the adoption of a bailout in the form of 700 billion dollars (about 12 trillion kroron), granted to the U.S. financial system.
However, success is not safe.
Indeed, before they can publish the final version of the law and submit it to the vote, Congressmans must complete a number of details of the agreement.
The support plan for the financial system will be discussed Monday in the House of Representatives.
Barney Frank, chairman of the House Banking and Financial Regulations Committee, told Reuters on Sunday.
According to sources, by Wednesday at the earliest, the plan should a priori be put to a vote in the Senate.
According to economists, the announcement that the rescue plan will be adopted should be a major first psychological factor in reviving financial markets.
However, given the complexity of the mechanisms through which market aid can be effective, "disappointment".
Paulson: The plan must be effective
“We’ve made great progress.
We’ve settled our differing views on the form the market stabilization package should have,” Nancy Pelosi told the Bloomberg Agency.
According to her, the final vote could be made as early as Sunday
In the night of Saturday to Sunday, representatives of legislators met with U.S. Finance Minister Henry Paulson, in order to give the bottom of the government a final form.
It is expected to repurchase unsaleable real estate assets from financial companies that drive financial companies into significant losses and threaten the stability of the system in its entirety.
"We are about to agree on a plan that will work and that also needs to be effective in the market.
We still need to continue this market recovery plan, but I think we’re there,” Paulson said.
A Signal for Asian Exchanges
The global financial crisis has a significant impact on falling equity markets.
According to the Democratic senator do not evade Harry Reid, legislators ensure that Congress reaches a definitive deal as early as Sunday.
They want to calm investors ahead of the start of trade in Asian financial markets, which are located in the first hourly zone, where Congress’s decision could influence Monday’s exchanges.
However, for the moment, it is impossible to specify with certainty when the two Houses of the U.S
Legislators hope that it will be adopted in the coming days.
However, there will still be many changes in the law.
The total amount used by the system must be divided into two parts.
The first 350 billion dollars, as President George Bush wanted, must be available as soon as possible.
But, if Congress will have the impression that the program does not fulfill its function, it has the opportunity to block the granting of the remaining amount, following two steps: first an additional 100 billion dollars, then, finally, 350 billion dollars.
Mr. Bush commended the progress in the negotiations
Although the president can veto this decision, Congress has the opportunity to put it as a minority.
Despite these changes, the main idea of the program, which was to obtain finances for the redemption of bad real estate securities, whose value fell, because hundreds of thousands of Americans are not able to pay their mortgages, was maintained.
“All work is done.
The House of Representatives is expected to be able to proceed on Sunday to pass the law, and the Senate on Monday,” Republican Senator Judd Gregg said.
U.S. President George Bush himself is pleased with the progress in negotiations.
Spokesman Tony Fratto said Bush was pleased with Saturday’s progress and appreciated “the two-party effort to stabilize our financial markets and protect our economy.”
Limitation of golden parachutes
Senate Banking Committee Chairman Christopher Dodd, chief negotiator, says legislators have agreed on the accountability of the financial program, the protection of contributors against losses, the relief agreement, the progress of the plan on the axis of time.
The new Act also provides for the limitation of said golden parachutes for the directors of the companies participating in the programme and establishes a committee, which will supervise the fund.
It should be managed by the Ministry of Finance.
“We’ve worked very hard on the issue and have made significant progress towards a deal that will work and be effective for the market and for all Americans,” Paulson said.
The plan also includes assistance for real estate owners, who are having to pay.
The government is expected to negotiate new installments in the case of mortgages that it will redeem, while reducing the monthly payments of debtors and allowing them to keep their homes.
Marek: Psychological help
According to Patria Finance chief economist David Marek, the proposed plan is a good idea, but its achievement is going to be very difficult.
"And this is due to the fact that we do not know what will be the mechanism and the price of the buyback of problem assets.
This point will be decisive to decide on the sharing of losses between the financial sector and the government," Marek said this week at server iHNed.cz.
If the plan will be adopted now, or early next week, isn't, according to Marek, that important.
“It’s much more important for the congressmans to agree this week that they support this plan and dedicate it as soon as possible.
This psychological significance of the agreement, providing for the adoption of the plan, matters the most at the moment," Marek stressed.
A struggle pushed to the extreme: MP3 players against mobiles.
We'll advise you, how to choose
While virtually all mobiles know how to play MP3 files, no MP3 player knows how to phone.
It seems clear that it’s better to buy only a phone.
However, there are enough reasons to obtain an independent reader.
The choice depends entirely on the mode of use and requirements of the future owner.
It would be absurd to buy for the house an expensive high-end gramophone to put Maxim Turbulenc's new album.
On the one hand, such musical products don't come out on vinyl record, and, from a quality standpoint, there's no objective reason for this to happen.
However, if there are in your shelves piled up vinyl records, with rock art, jazz or blues, you at least probably dream of such a gramophone.
The same applies to music during travels, i.e. compressed music, to put it in a simplified way - music in MP3.
Again, there are categories based on price and quality.
For one, it may be unacceptable to spend eight thousand crowns on a reader, in the same way that a Pink Floyd fan would find it difficult to support headphones purchased for twenty wreaths at the market.
These are two extremes, between which there is enough room for everyone.
There's a wide choice.
The battery life is also decisive
Some high-end music players are capable of many more things than just playing music, but these are unknown features, of course, of most mobiles.
There is no current device as universal as the phone.
In addition to the primary functions, telephone calls and the sending of SMS, he is able to read music and movies, to record them, he has a camera of variable quality and can, for example, be used for navigation.
And if we take into account Smartphones and communicators, you will have in your pocket a small computer.
Since no fridge knows how to grill meat and no television will take photographs, we practically witness a miracle.
However, the universal nature of the mobile is not without drawbacks.
The larger the number of functions you use, the smaller the interval between two reloads.
And playing music is one of the most important energy bottles.
Are few mobiles who are able to play music for ten to twelve hours, in most cases, this figure is significantly lower.
In this respect, music players (especially the most rudimentary) are much more efficient.
In the case of cheap mobiles, count that they will only play music for a few hours.
If, in addition, you sometimes call from time to time, procure, to be sure, a second recharge for work - this being valid even for more expensive devices.
There will not be a problem for MP3 players, which are capable, as a result of recharging, of continuous operation of at least 9 hours, including the less efficient.
Another problem arises during the recording of the music in the mobile.
Unfortunately, that a limited part of the devices has a universal USB connector, especially since not everyone can be directly connected to the computer, without special software.
We can, of course, record music directly on the memory card, but few card readers know how to do it without reduction, being given the tiny dimensions of the memory cards used in mobiles.
When it comes to readers, you don’t need, with any exceptions (iPod), any software.
It is enough to fill, through the USB connector, the music device from the computer.
Most mobiles and readers will create themselves a media library classified according to the performer's name, the title of the album, the style, or according to the user's judgment.
Some devices display only an index with a tree structure, like the Windows Explorer, others are capable of both (iriver, Samsung, Sony, Cowon, smart phones with operating system).
Readers are easier to handle, mobiles have more know-how
In the choice of a concrete device, be particularly interested in the lifetime of the accumulator, the capacity of the memory, the quality and the type of sound output and, of course, the ease of control.
If the music is for you, essentially, a background noise and if during the day you are going to listen that for a limited time, for example by taking the tram, you only need any mobile reading MP3.
Virtually all mobiles are capable of this today.
You don’t even have to pay too much attention to music formats that the mobile or player is able to play.
All can read MP3s and the vast majority of the music that will reach you will have precisely this format.
It is one of the most popular formats and apart from it, the vast majority of devices also manage to read WMA music.
The AAFC (format used by the firm Apple and its iPods), meanwhile, is less common.
High-end mobiles also allow, for example, the purchase of music online.
You just have to visit, from the mobile phone, the web pages of the operator (or those of another provider) and buy the song or album in question.
This service alone should be a convincing reason to obtain a musical mobile.
The offer of the operators is unfortunately miserable and it is limited, in most cases, to radio tubes quickly made.
Prices aren't the lowest either.
Nevertheless, we generally consider that this way of buying music (whether by GSM, the internet, or even the wireless internet) will dominate the music industry in the future.
Most people always carry their mobiles on themselves.
Whenever you want to listen to something, you just have to take the mobile out of your pocket, while you’ve forgotten your MP3 player at home.
However, it is also important if you always have headphones or a handsfree with connection.
If this is not the case, you can use built-in speakers, which almost all mobiles have, but a minimum of MP3 players.
For many, the main advantage of musical mobiles is their price.
The cheapest normally cost less than three thousand.
For a price of three four thousand crowns, one can have an already very suitable walkman.
However, in most cases, you will be constrained by the lack of output for regular headphones.
The price of the best phones and the best equipped, also equipped with an input for ordinary headphones, does not exceed fifteen thousand.
Readers, on the other hand, have the advantage of having an easy order.
Truly anyone manages to ensure the command of the most rudimentary readers - who do not necessarily correspond to the cheapest (cf. our choice).
A minimum number of functions; and the resemblance of the control with that of a cassette station, commonly used in the past, or of a gramophone, is obvious.
There is no risk of collision in the form of a connected mobile internet by chance, followed by a huge data transmission invoice.
Which device to choose
Even the cheapest mobiles (e.g
Even taking into account the additional purchase of such a card, you will not exceed two thousand crowns.
But be careful.
The basic models in principle only support cards with a maximum capacity of 2 GB.
Only the most expensive ones even with 8 GB memory cards.
If you already own a mobile and still want to buy a cheap MP3 player, take a Cowon, a Creative, a Sony, a Samsung, an Emgeton, or an irrivant.
Depending on capacity (max. 2 GB), you do not have to spend more than 1,100 crowns.
If you’re one of the most demanding listeners, choose specialized mobile-walkmans (Sony Ericsson, some Nokia and Samsung).
These devices are provided with independent control keys for MP3, and this is how the ease of the command is close to that of an independent MP3 player.
Sony Ericsson, whose walkman series mobiles have the same user menu as MP3 players sold separately, is the one that has gone the best in this area.
The same service, in the same price category, but with a memory capacity (up to 16 GB) and a lifetime of the accumulator (normally 15 hours, up to 35 hours), is provided by an MP3 player produced by one of the brands mentioned above.
A first virtual operator will reach the Czech Republic this very year.
Maybe
The mobile market in the Czech Republic is relatively advanced, with our operators offering virtually the same services as their partners in Western Europe.
Despite this, we are still missing something - a virtual operator, which would force traditional operators to lower prices.
He may not miss him for a long time.
We’ve talked time and again about Mobil.cz the fact that Czech operators are one of the most expensive in Europe.
The ČTÚ (Czech Telecommunications Office), which pointed out that the price of an SMS was higher with us than in the rest of the Union, confirmed this.
For example, as a result of the European Commission's latest steps in the field of the regulation of roaming charges, a paradoxical situation is likely to occur - SMS sent from abroad will be cheaper for some customers of Czech mobile operators than in the country.
How is it possible that prices in the Czech Republic remain so high, compared to Europe?
The answer is simple, it lacks a competition that really wants to get into the fight.
This may seem paradoxical, given that we still have three mobile networks and a penetration that far exceeds 100%.
On the contrary, mobile operators like to complain that the Czech market is extremely competitive.
In this respect, Pavel Dvořák, the President of the Council of the Czech Telecommunications Office (ČTÚ), who has repeatedly stated that in the Czech Republic there is no more room for another operator, is chorus with the operators.
It is probably true that a conventional operator with its own network is apparently no longer very lucky (by the way, now it would hardly find frequencies).
However, there is still a possibility - a virtual operator.
It was precisely the entry of virtual operators into the mobile market that led to a considerable drop in prices, to which conventional operators must subsequently also react.
However, even before the entry of troublemakers such as Tesco Mobile or Virgin Mobile on the market, voices similar to those we hear at home today allowed themselves to be heard in Britain.
However, it appeared that there was still room for such operators and that they could have a real success.
We don't have to look too far to find examples.
In February we informed you of the launch in Poland of the virtual operator Mobilking.
In just six months, this operator managed to earn more than 100 thousand customers and become the country’s fifth-largest operator.
Add that behind the Mobilking, there is the financial group Penta, which owns among others the U:fon in the Czech Republic.
The advantage of the virtual operators being the possibility of focusing on a much more concrete target group.
In order for their operation to be efficient, they indeed need far fewer customers than a conventional operator.
They are, in fact, free from the relatively high construction, operation and maintenance costs of the mobile network.
That’s whale around the world, virtual operators loom as low-cost operators – they offer cheap calls and SMS, which is often counterbalanced by a limited service offering.
For example Tesco Mobile offers SMS in the UK at least twice as expensive as its competitors.
However, as shown in the example of Mobilking in Poland, betting on an unusual target group can also be a good blow of advertising.
Indeed, this operator specifically targets men.
“Real men choose Mobilking – an operator they like and can trust,” says David Seibert, the CEO of Mobile Entertainment Company, operating this virtual operator.
It is difficult to say, if such a concept would be likely to walk at home too.
Miroslav Čepický, Vodafone’s spokesman, in doubt: “The Czech market is relatively small for a segmental approach, on which most virtual operators are based.
Especially since it is relatively saturated from the point of view of the supply of services."
In the Czech Republic, there is no chance, mobile operators say
However, it seems almost certain that if there will be a virtual operator in the Czech Republic, it will not be Mobilking.
"We are discussing the expansion of this successful project, but in the Czechia the negotiations are not very promotive.
The launch of Mobilking will instead take place in other countries in our area, "said in a telephone interview for Mobil.cz Jana Studničková, spokesman for the Penta financial group.
It thus indirectly confirmed that the operators in the Czech Republic were, in any case, not in favour of this idea.
On the contrary, they are trying to do everything possible so that a potential competitor cannot appear.
The GTS Novera group, which would like to take a new step in order to become a real competitor of Telefonika in the field of enterprise customers, does not hide, for long enough, its intention to launch a virtual mobile operator.
"We really want to be able to offer our customers not only fixed telecommunications services, but also mobile.
And the sooner it will be better.
We have already held negotiations on this possibility on several occasions with all Czech mobile operators.
With some, we have managed to move forward in the negotiations,” said Pavel Kaidl Group spokesman.
Perhaps the future is promising.
According to unofficial information, it seems that we could perhaps host the first virtual operator already by the end of the year.
But for the moment, his creation is accompanied by complete silence.
“Right now, we don’t think there’s room in the Czech market for a virtual operator as such.
The Czech market is in comparison with others very specific.
For the moment, no subject with a complex and serious project in this area has contacted us, "said Vlastimil Sršeň of the press department of Telefonika O2, in his statement for Mobil.cz.
Other operators also refer to the specificity of the Czech market.
T-Mobile spokeswoman Martina Kemrová said in a rare agreement with O2's statement: "We have not yet registered a viable and bilaterally advantageous project, which could serve as a basis."
"It is practically excluded that a virtual operator will appear in the T-Mobile network by the end of the year.
To operate in such a limited period of time, we should now have a concrete agreement."
Vodafone instead directs his focus on completing requests to obtain his own 3G license.
Miroslav Čepický says about this: "We are open to negotiations with potential virtual operators, and especially in the area of shared costs and infrastructure.
This is what, in our opinion, means the most contributions from the point of view of customers.
Vodafone's main strategy in this area being the discovery of a partner to set up a common network for a fast mobile internet."
The responsible authorities: in line with the operators
The approach of mobile operators is easily understandable, the entry of another operator would mean an increase in competition, would have led to a drop in prices and therefore a decrease in their profit.
So what's the point of facilitating the advent of such a competitor?
When it comes to their interests, the operators know how to understand each other as thieves in fair. The chicane accompanying the creation of the U:fon also testifies to this.
The greatest apparent pressure on the ČTÚ, during the creation of the U:fon, was that exerted by the company T-Mobile.
This is evidenced by publicly available information from the ČTÚ web pages.
However, today, the U:fon lends itself as an argument not to accept a virtual operator.
Martina Kemrová said: "Competition in the mobile market has, moreover, recently been amplified by the entry into the market of a fourth mobile operator.
Considering the character of the RT mobile market, it is obvious that the entry of an additional player would subject him, and especially his services, to high requirements."
However, given the above attitude of mobile operators, the indifference of Czech regulators seems disconcerting.
However, it is not only a question of the ČTÚ attitude, which, unlike Britain and other developed countries, unfortunately does not have the power to intervene in economic competition in the Czech Republic.
It belongs exclusively to the Office for the Protection of Economic Competition (ÚOHS).
However, the latter adopted, faced with this problem, his typical position.
"The entry of a virtual operator into the mobile market would be possible following the conclusion of an agreement between a mobile operator and the virtual operator in question regarding the rental of the necessary infrastructure.
No such agreement has been reached at the moment, because the offer of potential interested parties has a priori not shown enough interest for conventional operators, "said in response to our question Kristián Chalupa from ÚOHS Department of External Relations.
A statement worded in this way would not be surprising by representatives of the operators.
But on the part of an Office, which must represent the interests of consumers and, among other things, also take care, that no one benefits from its position in the market, such a declaration is more than confusing.
Kristián Chalupa adds: "Abroady, where virtual operators are normally present, we can see that their entry into the market has contributed to a drop in the prices of mobile telephone services."
However, putting pressure in the direction of an honest functioning of the market and a drop in prices is apparently not part of ÚOHS's interests.
What is indeed shocking is the almost touching concord of the three mobile operators, which stems from their reactions.
The question, if this is really a coincidence, arises quite naturally.
In the same way, a gasoline price strictly identical to all service stations is probably due to chance and the hard struggle of the competition.
"In this respect, the Office did not identify any signs of anti-competitive behaviour, so it has no reason to intervene in one way or another.
None of the operators has a dominant position, therefore none has an increased corresponding responsibility for its behaviour in the market.
The tools necessary to open up the mobile market to virtual operators are probably even lacking in the telecommunications regulator," Chaloupa said in a statement.
The interpretation of the dominant position in the market is one of the many points of discord.
Of course, there is an analysis of the relevant markets developed by the ČTÚ, according to which telecommunications constitute, from the point of view of economic competition, a single market.
None of the Czech operators has a dominant position in such a market.
However, some legal experts propose a different vision: for the creation of a virtual operator to be possible, it is necessary to obtain, each time, the consent of the network operator.
And the motival operator logically has the monopoly on its network.
This is how it necessarily plays a dominant role in a certain segment of its activity.
However, even without admitting this argument, it should be at least worrying for the ÚOHS that three mobile operators visibly proceed in a similar way.
However, complaints from many stakeholders (including GTS Novera, already mentioned) show that they face similar constraints in negotiations with all operators.
Breaking responsibility on the ČTÚ is a fairly common turn in communications, through which the ÚOHS washes the hands of the case.
We've already encountered this kind of attitude in several similar cases.
Unfortunately, it has not been possible to pass into the novelization of the recently adopted Telecommunications Law, an expansion of ČTÚ's powers in the field of economic competition in telecommunications, although many specialists promised an improvement in the situation.
This, the representatives of ÚOHS must know very well, but their interests are apparently elsewhere.
When no one is looking, it can work
That said, the operation of a kind of pseudo-virtual operators, many in Czech mobile networks, remains unnoticed.
They are companies publishing their own price lists of mobile telephone services, but they are only partially public.
In most cases, there is talk of multi-level marketing firms.
Particular mobile telephone services are offered for example by Amway, Bohemia Telecom, Starlife and many others.
The price lists of these companies generally only reach elected officials.
The magic trick consists in the fact that the company does in front of the operator figure of a classical corporate customer, who orders a large quantity of SIM cards.
As an important customer, she gets price offers (and others) specials.
What is problematic is that the commercial conditions of all Czech mobile operators prohibit such resale.
"If it happened what you mentioned, it could be a violation of the contractual conditions that we would consider and that could lead to a stoppage of collaboration with the company in question," replied our question relating to this problem Martina Kemrová.
Nevertheless, it is quite unlikely that an echo of such an activity did not reach the heads of mobile operators.
Especially in the case of the three companies mentioned they can be thousands of customers.
An operator, however, does not rule against such proceedings.
Indeed, the activities of these firms bring them non-negligible profits, and, at the same time, as opposed to real virtual operators, they do not threaten them with their own official marketing.
Miroslav Čepický indicates the possible problems: "The law allows such speculations, nevertheless, the services of these companies are used only by a negligible percentage of customers.
In addition, they are forced to admit that the SIM card in their phone is not registered under their name, so they cannot fully dispose of it, in the manner of the ordinary customers of mobile operators."
But this is precisely what should seem strange in ČTÚ.
In ÚOHS, there is another question at ÚOHS.
If the mobile operators turn a blind eye to such a procedure, why are they blocking the creation of a virtual operator?
It should be added that the ÚOHS has relatively broad powers to be able to reveal such an activity, and if it does not wish to be satisfied with the explanations of the operators, who claim to have no idea that this would happen, their contractual conditions prohibit it.
When we will no longer be the most dear country
As we have already mentioned on several occasions, the entry of virtual operators into the mobile market has brought about a considerable drop in the price of services.
However, practically in all countries where virtual operators appeared, conventional operators tried, sometimes more, sometimes less, to prevent their creation.
The arguments used by them were practically the same - a market too small, even too saturated, problems with the quality of services, etc.
However, it is precisely at this level that have intervened, in developed countries, regulatory bodies, which defended not the interests of mobile operators, but those of their customers.
Both by formal measures and by informal pressure, they ended up obtaining the creation of a first virtual operator, followed by others.
Unfortunately, Czech regulators are dealing with this issue in the manner of a warm potato that is getting rid of by transmitting it to a nearby target.
As a result of the experiences we have, we cannot get rid of the impression that confusion in skills is more than profitable for them in these cases.
Don't throw the handle after knocking.
If behind-the-scenes information says it’s true and a virtual operator will soon appear with us, the Czech mobile market may get back in motion.
If one of the operators has accepted the creation of a virtual mobile operator, the ÚOHS will hardly remain impassive in the face of the rejection (discrimination) of others concerned.
And perhaps we will cease to call more expensive than in the rest of Europe.
The new Czech anthem has four new versions.
Choose the Best
Hear the four realizations of the new version of the Czech national anthem.
Sunday, on the occasion of Czech Nationality Day, Prime Minister Mirek Topolánek solemnly presented the new recordings of the national anthem Kde domov můj (Where Is My Homeland?).
The anthem was again recorded by the National Theatre Orchestra under the direction of Jiří Bělohlávek.
We have four versions - with a male or female solo singing, an interpretation with chorus and an instrumental version.
The song is performed by Kateřina Kněžínková and Adam Plachetka.
Otakar Jeremiáš and Jaroslav Krček are the authors of the anthem arrangements.
You can listen to all four new versions on the iHNed.cz server.
Bělohlávek considers Czech national singing to be one of the finest hymns.
As he had previously confided to the Český rozhlas (Czech Public Radio), he chose for the interpretation of the solo versions of the singers with young and representative voices, which are marked and appreciated.
"From the beginning, I was trying to take care that the two singers, as well as the choir, have a good diction, so that we understand the lyrics well, that they sing simultaneously," he said.
The Czech National Anthem will celebrate its 175th anniversary next year.
Kde domov můj sang for the first time in 1834 on the stages of Stavovské divadlo (the State Theatre) in Fidlovačka (Kermess of the Savetiers), a Czech original play by Josef Kajetán Tyl, accompanied by František Škroup.
It has become popular and people have spontaneously appropriated it.
After the creation of Czechoslaovakia in 1918, the first verse naturally became an integral part of the national anthem.
It remained one of the seven national symbols even after the creation of the independent Czech Republic.
How the "new" hymn sounds
Pragois public transport (MHD) will introduce the chip into their ticketing system on Wednesday
Wednesday, October 1, 2008 will be inscribed in the history of Pražský Dopravní podnik (the Prague Transport Agency) as the date on on the subway, the trams and buses the first passengers, who, instead of having a paper ticket, will have an electronic coupon in their pocket.
At the moment, the latter is accessible for about one hundred thousand people, owners of the Opencard smart card.
Passengers can purchase a coupon 30 days or 90 days (this will be valid from 1 January 2009) and at the same time register it on their smart card in the information points and offices of the Dopravní podnik (the Transport Agency), located in the metro stations Muzeum, Můstek, Nádraží Holešovice, Andě
From yesterday, there is also the possibility to buy coupons via the internet on the web pages of Dopravní podnik: www.dpp.cz.
More than four hundred people took advantage of the service during the morning.
Those who will start using the online store can definitively forget the counters in the subway stations, where they until now had to trample impatiently into long queues in order to get paper tickets.
Indeed, they will buy their coupon on the internet and will be registered on their Opencard in one of the thirteen Městská knihovna (municipal libraries).
"In these points of contact, there is at the disposal of the passengers a counter with trained staff, who will help those interested with the purchase over the internet and, in addition, will record the coupon they have bought on their card," says project spokesman Martin Opatrný.
In addition to libraries, it will also be possible to record electronic coupons on the Opencard in vending machines installed in the vestibule of several metro stations.
What do these distributors look like?
They are red, reminiscent the machines to compost tickets and the passengers may have already noticed them in the metro stations Roztyly, Budějovická, I. P. Pavlova, Florenc and Vltavská.
"Today the city has five, but by the end of the year, there should be eighty," Opatrný added.
An automaton will control the coupon in place of the driver
Automatons that will work from tomorrow, are easy to use.
Just enter the Opencard smart card and the previously purchased coupon will register itself on the card.
Electronic coupons are valid from tomorrow not only in the capital, but also in the vicinity of the city.
Namely in peri-urban trains and bus lines 300 to 499, used mainly by the inhabitants of the communes of Central Bohemia to access the capital.
"In trains integrated into Pražská integrovaná doprava (built-in transport in Prague), the controllers are equipped with card readers, using which they establish the temporary and zonal validity of the coupons;" explained the control of Filip Drápal tickets from the Ropid organization, which designs the schedules of Prague public transport.
Peri-urban buses are equipped with card readers built into the driver’s crate.
When boarding it is therefore necessary not only to show the smart card to the driver, but it is also necessary to place the Opencard at the place indicated on the side of the checkout.
This is how the driver checks the validity of registered coupons.
"In case the passenger needs to check the validity of the recorded coupons, there is nothing simpler than to request this information from the driver," Drápal said.
Passengers who do not take periurban buses and will not remember the date on which the validity of their coupon expires, can re-join the ATMs, initially used to register coupons on the card.
It is here, on the screen of the device, that the information about the validity of their document will be displayed.
Sudeteners demanded a referendum in the United States
Sudeten Germans demanded in 1945 that Šumava be integrated into the United States.
This stems from a letter deposited in the collections of the Jihočeská vědecká knihovna (The Scientific Library of South Bohemia).
If we cannot be part of Germany or Austria, we at least want to live under the employers of the United States of America.
In 1945, some groups of Sudeten Germans were allegedly addressed to the President of the United States with such desire.
This stems, among other things, from a letter preserved in a private archive, which is now deposited in the collections of the Jihočeská vědecká knihovna.
Germans de Český Krumlov asked in the letter not to allow "šumavská župa" (forest region south of Bohemia) - annexed by Hitler under the Munich Agreement - to the Czechoslovakia.
"We have fully understood the situation and want to be really sure that the words proclaiming that American troops will come to German territory not as oppressors and enemies, but as liberators," reads the letter, signed "Persons, to whom the fate of the Šumava is important."
Since rallying to Czechoslovakia is purportually promising - knowing the Czech national character - only of brutality, unspeakable attitude, misunderstanding, oppression and servitude, the authors of the letter ask the American president to take in his hands the Šumava protectorate for now and forever.
"We cannot believe that you would grant this territory to a foreign state to us, which oppresses and destroys minorities and which has in no way contributed to the victory of the Allies without taking into account the opinion of a quarter of a million of the inhabitants," the letter reads.
The so-called reason why the authors cannot sign the letter is the fear of the “Czech oppressors’ desire for revenge.”
If the president of the United States doubted the opinion of the Germans of Šumava, he would only have to organize, according to the authors of the letter, a referendum in the region to establish whether its inhabitants wanted to be part of the United States, or live under the employers of the United States.
Although the text contained in the letter can, given the current knowledge of post-war history, appear somewhat naïve, according to some survivors, it illustrates well the expectations of Sudeten Germans of the time.
While the Czech Germans had to at least foresee that the question of their expulsion from the republic arose, very few of them confessed such a possibility.
Some were waiting for the United States and Great Britain to obtain some type of autonomy for the Sudetens.
Others believed they would simply stay in Czechoslovakia.
The famous Austrian journalist Gustav Chalupa, born in České Budějovice, confirms this.
During the war, it was, as German from České Budějovice, engaged in the navy in the Baltic countries.
When, at the end of the war, he was captured by the English and placed in the capture camp of the Holstein region, dozens of Czech Germans came to consuct him to teach them Czech.
"They believed that once they returned home, the Czech would benefit them," Chalupa said.
None of the captured Germans who lived in Czechoslovakia before the war considered, according to him, that, as a result of the Beneš Decrees, he would be expelled from the Republic.
You don't think - you're paying!
Emottive campaign will attack drivers
Action aimed at the most expensive road safety in Czech history.
A way to characterize the emotional media campaign that intends to launch, Wednesday, the Ministry of Transport.
Minister Aleš Řebíček's office believes that it will discourage drivers from dangerous driving behaviour.
This campaign costing 150 million will appear on television, radio, cinema, the internet, but also on leaflets.
It should discourage drivers from fast driving and driving alcohol.
But also remind them to take the belt before starting.
Above all, she wants to touch the awareness of young drivers, motorcyclists, pedestrians, without forgetting professional drivers.
What will appear in the spots?
Especially personal stories that will attack emotions.
It also bears witness to the "bloody" slogan of the entire campaign:
You don't think - you're paying!
The phrase "You don't think" is inscribed in capital letters on the license plate.
Then, in a puddle of blood, there's the phrase "You'll pay."
This must symbolize that one can pay for life a moment of inattention.
The spots will "haunt" spectators until 2010.
The ministry was inspired by similar campaigns in Anglo-Saxon countries, where we can commonly see images with drastic images of the victims of accidents.
He believes that this will lead to a decrease in the rate of accidents and the number of deaths on Czech roads.
The spots were prepared by Euro RSCG.
The strategy of sugar and the whip against drivers
"Which works with drivers is the sugar and whip system.
For them, sugar is represented, for example, by saving time, when they burn a red light.
The whip is the threat of a fine or an accident.
The risks must be blamed on the drivers,” said Martin Kořán, the transportation psychologist at Na Homolce Hospital.
That's how we need information campaigns in the press and especially negative advertisements, believes the psychologist.
According to him, panels pointing out the danger to drivers are already less efficient.
"When a static panel that warns of a danger is installed, it will probably quickly merge with the environment and drivers will no longer notice," Kořán reported.
Olmert would return in exchange for peace almost all the occupied territories
Ehud Olmert, the president of Israel’s Provisional Government, voted in favor of returning virtually all the territories occupied in 1967, including the West Bank, East Jerusalem and the Golan Heights, in exchange for peace with Palestine and Syria.
He made this statement in an interview published Monday by the Yedioth Ahronoth newspaper.
"We must reach an agreement with the Palestinians, which means that it will be necessary to make almost all (occupied), even all)," Olmert said.
"We will keep a certain percentage of the territory, but we will have to cede to the Palestinians a similar percentage (of Israeli territory), since otherwise, the peace will not happen," he added.
He admitted that possible restitution would include East Jerusalem.
"He who desires security in Jerusalem, and above all does not want tractors and bulldozers to crush his feet, as has happened to one of my best friends, will have to renounce parts of Jerusalem," Olmert remarked, referring to the two attacks in the city, in July, in which several people were killed and dozens injured.
According to the Prime Minister, it will be necessary to find "a special solution for the Temple Mount, the sacred and historical places" located inside the Old City.
"Whoever who wants to maintain Israeli sovereignty over the entire city will be forced to integrate 270 000 Arabs.
It won’t work,” he said afterwards.
Retreating the Golan Heights
Olmert also supported Israel's retreat from the Golan Heights, which he said was an indispensable condition for the conclusion of a peace agreement with Syria.
However, this would also require some efforts from Damascus.
"I am not proposing to conclude peace with Syria solely on the basis of a retreat from the Golan," he said.
“Syrians know very well what they’re going to have to give up in order to get the Golan.
They will have to renounce their current relations with Iran; they will have to renounce their relationship with Hezbollah (Shiite movement); they will have to give up the constant support that they give to the terrorism of Hamas (Shiite movement), Al-Kaida (terrorist network), as well as that of Jihad (holy war) in Iraq.
Questions that arise after the accident: Are stewards in the bus illegal?
The law ignores them; walking in a long-distance bus is forbidden
In the context of the tragic accident of the coach of the Student Agency near Padua, Italy, the question arises if stewards and passengers can travel within the bus.
"If we wanted to respect the law strictly and formally, the activity of a steward is contrary to the road traffic law," said Transport Ministry spokesman Karel Hanzelka.
The accident in question, during which a 22-year-old hostess perished, was apparently caused by the driver of a truck that crossed the path of the bus.
At that time, the girl was sitting next to the driver, in the most dangerous seat of the vehicle, although she had at her disposal two other seats.
However, the bus can have an accident, or even brake abruptly, also at the moment when the steward serves coffee or distributes the newspapers.
What is important is whether the bus is by virtue of its technical sheet equipped with standing seats or not.
If this standing equipment is not approved, passengers must remain seated.
Long-distance Coaches Student Agency do not have standing seats; this has been confirmed in Aktuálně.cz by the director of the company Radim Jančura.
"This is an issue that we dealt with with the ministry and, at the beginning, it prohibits the movement of passengers.
Then they canceled it and we got the passengers can go to the toilet during the ride provided they have their own seat.”
The law ignores the steward
“There is no unequivocal legal interpretation of steward’s role – there is no legal requirement to deal with this role.
On the other hand, there is also no legal requirement prohibiting the carrier from offering services.
The law on road transport knows only the term vehicle receiver; it does not define its rights and duties, "adds with more ambiguity the spokesman Hanzelka.
Josef Pokorný, the director of the Department of Road Vehicle Traffic, believes, on the contrary, that the law adequately addresses the issue.
"It all depends on the fact whether the bus has in its homologation permission to offer standing seats.
It’s all in there,” he said.
Coaches are divided into urbans, where there are upright spaces and a limited, inter-urban maximum speed with higher seats and higher allowed speeds and long-distance coaches without a standing place with the highest maximum permissible speed and seats equipped with seat belts.
As in the plane, says an expert
Jaroslav Hořín, an expert at BESIP (Road Traffic Safety) in transport, does not see the movement of stewards on board as a major problem.
"During regular traffic, there is no danger. The situation can be compared to that of service in airplanes.
There either, it’s not safe when a turmoil will occur and the flight attendant can hit his head.”
"Now, in the case where the bus would have braked abruptly, the laws of physics would obviously be valid.
In the same way as on an urban bus or in a tram.
The carrier must manage the safety of stewards with internal prescriptions,” he said, and also believes that from the point of view of the law, the issue is not resolved.
Jančura is ready to discontinue these services
Student Agency is the only coach carrier to offer steward services.
He's hiring two hundred.
Even after this accident, the director does not consider their job as a risky trade.
"Nevertheless, as soon as I have the impression that this is a risky profession, I will remove it.
Passengers can receive their coffee and their diary at boarding.
It would also reduce costs,” he said.
The concern for safety is the reason given to the absence of stewards in his vehicles the director of Asiana Alexei Litvine, which ensures, in comparison with SA, a very small number of lines.
"We don't have hostesses. We've been opposing it from the beginning.
Sooner or later it will end with a fatal accident,” he said, a year ago, at Týden.cz.
An immense tragedy has arrived
A young hostess from the Plzeň area, who had been working for the company for two years, perished during the accident on the Naples line.
A Slovene truck probably left the emergency stop tape without using a turn signal and it blocked the way to the bus.
The driver of the latter to try to avoid it by means of a sudden maneuver, but he approached him by his right side.
Jančura refuses that the two seats reserved for rest for the stewards have been sold.
"In the case of long-distance lines, it is not possible to reserve these places, they are blocked."
The company intends to file a complaint against the driver of the truck.
For the compensation of the girl's family, the agency will not wait for the judgment of the court, but with regard to financial compensation, it also relies on the culprit.
An amendment to the law?
Not in sight
An amendment to the law, which has been elaborated for more than a year, by a group of thirty experts from the Ministry of Transport, could clarify the movements of stewards aboard the bus.
"However, it did not reach any conclusion on this point.
In fact, she is not too interested in buses, but rather on the point system, the maximum speed on the highway and the use of the laptop behind the wheel, "says expert Hořín.
"The Ministry of Transport does not consider steward activity to be something that would fundamentally jeopardize the safety of passengers," said spokesman Hanzelka, noting that the control of bus security is under the power of the police and the Office for Occupational Safety.
Düsseldorfers’ hopes are based on Frankfurt.
There was no shortage of words in the WestLB Steering Committee's letter to the employees of the house.
According to a union of the entities of the Düsseldorf Financial Institute with the Deka-Bank in Frankfurt, "the central financial markets institution of Sparkasse's financial group" could be formed, according to the letter made public on Monday.
The WestLB would provide a "competence block" coupled with sustainable productivity in such a case.
All in all, such a relationship could be the decisive kick-off towards other mergers in the Länder banking sector.
This is what it looks like when a policy committee wishes to demonstrate its ability to act in difficult times.
Because the WestLB, injured, is still under a lot of pressure.
It is not only the European Commission, but also the Federal Supervisory Authority of Financial Services that has, only recently, made it clear to the government of North Rhine-Westfalie that time was running out in the search for a future solution.
However, there has been talk for some time now - also on the side of Frankfurt - of a relationship of any form between Deka Bank, which knows Sparkasse's customers because of their investment funds, and the WestLB.
Admittedly Deta-Bank leader Franz Waas said during the presentation of the figures of the semester at the end of August that he saw no meaning in the merger of Mainzer Landstrasse's house with a bank in Land, whether it is the Helaba or the WestLB.
However, the Deka's Board of Directors, each half of which is supported and by the banks of the Länder and by the groupings of savings banks, instructed the steering committee to conduct discussions with the WestLB for joint commercial activities.
It could then be the debit of the financial market, or parts thereof.
The talks have only just begun, it is said in the financial center.
This is not the first time that the Deta-Bank, which, together with its 3000 employees, counts for one of the largest employers in the financial centre of Frankfurt, has come into play when it comes to consolidating the savings bank sector.
In 2003 it was believed for a while that the Deta-Bank could merge with the Bank of the Land of Rhenania Palatinate and the Halbab.
However, nothing came out of this ternary bouquet.
Barely the plans had been made public that the government of the Land of Hesse was pronouncing against any participation of the Hellaba in them.
Thereafter, the other two houses also did not meet again.
The Bank of the Land of Rhineland Palatinate has been attached to the place at the Bank of the Land of Baden-Wuttenberg in Stuttgart.
But there have always been further negotiations on the grouping of the Deka-Bank with banks in Länder.
Finally, Günther Merl, chairman of Hellaba's steering committee, voted against any commercial merging strategy with his bank.
Regardless of this, there seems to be a growing feeling that the consolidation of the various institutions in the public sector is particularly urgent.
Now that the election to the Parliament of Bavaria has passed, a BayernLB union with the Baden-Wuttennberg Land Bank could come to fruition in the first.
For Helaba, this is not particularly good news.
If the pressure that forces new mergers were to increase, the possible partners gradually escape him.
In the South, it would now be a junior partner, although it has hitherto withstood the crisis better than the banks of Munich and Stuttgart.
Hesse-Thuringe's grouping of savings and transfers, which owns 85 percent of Helaba's capital, fired a lock at the beginning of the year on any union with WestLB.
Remaining the NordLB rigorously.
Such geo-strategic reflections, however, reflects only one of the facets.
Another facet raises the question of how the different business models of each of these banks can match.
In June, Merl stated that the decisive question, thanks to which business model the future capacity of the Länder banks should be assured, had not yet been resolved by the German grouping of savings banks and transfers.
In the long term, a westLb breakdown could take place, dewatering which here is obviously not desired.
This is also referred to in the letter of the existing Executive Committee: in any case, a `comprehensive solution' for the establishment would be addressed.
How all this will be put in the same basket with the union of some entities with the Deta-Bank remains to be seen.
The second weekend of the beer festival is also known as the Italian weekend.
For Elena, it's the first time for Luca already the third or fourth time.
But this time, Elena accompanies him as a girlfriend, and therefore nothing is the same, he said.
Behind them, near a barrel of five liters of beer from the Kaiser brand, stands a well-built beard that shows about 200 visitors of the beer festival what deep sounds its gosier can produce.
Elena tapes Luca first, then kiss her.
From Bologna they came to Verona.
From there, from the bus station near Puorta nueva train station, four buses take the start tonight at 23h45, `destinazione Monaco, festa della birra', arrival scheduled at 6h30.
Three buses are booked above all for the Italians, in one of the buses travel Braziles exclusively.
In one corner of the car park, the South Americans are singing gold and already next to their flag, they have already celebrated last night.
And also noisy, explains Marco Guarda, it's not for Italians.
The buses belong to Marco, who wears a long coat lined with faux fur and glasses with large silver frames.
For five years, they've been going to the beer party, already three trips this year alone.
They go there at night, stay during the day at the party, and return at 22h30.
Spending the night would involve unnecessary costs, Marco says.
The trip costs 75 euros, including the welcome jar with a Kaiser beer, brewed in accordance with the Bavarian Purity Law, health insurance and an official of the German speaking group - Marco did a language course in Frankfurt.
And already the buses run in the night on the Autostrada direction `Brennero'.
There's no beer, not yet.
Quickily, everything becomes quiet on the bus, travelers sleep.
It's better that way, they'll still need their strength.
Breaks will be taken in Bressanone, and Holzkirchen, while a large number of other buses with Italian license plates take a break again shortly before Munich, on Highway 8.
When in the early morning, Marco's group finally arrives at since-and-a-thirties-twenty as planned in the parking lot near Place Thérèse, nearly 50 other buses from all over Europe are already there.
Elena, Luca and the others gather, Marco holds a final speech, explains the plan of attack.
Firstly : to style the small yellow cap.
Desus is written on the left `Beer Festival', in Gothic, and next to the internet address of Marco's travel agency `conoscersiviaggiando.it".
Secondly : `When the doors of the marquee of the beer open, we run', says Marco in the early morning.
`Run!' Run and reserve the seats at the bottom of the tent.
`Not in front, otherwise you block, and not all the yellow caps on the same benches, you have to distribute them in the room!'
The mug, shouts Marco, costs 8.25 euros.
And don't forget the instructions!
`Show security guards that we behave well!
As soon as you leave your seats, they're gone!"
Third: `Tonight, having come back here to ten and a half hours!' Including?
And that's it, it's up to the beer party.
On the way, someone makes yellow caps to the trees to find the way back, to the signs and even to an electoral poster of a large Bavarian popular party.
We'd like to be ready for the return.
It is the second weekend of the Beer Festival, known as the `Weekend of the Italians'.
There are no precise figures; we are talking about 50 000 who set out through the Alps and collapse in the beer kiosk, 10 percent of the visitors would have come specifically from Italy this weekend.
The Munich police are reinforced by seven Southern Tirol policemen, by Italian volunteer rescuers who support the Bavarian red cross, which carries out a statistic on the countries where the Italians occupy the first place in front of the British (60 interventions) and the Austrians (about 80) with nearly 200 medical interventions.
The one that is particularly appreciated by Italians is the tent of the Hofbräu brewery.
Insomnia Nights for Hypo Real Estate Shareholders
Germany’s banking system is relatively stable, the Federal Minister of Finance insisted just a few weeks ago.
How fortunate that the politician used the word `relatively' and that he slipped that the German banking sector would not be spared by crisis movements.
This happened this weekend.
Bavarian real estate financier Hypo Real Estate (HRE), alongside the DAX, obtains from a consortium of German banks a credit of one billion to protect themselves from market turbulence.
A person in the confidence of the situation told Reuters on Monday that aid credits are issued up to an amount of 35 billion euros.
The Hypo Real Estate would now have to pay interest on the fraction to which it would thus be entitled.
The money comes as much from private banks as from banks under public law.
In the background, it's Depfa-daughter-companies that supports losses.
According to a newspaper, the state financier had, according to a newspaper article, refinanced in the very short term for long-term projects for which he had borrowed money - which is no longer possible since because of mistrust against the markets.
In the first half of 2008, the bank's liquidity reserve had been reduced by 95 percent to 2.37 million euros, and the own capital rate had slightly declined from 1.6 to 1.45 per cent.
Ongoing profits and net profits from financial transactions were, compared to the same period in the previous year, fallen to point zero, profits from the allocation of specified receivabilities and securities as well as the termination of provisions in credit operations had fallen from more than 70 percent to 5 million euros.
And it is only thanks to the large profits derived from reimbursing downstream accounting costs resulting from the restructuring of the former Depfa group, as well as the reimbursement of interest according to the book of tax procedures and commercial purchases for Konzern companies that the earnings deficit of about 16 percent turned out to be moderate.
After its privatization in 1990, the former state financier had initially poorly speculated on industrial real estate loans, which had then been filialized at Aareal Bank.
Because conventional state financing was not profitable enough, the Depfa had then engaged more intensely in infrastructure projects and in the financing of joint projects between private and state investors (Privat-Public-Partnership, PPP) and diversified internationally.
On the value of the daughter company, the Hypo Real Estate must carry out depreciations that should have on the calculation of the gains and losses of the group an essential material effect that still remains quite unclear.
The fact is that the dividends have been amputated.
Already for 2007, they had been truncated two-thirds.
The Hypo Real Estate is, since the acquisition of Depfa, a highly important state and infrastructure investor.
It is stronger than other refinancing institutes on the inter-banking market since it has no deposit of customers.
With the bankruptcy of investment bank Lehman Brothers two weeks ago, the allocation of loans between banks has once again dried up considerably, the banks having accumulated their liquidity.
The Hypo Real Estate needs annually for a part - about 50 Billion euros - of all Depfa's appropriations for a short-term means of refinancing.
According to a statement from the Hypo Real Estate, the group’s need for financing is covered with medium-term credit and the company protected from the influence of international financial markets largely out of the running state at present.
Other sources say refinancing would be provided through funds until the end of 2009.
The chairman of HRE’s executive committee Georg Funke, on the other hand, explained that Hypo Real Estate should no longer have to resort to financial markets in the medium term.
The Federal Bank and the Federal Institution for the Supervision of Financial Services (Baffin) assume that Hypo Real's capacity in the market is now assured.
Shareholders and creditors of the bank only have to hope.
However, in view of the dynamics and imponderability of the crisis, there is no certainty.
Moreover, it is not unthinkable that funding should not be used in all its extent.
However, the opposite cannot be discarded either.
Following the fact that the bank is, according to the last press releases, still at the gates of insolvency Sunday at the end of the evening, this temporary rescue will not succeed in reassuring the spirits either.
The share price collapsed from 75 percent to 3.30 euros.
Bond prices, on the other hand, are doing particularly well.
While debts in the foreground have barely moved, prices for second-level loans are falling.
The most affected are mainly the investment funds of the former Daughter company Württemberg Hypo and those, of course, that have a lifetime beyond 2009.
Hypo Real Estate’s bonds previously seemed to be an investment of alternative speculation, now their value must be seen as dependent on the stability of the international banking system after the net hardening of the banking crisis and the crisis of the very institute that brought the crisis into the institution through the acquisition of Depfa Bank.
As long as issuing institutes, less-affected governments and commercial banks are still ready to support the system, it is possible to presume an arrest reduction over time.
Or will happen what no one dares to hope right now - that the financial crisis can be overcome in the next 15 months.
But in the face of the renewed noises of partial nationalization, the probability that approached safety has now become a simple well-founded hope.
This should reserve nights of insomnia primarily for long-term creditors on the decision to know whether they should part with their commitments.
Short-term investors can still use what they’ve drawn a line to be a deferral of losses for the next few years on the upcoming capital income tax on stock earnings automatically levied.
Sylvana and the Secrets of the Bento Box
When Sylvana Hiltrop, 16, has to introduce herself for the first day at Tachibana Highschool, the girls begin to laugh: the German is so "kawaii"!
- What does that mean?
Sylvana already knows this word thanks to the Mangas - in her new Exchange Log, she talks about Japan.
“The first day of class in Japan.
I was excited about it.
Already the way to go to school scared me.
I live on the outskirts of Tokyo towards Kawasaki and take the 40-minute train to the Tachibana Highschool.
I had to find the way by myself, fortunately I didn't get lost.
The beginning of classes does not seem to be fixed as strictly as at home in Lower Saxony.
The students appear one after the other from eight hours, until at nine hours minus twenty, one lap begins with the teacher, the "home room".
But before the class began, it was up to me to introduce myself.
“I’m not at all the type to like to talk in front of people, especially in front of a class of 40 students.
Since I didn't yet have the school uniform, I put on an old skirt of my foster mother, as well as a shirt, bright blue socks and black leather shoes.
In this outfit, therefore, I appeared in front of the desk, and tried to pronounce everything correctly: 'Sayonara Wo she Sylvana...' - My name is Sylvana, I come from Germany and my birthday is in a week.
At that time, I was very happy to have started learning the language two years ago.
The 31 girls in the class have all made low masses, pumped laughter and made signs, and I have constantly heard the word "Kawaii".
I already knew him because of my mangas.
It belongs to the vocabulary essential to the survival of Japanese teenagers and means "cute, cabbage".
Then I quickly slipped into my place.
The girl next to me gave me a big ironic smile, she thought it was an honor that the correspondent had to sit next to her.
The rest of the hour was pretty quiet, but when the gong resounded, I was assaulted accordingly.
Everyone was talking to who better, puffed to laughter and asked me a lot of questions – in this case, it’s quite difficult to understand English Japanese.
During the break, they all took out their Bento.
My foster mother had also given me this kind of Lunchbox - it's really something other than the wretched German snacks.
I had rice with aromatic herbs, small sausages, a cucumber and algae salad, fruits and a kind of meat surrounded by Tofu.
Most of the time, I have no idea what exactly I’m eating here in Japan.
Things have a bizarre appearance and a completely new taste.
What's certain is that everything is really good.
Well, except maybe candied savory plums, brrrr!
Bentos aren't just lunches in Japanese schools.
It sometimes happens that a girl offers a boy a Bento that she prepared herself, and who is then prepared particularly carefully - in the hope of being able to do a little better knowledge of the boy during the break.
I’ve read that in my mangas.
I'm curious to know if I could observe it here too.
"Why Japan in particular?" asked my friends when I told them that I wanted to spend a year at school in Tokyo.
Then I always replied:
"Because it fascinates me, what."
In the end, reading mangas, that’s what I’ve already preferred for years.
And in order to better understand the mangas and the culture found there, at one moment, I also started to learn Japanese.
For me, it was really clear that I had to travel there one day.
Because I also have a weakness for traditions, customs and customs.
And here they are given a lot of importance.
On the one hand, Japan is the country of the latest technologies and trends - on the other hand, it is harsh, disciplined and traditional.
And since everything is so well organized, I’ve never felt lost here, even though Tokyo is the biggest city I’ve ever seen.”
Rats also have sensations
A rat or cockroach flees in the face of danger, does it mean that he also feels fear?
Neurophysicist Joseph LeDoux is convinced of this: many animals also have sensations.
But how can we verify that he experiences the same when he behaves in the same way as we do?
In the online magazine "Edge", contributions have been published for several years by famous scientists.
SPIEGEL ONLINE presents in a series of articles answers to the question "What do you think to be true without being able to prove it?"
I believe that animals have sensations and other states of consciousness without me or anyone else having so far been able to prove it just as one cannot prove that other human beings have a conscience.
But in this case, we can at least hope that all human brains have the same configuration.
As soon as we turn to other species and we address the questions of feeling or in general consciousness, we penetrate into hard-to-practice grounds because the Hardware does not correspond.
When a rat faces danger, it does the same as many other animals: it kills, leaks or attacks.
Humans behave in the same way, which is why some scientists deduce a comparable subjective experience.
However, I don't believe it.
Two aspects of the structure of the brain make the transposition of our subjective experience to that of animals difficult.
One aspect is that circuits almost always associated with human consciousness include the prefrontal lateral Cortex (because of its participation in short-term memory and direction and execution functions).
This vast region is much more developed in humans than in other primates and appears to be entirely absent in others.
At the very least for those aspects of our consciousness that depend on the prefrontal Cortex, among them the consciousness of whom we are, and the ability to foresee and decide, it is reasonable to assume that the human being even differs from the other primates.
Another significant peculiarity could lie in the linguistic nature of Man: a large part of his existence being related to language, it often means that his consciousness depends on it.
In this case, the animals could be excluded.
But even if the consciousness should not depend on the language, it would surely influence it, so that animal consciousness, regardless of the way it is produced, would probably differ greatly from most of our states of consciousness.
That’s why it’s so hard to figure out what consciousness can look like in other animals.
If this, internal and subjective, does not let itself be measured or, because of this particular Hardware, analyze based on our own experience, then its observation poses big problems for us.
Most of my developments refer to the content of conscious experience, but there’s just another aspect of consciousness that should be more scientifically accessible.
We could look at animal consciousness processes without taking into account their contents, and this is exactly what has happened during studies on the short-term memory of primates, especially with the example of visualization.
But in this approach that Christof Koch and Francis Crick have followed, we focus more on the nerve correlations of consciousness than on the causal mechanisms: both can be identical or not.
Curiously, this approach also emphasizes the weight of the prefrontal Cortex in the realization of visualization.
So what about emotions?
I conclude that an emotional system such as that of fear is activated in a brail aware of its own activity.
What we denote as "fear" is therefore the mental state in which we find ourselves when the activity of the defense system located in the brain (or its consequences, such as the reactions of the body) seizes living memory.
Thus observed, the emotions are closely related to the regions of the cortex that characterize primates and especially humans.
Subtle degrees of feeling, associated with our voluble nature, emerge, because we can, with the help of words and grammar, differentiate and categorize states to impute them to others than to ourselves.
On the other hand, Antonio Damasio, for example, believes that sensations from primitive activities are born in the regions of the bodily sensation of Cortex and the breave, as does Jaak Pankseep, which, however, focuses more on the cerebral stem.
As this network has not changed much over the course of human evolution, it could quite participate in transversal sensations of all kinds.
From a purely theoretical point of view, I cannot contradict it, but I believe it is indemonstrable.
Pankseeep argues that what appears as fear in Men and rats is presumably also felt as such in both cases: how could one nevertheless check whether rats and humans feel the same when they behave in a similar way?
A cockroach flees in the face of danger, but if he feels fear?
In my opinion, similar behaviors are not enough to prove identical experiences.
Admittedly, nervous comparison helps: rats and humans have similar brain trunks, cockroaches on the other hand don’t even have a brain.
But is the brain stem causing sensations?
And even if we could prove this for humans: how could we demonstrate it in rats?
I believe that rats and other mammals, maybe even cockroaches (who knows?) have sensations.
But I wouldn't know how to prove it.
And because their sensations are probably completely different from ours (since human consciousness relies on special circuits and language), I prefer to explore the emotional behavior than the emotional sensations of rats.
In any case, advances are marked on the nervous level to the extent that the same is gauged in rats and in humans.
As I don't explore language and consciousness in rats, or sensations, I don't know if they exist.
One could judge this as short-term, but I prefer to move forward rather than to constantly knock myself against a wall.
I am indeed a pragmatic emotion.
