Airline tickets and hotels are falling
In the first half of 2008, hotel flights and nights were cheaper than the previous year.
In Germany, according to a current study, flights would even be reduced by up to 50 percent.
Flights and hotel nights would again become advantageous - this is the result of a study by financial services and travel provider American Express.
The company analysed its booking data for the first half of 2008 and established, after an increase in prices in the first quarter, a sharp decline in profits in the second quarter.
After prices in the hotel market have risen for years, hotel nights have already reduced in many places.
From January to March, prices increased in 41 of the 48 European cities still examined by an average of 6.9 per cent compared to 2007 in 41 of the 48 European cities examined.
From April to June, however, prices were already 3.6 percent lower than the previous year.
The trend was sharp especially in Paris and Rome, where travelers had to pay 30 to 37 per cent less than a year earlier.
The causes of all of this would lie for American Express in the weak price of the dollar that led to a "desire for declining travel of American tourists".
Financial markets such as Frankfurt or London would also be affected more by the decline in hotel prices than industrial sites such as Hanover, Cologne or Stavanger.
In the Swedish city, hotel prices are held with an increase of 26.3 per cent, the record in Europe.
Also in terms of flight prices, the trend was upwards in the first quarter:
Due to the increase in the price of kerosene, passengers in economy classes had to pay an average of 7.1 per cent more than in 2007, long-haul flights, which are highly fuel-consuming, even increased by up to 29.2 per cent.
From April to June, economy-class ticket prices fell on average by 12.5 per cent compared to the previous quarter, in business and first-class, however, price changes were much less pronounced.
In Germany, prices fell even further than in the other European countries:
From January to March, prices increased by 11.9 per cent, in the second quarter they fell by 23.6 percent.
German domestic flights have even been halved according to this study.
The reasons for this fall in prices would on the one hand be weakening economic growth, explains the firm.
Demand would cede terrain in many segments of the air travel market.
On the other hand, there would be heightened pressure from competition under the "Open Sky" Treaty, which liberalizes air traffic between the United States and Europe.
A sign of the tense situation is the removal by airlines of non-responsible routes, as well as the high number of airlines that would have filed for bankruptcy during the first half of 2008 - there were 24 airlines in Europe alone.
