Dark Matter Influences the Theft of Space Probes
For years, a mysterious phenomenon leaves researchers in perplexity: dark matter influences the speed of space probes during so-called gravitational support maneuvers.
Experts fear that the general theory of relativity will not be completely correct.
For almost 18 years, the "Ulysses" spacecraft has been flying in space.
Due to soon-frozen fuel conductions, cryogenic death threatens it.
For years, a mysterious phenomenon has been the cause of disarray among researchers - the anomaly of gravitational support.
Gravitational support is a maneuver of space probes, by which they obtain additional momentum in the gravitational field of a celestial body, the Earth for example.
Thus, probes can save a lot of fuel.
Cassini-Huygens American-European research probe used this physical trick, just like the Ulysses solar probe.
This is what has surprised scholars elsewhere for years: flying objects do not always behave at 100 percent of the way it is awaited according to the venerable laws of gravitation.
Moreover, irregularities arise in terms of speed.
Probes unexpectedly become quieter or slower.
Until now, physicists couldn’t explain that effect.
They named him the anomaly of gravitational support.
Some experts feared that Albert Einstein's theory of general relativity, which describes matter's force of attraction because of the curvature of space, may not be entirely correct.
American physicist Stephen Adler of Princeton's Institute for Advanced Study still believes to be iron that Einstein was not wrong and tries to explain the anomaly of gravitational support with the help of so-called dark matter that astronomers have identified in the universe.
"Dark matter could be responsible for this anomaly, which is also close to Earth," he wrote.
Space researchers have only been aware of the existence of dark matter for about ten years.
It apparently emanates from it a force of attraction whose effect is observable on other celestial bodies.
It, on the other hand, is completely invisible, i.e. it does not send or reflect any electromagnetic radiation, especially no visible light.
According to the calculations of the scientists, dark matter would still represent a good fifth of the entire mass of the universe.
