.The Crown Prosecution Service suggested that the ministerial officer be placed under the guardianship of the Metropolitan Police, given the very sensitive nature of his work related to the Associate Secret Services Committee
The documents he lost contained assessments on Al-Qaeda as well as on the resources of the security forces in Iraq.
He was informed of the decision on Monday morning and was then transported from his home to a secret location.
Construction companies facing the B&B invoice.
British construction companies may have to pay more than £80 million, to cover the Bradford & Bingley bank rescue.
The government has provided £14 billion to protect depots of Bradford & Bingleys 2.6 million savers.
Interest on this loan will be paid by all companies that raise money from investors, including 59 construction companies.
Building Societies Association (BSA) CEO Adrian Coles said it’s “irritating” to know that companies and their members will have to pay the broken jars.
The £14 billion loan will be repaid when Bradford & Bingleys mortgages are cleared.
But the interests that at the same time, will be debited, every year, to the 700 financial companies that collect the deposits.
The Financial Services Compensation Scheme confirmed to the BBC that all 59 construction companies are included in this list.
It's irritating to see that institutions that have behaved cautiously... now have to pay some of the bills from institutions that were much less cautious.
Adrian Coles, BSA
It is proclaimed that each company must pay an amount proportional to the deposits it holds, and that the intermediary companies have to pay about 18% of the cost.
The Treasury estimates that interest on the loan will rise to £450million in 2009.
This means that companies will have to find £81 million to settle the interests of the first year.
Adrian Coles, managing director of the Commercial Committee of Building Companies, told the BBC that this decision seemed unfair to him.
It is "irritating" to see that institutions that have behaved cautiously during the resumption of the real estate market, now have to pay for institutions much less cautious.
According to the BSA, no company has failed since 1945, the date of the first annals.
“We need to look at all aspects of this case for the next few weeks, to assess the options to be taken to prevent construction companies and their members from paying this salted bill,” Cole said.
Interests for 2009 cover seven months, from the end of September this year until the end of March.
In 2010, it will be necessary to pay the interest of the whole year, which could moreover double.
Since construction companies are non-shareholder organizations, their member ends up paying the bill either by higher expenses, or lower interest rates for savings accounts.
