A new investigation into the case of American prosecutors.
The U.S. attorney general has appointed a special accuser to verify the issue of the dismissal of nine U.S. prosecutors in 2006.
The whole was born from the account of the case of the inspector general of the Department of Justice, who criticized the actions of certain ministerial officers.
The report was critical of White House officers, legislators and former Attorney General Alberto Gonzales.
The investigation is based on the thesis of lawyers who argue that their dismissal is due to political causes.
Special Accuser Nora Dannehy will now investigate whether the officers interviewed during the investigation have lied to investigators, obstructing justice.
“Serious allegations of potentially criminal conduct, unfounded, have not been fully confirmed or resolved,” Inspector General Glenn Fine said in his report.
The appointment of a special accuser capable of forcing witnesses to testify was one of his greatest recommendations.
The dispute began with the release in December 2006 of nine U.S. prosecutors by the U.S. Department of Justice.
At the time, the department said prosecutors were dismissed because of mediocre benefits.
But it then emerged, that dismissed prosecutors were not more criticized than their other colleagues, which prompted some observers to attribute a political connotation to these dismissals.
Some of the prosecutors have been accused of being close to Congressional Republicans, while others have reportedly refused to investigate accusations of fraudulent voting against the Democrats.
U.S. prosecutors are investigating and suing judicial bodies for the government.
It is certain that they may be dismissed at any time, but cannot be for political reasons.
The head of the Department of Justice, Attorney General Alberto Gonzales, finished accused of being involved in this dismissal case, and in the end he resigned.
A number of ministerial officers in the Department of Justice have also resigned.
The Inspector General's report was very critical of Mr. Gonzales' role in this case, stating that he considered him "first responsible" for these layoffs, and ended up "abdicating" his role as a leader," remarkably succeeding in not involved in this case.
Other ministerial officers, including Bush’s former collaborator, Karl Rove, were critical for failing to cooperate during the investigation.
Meat Loaf's epic must be honored.
Rock star Meat Loaf must be honored at the Q Awards for her epic singing Bat Out Of Hell.
The singer, who will receive an oscar at the ceremony in London next week, said he was "honored" to obtain this recognition.
