In 2007, strong laryngitis forced him to cancel concerts.
Then he found a cyst in the vocal cords.
A Dark Street in the City, Often Forgotten, and Can Soon Be on Sale.
New York has hundreds of avenues, boulevards, streets and other parallel roads, some famous, others only functional.
And even some veteran taxis as well as long-dated local residents do not know how to give directions to go to Extra Place, because they have never heard of them.
The street is located in the East Village and travels north of First Street – not to be confused with Second Street – between Bowery and Second Avenue.
Approximately 30 feet wide for 120 long, it resembles a dusty alley rather than an active artery.
. No signs indicate the street.
It is virtually impossible to find it on a standard atlas.
However, this street has admirers.
For example, there’s Avalon Bay, the builder of recent luxury buildings on First Street.
He would like to repaver Extra Place, and create a smoother passage to shops and boutiques, which should open in the new buildings.
Other admirers, including long-time neighbors, said they were charmed by these shortcuts simply because it remains one of the last and oldest streets of the block.
But the Preservation and Real Estate Development Department of the city, which manages the street, has no interest in keeping it.
This month, at a meeting of the Neighbourhood Owners' Committees, representatives of city agency and Avalon Bay drafted a proposal authorizing the builder to buy the street.
But the committee prefers that the street remain in the hands of the city, and has asked for a new proposal to submit next month, said Susan Stetzer, the local manager of Committee No. 3.
“There are very few spaces left belonging to the city, and we would like the city to retain Extra Place,” she said.
“They could offer to rehabilitate it and manage it, which Avalon could do, but we want to keep it public.”
Avalon Bay announced several months ago that she wanted to repave the small street and install seats there, some would be used by a café to which the builder will concede the space.
The proposal for this potential acquisition was made public at this month’s meeting.
Michele de Milly, Avalon Bay's spokesman, said that although the company wants to operate the street, it doesn't necessarily mean she wants to own it.
"Avalon Bay's main interest is to relook at this abandoned alley, and make it public," she said, stressing that it was the city to suggest the sale."
Seth Donlin, a spokesperson for Immovable Preservation, confirmed that the city wanted to sell the street, further stressing that the agency's mission was to develop the property rather than manage it.
Compared to its more colorful neighbor, Bowery, Extra Place has left a slight historical imprint.
Some believe that Extra Place bears this name since 1802, when the legacy of Philip Minthorne's great farm of many children was divided, leaving aside this little piece of land, which has since remained abandoned.
The subsequent entrance to the CBGB, a punk rock monument closed in 2006, reopened at Extra Place; the street is certainly more famous thanks to the cover photo of Ramones' "Rocket to Russia"
"this place was beautiful," said Danny Fields, the manager of the Ramones, who took the photo in November 1976.
It was full of scrap metals, rags, barrels, posters, leaves and ropes.”
Some residents, including bloggers who had already talked about the evolution towards the luxury of the East Village, objected to the idea of selling Extra Place, saying this humble little property should remain public.
Others have proclaimed Extra Place as a true rarity – a fragment virtually identical to the city that should be ruined in peace.
Jeremiah Moss, who runs a blog called Jeremiah's Vanishing New York (subtitled "The Book of Protests: A Bitterly Nostalgic Look at A City in the Extinction Process") tells the story of the City and Extra Place, published in 1952 in The New Yorker.
The author, Brendan Gill, wrote: "Extra Place is a small narrow cul-de-sac, dark by day and night, and delimited by the doors of a rusty iron warehouse and louvers, full of newspapers from last week that fly along the gutters."
With few exceptions, the street always has the same pace.
A rat runs agilely at the back of the alley, near a door that once led to the CBGB, and that has now become the seat of a chic clothing store.
Graftification on the walls, an empty 22-ounce bottle of beer, rests next to newspapers that have just been discarded.
Some passers-by said they rarely think of Extra Place, and suggested its enhancement.
Mr. Field, for his part, said he thought that some underestimated the attractiveness of the street in its current state.
“The best years of my life,” he said, “unfolded in dark, damp and disgusting places.”
The Bilingual Debate : Immersion in the English language
In this part of the Education Supervisory program, Bruce Fuller and Lance T. Izumi discuss candidates' position on bilingual education.
Lance T. Izumi, a former member of studies in California, and former director of the Department of Education at the Pacific Research Institute for Public Policy, is the co-author of the book "not as Good as You Thought. Why the middle classes must be able to choose their school."
Attracting the favors of Hispanic voters is a complicated business.
Barack Obama’s proposals on education are an interesting example of this.
Obama’s campaign points out that “African-American and Latino students are less likely to decomplete than white students.”
To combat these differences, Mr. Obama’s education program supports in particular among so many other topics, “bilingual transitional education” for those learning English.
However, Obama’s question is whether his desire to carry out a bilingual education that defends instruction in languages other than English goes beyond his interest in eliminating differences.
For example, Sixth Street Prep, an elementary school in eastern Los Angeles County.
The students of this school are mostly Hispanic, and of low income.
More than a third of students, made up of many newcomers, study English.
Among the students of the CM1, 100% passed the state math exam of 2008, expert level.
Similarly, almost 93% of students passed the English-Art Languages exam
This incredible success was achieved thanks to an ingredient other than Mr. Bamba.
Sixth Street relies on analysis, practice, a constant assessment of abilities and an exemplary attitude.
In addition, and here’s an example that Mr. Obama could copy, reports Linda Mikels, principal of Sixth Street, this school has instituted “full immersion” English language instruction.
Total immersion in the English language focuses on the almost constant use of English in instruction.
Miss Mikels, contrary to bilingual education, told me "we had a huge success with a student who had just arrived from Mexico, 12 months later, if you went without his class, you wouldn't have had any chance to identify her."
"It works," she observes, "it worked for us"
Shouldn't Mr. Obama take an example of Sixth Street Prep and replicate it in the other schools composed mostly of Hispanic students learning English.
The excellent results obtained by this school speak for themselves; but the policies on education within his party (the National Education Associations have long supported bilingual education) and its enormous support for bilingual education obscures the prognosis of Mr. Obama's response.
Although he is supportive of the fact that immigrants are learning English, Mr. Obama recently considered this issue to be insignificant, when he proclaimed that we must all stop polemicating the wording of English-language laws.
"On the contrary, he said, "you have to make sure that your child speaks Spanish."
If Mr. Obama really wants to eliminate the differences, then he should carefully consider the models of education that work, rather than disdaining them or seeing them as insignificant.
Net Fall of Oil Following the New Concerns of the World of Economics
Oil prices fell sharply on Monday, as people fear that the bailout in favor of financial marches will fail to revive the economy.
Crude oil futures, fell by 7 percent arriving at $99.80 per barrel. The Mercantile Stock Exchange of New York, during sales this morning.
Prices have fallen by more than $20 per barrel since last Monday.
Over the past two weeks, commodity markets have been shaken by the financial turmoil of Wall Street, as well as two powerful hurricanes in the Gulf of Mexico.
After experiencing an increase of up to $145.29 per barrel, prices fell to almost $90 per barrel this month, before rebounting again in view of the profound uncertainties of the financial markets.
On Monday, investors are once again worried despite these agreement on the rescue plan, the largest in history, concluded this weekend by congressional leaders.
The news has been mitigated by recent concerns that the crisis would be durative.
In the very last episode of this financial disaster, Citygroup will purchase the banking operations of Wachovia Corporation, the government said Monday.
At the same time, the Belgian, Dutch and Luxembourg governments nationalised the European financial conglomerate, Fortis.
Barclays Capital analysts said this weekend’s frantic negotiations with the rescue agreement “seem not to have managed to revive the market.”
As the economic situation deteriorates, demand for raw materials, including oil, is expected to slow down.
"The foresight of global equity, interest rates and market exchange, has become uncertain," Deutsche Bank analysts wrote in a letter to their investors.
“We believe that raw materials cannot escape this contagion.
"As far as raw materials are concerned, our most pressing concern is to understand how far the U.S. virus would spread globally and in particular in China."
Analysts at the bank reduced their expectations for next year, given the drops in oil consumption due to the slowdown in economic growth, were therefore forced to decrease by 20 percent, forecasts of oil and gas prices for 2009.
The Determining Second-Leave Festival for the Improvement of the Team's Record.
