Gebrselassie rested, exceeds his world record.
Haile Gebrselassie, an Ethiopian, once again proved to be worthy of his title as the best background runner of the century, after breaking his own world record at the Berlin Marathon on Sunday, becoming the first to run 26.2 miles in less than 2 hours and 4minutes.
With a win in 2:03 :59 of an accommodating race on the loose, Gebrselassie broke his 2:04 :26 record by 27 seconds, played last year in Berlin.
At 35, Gebrselassie won the 26th world record of his superb career during which he won the world championship or the world record for any distance, from the 1600meters of the marathon.
Last month, Gebrselassie had missed the Beijing Olympic marathon, fearing - perhaps exaggerated in today's eyes, - that air pollution does not increase his asthma problems.
On the contrary, he promised to run in Berlin.
In near-perfectual-sunday weather conditions, 48 degrees at the start, 55 at the finish – Gebrselassie won on Sunday, maintaining an impressive average of one mile in 4 minutes 43 seconds
Track runners like Gebrselassie and Paul Tergat, both Kenyans, converted into long-distant runners, have contributed to the reduction of the marathon record of more than two minutes in the last ten years, since 1998, when Brazil’s Ronaldo da Costa won in Berlin in 2:06:05.
After Sunday's victory, Gebrselassie told reporters, suffered from calf cramps two weeks before, and missed training for a week.
It is obvious that the rest allowed him to remain bright, but not rusted.
“I’m so happy,” he told reporters.
“Everything was perfect.
I got back to it last week, and today I had doubts; but in the end, everything went for the best.”
We blamed him – especially in Kenya, Ethiopia’s African rival—for having orchestrated his races too much, avoiding the world marathon stars, actually relying on hares as if they were in a track competition, and looking more at world records than the sense of competition.
But this criticism does not diminish the magnitude of Gebrselassie’s abilities as a long-distance runner.
Sunday Gebrselassie, preceded by four Kenyans, recorded a time of 1:02 :04. halfway.
Two elite Kenyan riders, James Kwambai and Charles Kamathi, continued to challenge Gebrselassie after the leaders disappeared.
Just over three miles from the finish, Gebrselassie made his final comeback to detach from Kwambai, who finished second in 2:05 :36, improving his best personal record of five minutes.
Kamathis came third in 2:07:48.
In the women’s race Irina Mikitenko, born in Kazakhstan, now a German citizen, finished a remarkable year getting the seventh time of 2:19:19.
England’s Paula Radcliffe holds the 2:15:25 world record.
Last spring, 36-year-old Mikitenko surprised everyone winning the London Marathon in 2:24:14.
She couldn't participate in Beijing games because of a hypothetical backache, but promised to win in Berlin, improving at her third marathon, her personal best by almost five minutes.
She ran using a skillful strategy, letting the Ethiopian and Kenyan group of runners take on a nice advantage, then surpassing them about 18miles 1⁄2 afterwards.
Askale Tafa Margasa of Ethiopia came second in 2:21:31, while Helena Kiprop, Kenyan, finished third in 2:25:01.
In a City in Part, Pride and the Trials of Black Life
Hidden in the development of its huge park, a few miles from the sun, the brilliant and the giant Orlando, there is a quiet little town where the pride and complications of the African-American experience are born.
"Eatonville, the first all-black town to integrate, Zora Neale Hurston's childhood house, this description she made in 1935, is no longer so simple to do these days." The city with five lakes, three croquet courts, 300 dark skins, 300 good swimmers, full of goyaves, two schools and no prison."
It has now become a pilgrimage town
Alice Walker, Toni Morrison and Ruby Dee came to the Zora Festival in Eatonville every year to pay homage to Hurston, Harlem Renaissance's most famous writer.
And in various ways, the city it describes—has become tourist through its inclusion in the Florida’s tourist guide published by The Depression—era Federal Writers’ Project—remains a special place.
She's as independent, dignified, and private as she already was in the 30's, when Hurston told that Florida's black rural population was often reluctant to share their true thoughts with the white man, who "knowing us so little, doesn't know what he's losing."
Even if it is the year of the election of a black president, Senator Barack Obama, who also invoked an open dialogue on the theme of race, many here remain cautious in the eyes of abroad.
“We’re very cautious about how to tell the story,” said Hortense Jones, 59, who has always resided and the city’s oldest parishioner.
“It has to conform to reality.
Eatonville has long been defined as a paradox of triumph and fighting.
It represents both a historical model of a black seizure of power as well as a community of almost 2400 people where the poverty rate is twice the national average.
It is a central point but also an example of southern black rural cultivation - sometimes disdained, other times acclaimed - which recalls the birth of American slavery.
That would explain why residents are both proud and protective.
Interest in Eatonville's image born with Zora, which everyone here calls Hurston.
She presented her hometown to the world through sincere books, full of dialects, such as "Mules and Men" (1935) and "Their Eyes Were Watching God" (1937).
Five paragraphs in the Florida guide transformed this city, located just outside Route 17, a road that crosses the often-forgotten center of Florida that ends up in a chapter of black history and human drama.
Bold like a bass drum in both life and literature, Hurston led readers to Eatonville's first mayor, Mr. Joe Clarke, who was later divided into several private parties.
"Past the Road on the Left," she writes, "you'll find the modern brown and white public school, with its gardens and well-preserved playgrounds that Howard Miller has always cared for, although he can barely read and write.
She also referred to Widow Dash's new husband and wrote that Lee Glenn" sells drinks of all kinds as well as anything that can serve people passing through.
So, in just a hundred words, Hurston associated Eatonville with a self-government but also to anil literacy, remarriage, and sex.
Of course, Fodor wasn't like that.
It wasn't actually a portrait that everyone appreciated.
Zora described the reality well, "said Ella Dinkins, 90, one of Johnson's young girls that Hurston immortalized, quoting colorless songs, performed by men who praised their beauty."
She added, “Some people didn’t like it.”
Hurston is always remembered as a lively and eccentric person, who came back frequently after his family moved to Jacksonville, Fla.
Augustus Franklin, 77, recalled that Hurston usually arrived in the city without warning, aboard a big Chevrolet, a cigarette in his mouth and wearing pants here, where even today, we are proud of traditional clothes.
Many residents were fascinated, Mr. Franklin recounted, while many mocked her.
“People were always happy to see Zora,” he said.
But he also adds, swinging in his chair of the hiffer patio that overlooks Lake Sabelia, where Hurston was certainly baptized, that "it never remained too long".
When Hurston died in 1960, she was poor and her books were out of production.
Eatonville and the whole world seemed to have forgotten about it.
Even though she was once a star of literature, a beginner by Langston Hughes and the only black woman at Barnard College in the 20's, she was buried in any grave, in Fort Pierce, Fla, where she lived.
In Eatonville, no great commemoration, nor plaque for the general public.
“I don’t think they’ve understood the contribution or legacy she gave the world,” said Valérie Boyd, author of “Wrapped in Rainbows,” a biography of Hurston published in 2003.
There was a crucial moment in the 1980’s.
Orange County public officials wanted to pass a five-lane highway in the city, to replace Boulevard Kennedy, the community's two-lane main street.
The development of Orlando has already pushed Interstate 4 to the western corner of the city.
The proposal was made when Eatonville was just recovering from a difficult period in its history.
Forced integration, among so many other things, put an end to the relatively idyllic isolation of the community.
In the 50's, the dispute over the mixing of races led hatred to the gates of the community.
"At that time, a bunch of young whites were throwing oranges or objects against people sitting on the side," Franklin said.
“A lady even died.”
They threw a watermelon from their car.”
In a 1955 letter to The Orlando Sentinel, Hurston questioned the Supreme Court's request for forced integration, defining his decision in Brown v. Board of Education "insulting rather than glorifious of my race."
Residents are now proclaiming that the elimination of racial segregation in schools, while positive in some aspects, has diluted Eatonville's cohesion and weakened youth confidence.
“Black children used to be kissed – I remember well – we were kissing our master in the morning, and in the evening too,” said N.Y. Nathiri, daughter of Ella Dinkins as well as chief director of the Eatonville Community Safeguard, a non-profit group.
This lasted, she added, until teachers and students no longer came from the same place.
“You don’t kiss your white teacher because he’s white – I mean there’s a line of cultural demarcation here,” Nathiri said.
Civilian rights, moreover, have made it possible to create space for many other Zora Neale Hurston - black writers, actors and artists who have gone beyond the prejudice, such as her, armed with a joyful self-confidence and lines of conduct such as: "how can they deprive themselves of the pleasure of my company!"
It's beyond me."
In 1975, writer Alice Walker went to Hurston's anonymous tomb and began to struggle to revive his reputation.
Five years later, an acclaimed biography of Hurston directed by Robert E. Hemenway, a real best seller, reintroduced her to the American canons.
The highway project came just as Eatonville’s most famous girl had regained her appeal.
And this time, Hurston’s old neighbors consider her a savior.
The community began organizing a Hurston festival to demonstrate the catastrophic consequences of this highway.
Hundreds of fans came to the inauguration of the festival two years later, and many return in January to commemorate it.
After many years, thalf has rejected the proposal of the highway.
"The fiasco of the five-lane highway, extremely fast, a real civic pride," said Ms. Nathiri.
Ms. Boyd summed it up stating that "Zora saved Eatonville."
The victory against the project of the highway allowed him to change his own image.
Non-residents, such as Rachelle Munson, a lawyer who has been attending our church since 1993, began to come in large numbers, and residents were beginning to reconsider the past.
Eatonville has been classified as a historic city since 1998.
A mono-theme library (of course renamed Hurston) opened in 2006, on a magnificent Kennedy Boulevard, repaved.
Today, Eatonville remains an anomaly for Florida: six miles from Orlando, she can at any time consider herself as a late-street back on a summer rainy day as she was when she was founded by 27 black families in the 1880s.
There are now 90 percent of black people.
Foreigners who come to discover Eatonville's history, his raison d'être, are still treated with caution.
You have to ask permission for most interviews, and some things — like the walls of Eatonville’s old church, painted by the white man, illustrating black men in the fields—must not be photographed.
Many in Eatonville, such as Melle Jones, a brave teacher who has a weakness for bright red, are always afraid that their island community is well understood.
However, given the expansion of the Hurston Festival, a certain level of local pride has emerged.
Young people in particular, tend to consider Eatonville as Hurston considered his entire race: beautiful, problematic and everything, neither better nor worse, but also proud, creative, hardworking, crazy and mixed as other ethnic and racial groups in America.
It is indeed the sincerely civic affection heard in Mr. Franklin's nephew, Edwin Harvey, 18, who plans to return to Eatonville after college to work for the local government or the police forces, who need additional forces.
And even for the youngest, such as Alondra and Alexia Kenon of 11years, twins of Winter Park, seem to have learned correctly the Eatonville description.
"Most of the people, who pass in this area there, will think: 'Oh, this city has nothing compared to others' Alondra Kenon said after Mass last Sunday.
But if you stop and take the time to contemplate history, it is a very beautiful city."
Here in New York, everyone speaks fluent finance.
In spite of everything, nothing has changed.
If tourists in Manhattan stayed away from the kiosk and giant TV screens in Times Square, they wouldn’t realize they’re in the eye of the cyclone.
They could never think that in the glass and honeycomb offices above their heads, bankers and hedging fund managers monopolizing New York pass the worst “work day in the office” since the crack of 1929.
Deliverers continue to sneak on the sidewalks, with their wagons and muffin trays for morning meetings.
The Latinos always hang between residential buildings wearing, like mules, about fifty luxurious shirts hanging from a yoke arranged on his shoulders.
Office workers continue to queue to buy their morning sandwich from street vendors, and it’s still impossible to find a taxi between 4pm and 7pm on weekdays.
I didn’t feel the crisis despite the completely pessimistic stories until this afternoon when I decided to go open a new savings account.
Luckily I was planning to work in the late afternoon, as I was discovering at my local subsidiary of JP Morgan Chase, about twenty people queuing.
Not in front of the cashier counters, nor to withdraw from the cash or make a payment, but in front of the customer service counter, where the usual customers ordered their checkbooks, the tourists applied for management for the Macy store, and new customers opened accounts.
Most of those in front of me, I'd later learn from Starr, my "authorized personal banker", were all lining up for the same reason as me.
Not in the same way, because they had already withdrew their savings from smaller banks, such as those in New Jersey, Pennsylvania, and Staten Island.
They withdrew their money from the savings accounts called thrifts (such as a construction company), credit associations, and the type of local savings banks and their subsidiaries that operate only in one state, and who claim to treat customers like their families.
In the recent past, 15 of these banks have gone bankrupt.
In recent days, Washington Mutual, a $327 capital bank, has resorted to bankruptcy trustee, and as New Yorkers went home for the weekend this Friday night, another bank, Wachovia, with savings accounts worth US$440 million, was desperately looking for a buyer to save it.
The office and store workers in front of me, frightened by another potential debacle, transferred their money to a bank that seems reliable to them, the one that does the most rescue on Wall Street.
Some New Yorkers have become very familiar to the world of finance in recent weeks.
"I heard a lady at my local Chinese supermarket talking on the phone to Lehman Brothers, the bank that went bankrupt, and its meager CEO Dick Fuld, with the same familiarity she could have had to criticize a colleague.
And Lyn, my building’s uniformed janitor, changed the topic of our usual conversation about Brad and Angelina or deliveries from my fruit and vegetable dealer.
She told me, following my visit to the bank. "I put all my savings into the money market.
“I’ve changed, I don’t want to take a risk.”
Desecrated Muslim tombs as Austria oscillates to the right.
Heinz-Christian Strache, who abandoned the right wing of the Austrian Freedom Party and Filip Dewinter of the Belgian right-wing Vlaams Belang, took part in a demonstration to present the organization of "cities against Islamization" in Antwerp, Thursday, January 17, 2008.
They wear road signs illustrating striped mosques in red.
The police accused far-right extremists of desecrating an Austrian Muslim cemetery, the weekend of the victory of far-Right political parties in the country's general elections.
More than 90 tombs were seriously damaged at Traun Cemetery, near Linz, between the night of Friday and this morning; it is according to the police, an organized action.
Delinquents have painted on several tombs, Jewish symbols such as David's Star; however, detectives believe that this could have been done to disguise the extremists' sense of hatred towards Muslim immigrants.
Could this accident somehow be related to the elections.
A spokesman for the Local Islamic Community said he was "deeply shocked" at the announcement of this desecration, which comes just during the religious month of Ramadan that is coming to an end.
Austria is seriously thinking of regaining its courage, following its oscillation to the right in yesterday's parliamentary elections.
The two far-right parties that won almost 30 percent of the vote, the Freedom Party and the Alliance for the Future of Austria, waged an anti-immigration campaign. Some of their slogans were even, according to critics, as xenophobic.
Heinz-Christian Strache, the leader of the Freedom Party who won more than 18 percent of the votes, ran his campaign against the wearing of the scarf and burkas, and even voiced his opposition to alaha food.
During his final race in Vienna, he spoke of "a European fraternity" to avoid the rise of Islam.
Both parties are trying to prevent the construction of mosques and minarets, proclaiming that they would constitute a political symbol of the Islamization of Austria and Europe.
Polls and analyses carried out just after the elections, defined the extreme right as the strongest political bloc in the country, which is demonstrated by the majority of young voters who made the difference, because worried about their future in the European Union.
Gary Glitter forbids traveling abroad.
The courts have banned glamorous rock star Gary Glitter, accused of child sexual abuse, expatriation in France and Spain.
The magistrates imposed on Glitter a six-month banishment, after he expressed to the authorities who have him under control, his intention to travel to Spain passing through France.
The police in Kent granted this travel ban last Thursday after proving Glitter’s guildom for child sexual abuse among magistrates.
Glitter, or of his real name, Paul Gadd, was not present at the hearing at Ashford's courts of limited jurisdiction, but was still represented by his lawyer Corker Binning.
A court spokesman told Ashford's court "on Thursday, September 25, the Kent police requested an application to prevent Mr. Paul Gadd from traveling to France and Spain until March 25.
“Paul Gadd wasn’t there then.”
Glitter, 64, has informed the authorities that he wants to go abroad, even if it means being registered in the lists of accused of sexual abuse.
He must inform the authorities if he wishes to go abroad for more than three days.
He must also inform the police, 7 days before his scheduled departure date.
If he does not communicate his movements to the authorities, he could serve up to five years' imprisonment.
Kent police said they would not discuss Glitter's special cases or potential movements in the tale.
A Kent Police spokesman said, "Kent police will do everything necessary to protect our children like those abroad.
“In our opinion, it’s easier to monitor someone who’s staying in the UK because we have devices capable of handling any potential risks, much more efficiently.
“Protecting the residents of Kent is the dearest intention of the Kent Police, and we are closely monitoring the behaviour of those accused of sexual abuse in the area, in order to minimize the potential risks to the public.”
Glitter returned to the United Kingdom last month, after serving 27 months in a Vietnamese prison for abusing two girls, respectively, aged 10 and 11.
When he was released, Glitter flew to Bangkok from where he should have boarded for a flight to London.
But he said he didn’t feel good and refused to leave for London.
The Thai authorities blocked him from passport control, and in the end he went to Hong Kong.
The Chinese authorities refused to enter their territory, and was therefore forced to return to Bangkok; the Thai police and immigration officers insisted that he return to England.
He finally arrived at Heathrow Airport Terminal 3 on August 22 and remained there while his lawyer, David Corker, went to the Uxbridge, West London Limited Jurisdictional Court for recording him on the list of people accused of sexual abuse, as required by Scotland Yard.
In the past, Glitter had already served a four-month sentence in the United Kingdom for detaining images of child abuse.
An Interior Ministry source said Jacqui Smith was reassured and happy to know that Glitter had been barred from travelling to France and Spain.
"I photograph the master off the stage to capture elements of his character" Günther Bauer - Photographer
No heels, no strange clothes.
The essence of the great masters of flamenco, freed from all tensions to obtain the relaxed appearance of the man or woman.
On the one hand the artist.
On the other, the person.
Inseparable except in intimacy, in these moments unknown to the general public, where we discover intimate gestures and stolen glances of artists who do not care to go on stage.
The German Günther Bauer mixes in "Flamenco - people" the theatricality of a few stagings with singers, dancers and guitarists with this lesser-known side of artists.
In this project, the photographer takes the particular iconography of flamenco from different angles.
Included in the Flemish Photography Month programming, the exhibition "reflects the international importance of flamenco, greater every day", explains the creator, who clarifies that "Flamenco - people" "can be anyone but, after seeing the photos, the observer realizes that they are not typical images of artists on stage.
These people photographed "in the street" are artists able to pass on stage passion, sadness, joy ... by captivating the audience.
To capture moments of personality of these artists do not have to take pictures on stage.
They have a personality and a character of their own.
What interested me was to create an intimate atmosphere that allows me to capture with the camera moments of personality of flamenco stars ".
With the festival "Chant populaire des Mines", Bauer began in 1997 a long trip to photograph singers, guitarists or dancers with a plate camera.
They are portraits that, thanks to the choice of the camera and the equipment, recalls classical photography.
The implementation of different stylistic means breaks with the classical side of the image, giving it a touch of modernity, this reflects the perception of flamenco according to Bauer: "Music with very traditional roots that evolves and surprises every day".
It is not a question of dancing or singing in itself, but of the personality and character of the artist who captivates the public with his art.
The search for the essentials and the magic was the constant of this work that is presented these days at the El Fotómata gallery.
Flamenco stars such as Diego El Cigala, Chano Lobato, Cristina Hoyos, Enrique Morente, El Lebrijano, Antonio Canales, Vicente Amigo, among others, are part of the selection made from about seventy artists photographed.
The project turned into an anthology of flamenco, which will soon be published in a collection book of portraits exhibited in the gallery.
Bauer's passion for portraits led him to repeat the idea two years ago with the "Portraits of Old Sportsmen" ("SportARTproject").
Although the concepts are different in "Flamenco - people", the photographer admits that he loves photographing the portrait of people, knowing them and being able to enter their lives for a more or less long moment.
I believe that in photography the portrait is the most difficult genre.
You find yourself in front of people, characters that sometimes you don’t know or that only leave you a few minutes to be able to give them a picture.
Despite these circumstances, as a photographer, I always try that my ideas become reality.”
This collection of portraits of the current flamenco entitled "Flamenco - people", is on display at the El Fotómata Gallery, located at number 20 rue de la Mata (street parallel to Hercules' Polestory) which will be opened until October 16.
Vargas Llosa confesses that the novel he writes is "true audacity"
The novel takes places that nobody knows and deals with unknown subjects, but once again Mario Vargas Llosa is determined to advance the new project on which he is working, a novel whose main character is Roger Casemant, an Irish nationalist who has lived twenty years in the Congo and has passed through the Amazon.
The favorite at the Nobel Prize in Literature confessed yesterday at the Hay festival Segovia, that the novel that currently occupies it "is a real daring" and that "everything demonstrates that he should not write it".
“If I were reasonable, I wouldn’t, but I’m going to do it and I’m hoping to finish it,” he concluded in his speech at the festival of exchange of ideas and thought, which for four days saw a hundred writers, historians and reporters and thousands of people march through the city.
The author of "The City and the Dogs" was the most awaited figure of the third edition of this festival and fulfilled his contract.
Vargas Llosa, who won the Hay Festival award by a majority of the voices of the attendees, was a packed room at the Juan Bravo Theatre.
The presenter of the Festival, Félix Romeo, found himself in front of a fascinated audience that did not see the past hour during which the Peruvian, with a prodigious memoir and with a great dose of humour, retraced the birth of his literary vocation, which is due to "his thirst to read" and "to live new experiences"; how he wrote in a "very methodical way"
The Spanish-American novelist acknowledged that "there is no way" to measure and know if books change life. "But I refuse to believe that literature is pure amusement, an ephemeral experience", stressed Vargas Llosa, who said that it "has effects" and "makes people less similar", because we asked the writers "not to let themselves go"
Bad weather ruins Pozoblanco Fair
The banner's party which concluded the bull races at the Pozoblanco fair was suspended yesterday morning, after the toreadors on horseback verified that the arena was impracticable.
In this way, the organizing committee, in agreement with Fermín Bohórquez, Pablo Hermoso de Mendoza and Léonard Hernández, has decided to suspend the celebration of the bull race.
The bad weather was relentless on this fair, since finally two out of three parties could not take place.
Saturday is the rain that prevented the celebration of the second scheduled bull race.
On the poster, there was José Luis Moreno, Curro Díaz y Juan Bautista, who should have competed in the arena of Castilblanco
So only the Friday party took place
Friday, the winner was Daniel Luque who cut three ears and was carried in triumph, after having made arm movements in front of the bull with taste and aesthetics.
The same afternoon Alejandro Talavante concluded on two banners planted on the back of the bull and a chopped ear that the uncovered bleachers applauded more than his fight in itself with the bull.
Worse still for El Juli, which has entered the arenas of Los Llanos without pain or glory.
In his fight with the second bull, after a markedly better fight, he was whistled by most of the audience.
Police control of a van in the province of Alava: two thousand kilos of hashish found
Five hours less than ten in the morning.
Traffic control of the Euskadi (Ertzaintza) police at the border of the Commune of Armiñón (Alava).
The police officers arrest two occupants of a van and the latter two begin the leak.
Its loading, more than two tons of hashish, enough to stop them.
The polices prosecuted them and arrested one of the two occupants, a 28-year-old man, identified as A.A.J., according to information leaked by the Basque Government's Department of the Interior.
The arrested individual has been charged with drug trafficking and falsification of documents.
The incident took place when various officials of the Alava Traffic Unit carried out vehicle control over the N-1, on the border of the Armiñón Commune.
They were about to stop a van to inspect it when the driver disobeyed orders, abruptly accelerated and control began.
Officers began their pursuit until the vehicle stopped two kilometers away on the aisle, where the two occupants descended to flee into a wooded area.
Basque police finally arrested one of the two occupants and seized the 2,178 kilograms of hashish they were transporting that they found after inspecting the van, an Iveco Daily.
The later part of the vehicle was loaded with hachisch bales. Then they proceeded with his transfer to a police station for an exhaustive inspection and to weigh the drug.
In addition, the officials found that the license plate had been "falsified" since it did not coincide with that written on the van's registration card, which is also why they charged the inmate with an offence of falsification of documents.
Ertzaintza continues its investigation to arrest the other suspect, who managed to escape and to determine the origin of the seized drug.
The 28-year-old detainee, A.A.J., has been taken into custody to carry out the various steps and will be tried within hours.
Actress Heather Locklear arrested for driving under the effect of medication
Actress Heather Locklear, the famous Amanda of the television series Melrose Place, was arrested this weekend in Santa Bárbara (California) for driving under the effect of a drug.
A witness saw her make strange maneuvers while trying to get out of her garage in Montecito, according to what a spokeswoman for the California Police Patrol revealed to People magazine.
The witness clarified that around 16h30, Locklear "breathed fully on the accelerator, making a lot of noise, trying to get the car out of the garage, and going backwards several times, she rolled on her sunglasses".
Shortly thereafter, the witness, who initially did not recognize the actress, saw Locklear stopped in the middle of the highway and got out of his vehicle.
This person, at that time, called the emergency services because "she was worried about Locklear's life."
When the police patrol arrived she found the actress sitting inside her vehicle, which was partially blocking the road.
"She looked disoriented", that's why the agents took her to the center specializing in drugs and alcohol to carry out a test.
According to a spokesman for the police, the actress was cooperative and the abuse of alcohol was dismissed from the beginning, although "according to the observation of police officers at first sight, we thought Locklear was under the influence of a drug".
Locklear was arrested on suspicion of driving under the effect of a substance that has not been specified and was incarcerated around 19.00 a.m. and released a few hours later.
Two months ago, Locklear came out of the Arizona clinic where she was interned to treat a nervous breakdown.
Llamazares requested the Andalusian federation of the United Left to assume its responsibilities in the Silva case
The general coordinator and deputy of the United Left, Gaspar Llamazares, said today that it is the regional federation to make a decision on the case of the former youth and sports councillor of the Municipality of Seville, Francisco Manuel Silva, who resigned last week after being accused of carrying out preferential treatments to the members of his family.
"In these cases, I am very federalist, and it will be the Andalusian organization that will have to assume its responsibilities," Llamazares said with regard to the behavior of the political party that it will have to adopt within the Seville City Council.
However, the leader of the United Left called for the investigations to show "transparency" for the irregularities that Silva could have committed: "The United Left has always defended this kind of business so that they have a maximum degree of transparency".
He also stressed that the political party has "very strict ethical codes for all officials".
"Sangre de Mayo", Garci's daring bet
Drawing inspiration from the stories of Benito Pérez Galdós, José Luis Garci recreates the famous battle of the Spanish against the French in "Sangre de Mayo", which is the most ambitious film of his filmography with nearly 15 million euros of budget.
The filmmaker tried "to stage the many characters and illustrate the social analysis" that he has always admired in the work of the famous novelist, he explained today during the presentation of the film, with Quim Gutiérrez and Paula Echevarría at the head of the long list of actors.
The more than two-and-a-half-hour-and-a-half-housed film “Sangre de mayo” will be released in movie theaters next Friday, which will then become a television series for Telemadrid of more than four hours.
An admirer of Galdós literature, José Luis Garci assures having realized with this shoot "a childhood dream" thanks to Esperanza Aguirre, who instructed him, three years ago, to shoot a commemorative film for the two hundred years of May 2 Madrid, something that he has always accepted when the story stuck to the national historical facts.
The 15 million euros provided by the Municipality of Madrid constitute for Garci a "single opportunity" to reproduce with the appropriate decorations and clothes the atmosphere of "a Spain who made the wrong leader for the fight, since, the struggle for independence led the people to rebellions under the reign of Ferdinand VII", he said.
But the filmmaker regrets that he did not obtain funding as for the films "Captain Alatriste" or "The ghosts of Goya" - more than double that obtained for "Sangre de mayo" - because he could not shoot a few scenes written in a script signed by Horacio Valcárcel, with whom he collaborated for most of his career.
The filmmaker of "Volver a empezar" influenced his usual way of working, on the staging of the actors, with whom he has the habit of rehearsing before starting filming and to whom he does not casting, since it seems "humiliating" and considers that "the role of the filmmaker is to go to the theater and the cinema to keep himself informed".
Many actors in "Sangre de mayo" rehearse in front of the camera, such as Miguel Rellán, Carlos Larrañaga or Fernando Guillén Cuervo.
Four-time Oscar candidate (he won one), Garci considers that the three candidate films that represent Spain this year at the Hollywood Academy, “Sangre de mayo”, “Seven French pool tables” and finally the winner “Blind Sunflowers”, are not representative of young Spanish cinema, “which deserves to be better known,” he said.
With this film Garci doesn't expect a price.
“At first you think you’re working for posterity, then you realize that you’re only working for prosperity, and now you’re content to make an extra movie,” Garci said after more than 30 years of professional career.
Isabelle Bayón puts herself in the shoes of Sevillian dancer Tórtola Valencia
A Sevillian dancer will pay tribute to such a Sevillian dancer.
Carmen Tórtola Valencia, daughter of a Catalan father (Florenç Tórtola Ferrer) and an Andalusian mother (Georgina Valencia Valenzuela), was born in the Triana district and who at the age of three will live in London with her family.
She was a free dancer and an oriental dancer.
She had an exciting life, a pioneer of the liberation of the woman, such as Isadora Duncan, Virginia Woolf or Sarah Bernhardt.
She was a Buddhist and vegetarian and advocated for the abolition of the corset that prevented the free movement of women.
The life of this woman who marked the history of dance in Europe of the inter-wars, fascinated Isabelle Bayón.
"I wanted to work with Pepa Gamboa and the latter offered me to interpret Sevillian dancer Tórtola Valencia and it seemed incredible."
According to the dancer, "we're not trying to recount her life but translating her way of thinking.
The titles of his dances made me think of flamenco.
Thus, "thousander dance" made me think of a Cuban popular dance and "snake dance" to some tangos.
Her dance was sensual and they told me that my way of dancing was identical to hers,” she says.
The staging was made thanks to two remarkable contributions: that of Miguel Poveda who will sing Isabela again in one of his shows, as he had done at "La puerta abierta"; and that of Professor Matilde Coral, who exceptionally wanted to collaborate with the one who was his student.
"Matilde turns Tórtola into a mature character.
“I’m happy because she agreed to play a role in my work and it honors me.”
The staging was also made thanks to the musical composition and guitars of Jesús Torres y Paco Arriaga, the popular song by Michel Ortega, el Pulga y Moi de Morón; El Pájaro's percussion, as well as Rafa El Viola's viola and the collaboration of an actorator, Juanfra Juárez, "who plays the role
This work also recounts, in a way, the life of this woman who exhibited herself in such distant places, especially for the time, as in India, the United States, or in Russia, but also throughout Europe, since she usually resided in London.
"I wanted to recall it because I think it's these women who have an enormous influence in their time and now no one knows and highlights his work," Bayón says.
The show will start at 20h30 at the Lope de Vega Theatre
Publication of the "Journal of a recently married poet" concludes the Triennate
The presentation of the first illustrated edition of the "Journal of a Newly Married Poet" in Saint Stephen's Church in New York, where Juan Ramón Jiménez married Zenobia in March 1916, is part of the activities planned by the Ministry of Culture of Andalusia to celebrate the "Triennate Zenobia and Juan Ramón" (2006-2008), organized to commemorate
In this way, the "Triennate Zenobia and Juan Ramón" comes to an end with the reissue of this work, with the International Congress which took place in New York, with the restoration of the Nobel Museum and with the travelling exhibition "Juan Ramón Jiménez: This poplar of light" which was inaugurated last May and which, after traveling to the various Andalusian provinces
Similarly, the Ministry of Culture of Andalusia bought eight hundred copies of the "Journal of a Recently Married Poet", edited by Pedro Tabernero and illustrated by Jacobo Pérez-Enciso, to perpetuate the work of one of the most well-known Andalusian poets.
America likes less without Paul Newman
“Sometimes God creates perfect people
“Paul Newman was part of it.”
This sentence is by actress Sally Field, who worked with him in "Absence of Malice", and illustrates the desperation of American cinema, and of all of America, in the face of the striking idea that Newman is no longer of this world.
He struggled to get an Oscar
He hasn’t been shooting since 2007.
Everyone knew he was sick.
It was a hard blow for everyone.
“Sometimes what one feels is beyond expression, and now I’ve lost a great friend,” Robert Redford cried, “my life and my country were better because of its existence.”
No one was afraid to be ridiculous or exaggerated.
One of the fathers of cinema and the homeland died during a particularly obscure period, when terrorists and stock markets scared, the esteem of the Americans fell very low, desperately seeking motives of dignity.
When times are so hard and without reason to live, human beings only have admiration and it is not necessary to be Luis Cernuda to love Paul Newman.
It embodied for everyone an ideal of beauty and truth.
He was Richard Nixon's enemy and defender of lost and defeated causes, such as the foundation for anti-drug fighting created in honor of his eldest deceased following an overdose and the creation of his own brand of organic food.
It was a victory for a country that struggled to impose itself against a suicidal food culture.
This expansive wave of admiration swept Hollywood without paying attention to nationalities or passports.
The last James Bond, the Englishman Daniel Craig, found that no James Bond died before him.
Another Englishman, Oscar-winning Sam Mendes, said that working with Newman marked the peak of his career.
The bad boy arrived from Australia, Russell Crowe, dared to name Newman as his greatest inspiration.
“He set us the bar high,” said George Clooney, who is perhaps the only one today who is trying to pierce the screen like Paul Newman did: by being very honest and having a serious private life away from goals.
It’s still not easy or possible in the media jungle these days.
But there's one who's trying, and Paul Newman is an example.
After a divorce, he was married fifty years to Joanne Woodward, to whom he was faithful even if he did not miss the opportunities not to be.
"But why should I eat a burger out of my home, if I have a steak in my fridge?" he used to joke.
In heaven, he attends with pride to meet his daughters with his fans on earth, something that is not so usual in America.
“Among all the great roles he’s played, he’s never been as proud as that of the devoted husband, the love father, the caring grandfather and philanthropist,” he said.
Madrid in the race to organise the 2016 Olympic Games
And yes, the dream of many of us is for Madrid to be able to host the Olympic Games, after the failure in 2012, it could come true in 2016.
But I'm wondering if we're ready.
If we take the example of yesterday's race: we are not!
They call it the perfect race, but in my opinion it was the worst race I’ve ever participated in.
If they had taken into account all the errors that occurred during the race, they would be the subject of an eliminatory rating.
Let’s look at everything in detail and from the beginning.
The mistakes begin with the removal of the bibs, which forces you to go early.
It's not bad but if they make you move it would be better for them to give you everything (including the electronic chip for shopping).
Well, no
They made you go there only to take the bib (first mistake, move only for that) and (after a long queue) surprise!, there was only the size 'S' for the jersey (second mistake, because I need a size XL)
What world do we live in?
Is it because of the crisis they have decided to buy only small-sized jerseys with less fabric and therefore cheaper?
Well, this is nothing compared to what happened on racing day.
Apart from the fact that you have to go very early to remove the chip, the queue is 15 minutes and, moreover, you have to leave your backpack at the entrance.
The wait lasted more than 35 minutes, this is incomprehensible given that 11,000 people are expected for these races.
Since there was a long queue, the competitions started and we hadn't left our backpack at the entrance yet, although once done, the withdrawal was worse.
In addition, the rain accompanied the expectation of more than 40 minutes during which there were rebellions and assaults of the shack.
I believe that about 500 people will have pneumonia tomorrow, and some people like me, will not even appear in the classification with the exact time, because of the chip they have given me which, I think, did not correctly mark the departure.
Of course, if they asked me to cite an example of a perfect race, this one would not come to my mind.
Thus I believe that we can always dream of becoming the host city for the 2016 Olympic Games.
Microsoft is looking for a (virtual) receptionist
At the conference on Emerging Technologies at MIT Microsoft was presented a new system of image recognition and human interaction, a kind of Artificial Intelligence with little intelligence but capable of accomplishing a few basic tasks, such as calling a taxi (or in the case of Microsoft, a shuttle to get around the company's campus) in response to what it was asked during a conversation.
Thanks to a camera and a system of recognition of shapes and faces, this "receptionist" can know how many people there are in the place, who they are - including remembering what positions they occupy - and listening and hearing the keywords of their conversations.
But also regroup them according to conversation groups
In the presentation video, the receptionist detects a third person but she understands that she does not belong to the group that is talking with her and that she is not part of the company.
She asks him if she's here to visit someone and she tells them she'll answer them in a few moments.
All this without a blue screen.
Impressive.
But of course this represents the future.
Now the company has other things to focus on, for example on the next version of its operating system, Windows.
This week we talked about a change of strategy at Microsoft.
Windows 7 will be lighter and it won’t have as many associated programs.
Steve Ballmer discovered that if the computers were sold with the essential instead of useless applications, they would go five times faster.
This represents a discovery since Microsoft, version after version, has not stopped adding functions and "extras" to Windows.
Changes in strategy are welcome.
Microsoft is right, its operational system loses importance but also its software.
When Google Chrome arrived on the network, I was one of those who adhered to the theory that Google Chrome is Google’s first step to competing with Windows.
Several people rightly pointed out that an Internet browser is not an operational system.
The operational system will always be necessary since it is the indispensable basis for computer hardware and software to be understood.
He holds the same position as your wife or husband when you have to say something to your mother-in-law.
That's right.
It will never disappear, but we must see the "death" of the operational system not as an absolute truth but as a relative truth.
Ballmer confirmed the idea this week.
People pay less and less attention to the operational system but more and more to the Internet browser.
We turn on the computer only to run Firefox, Explorer or Chrome and every day people spend more time browsing the Internet than browsing windows.
Who serve half of the applications that come with the system?
Which can the less can the least.
And Windows 7 will have to contain fewer apps, much less than Vista.
Less heavy, crowded month, less complex.
For the moment, Microsoft is more interested in the launch of its own online services so that they finally become an alternative to Google.
They desperately need this, indeed, and I'll give you an example.
The photo featured in this article, was taken earlier this month in one of the resting rooms reserved for Microsoft employees in Redmond.
On the beverage dispenser, a poster asks them to use their own search tool - "Your research. Our future".
In 2006, a study concluded that 66% of Microsoft employees used Google to search for information on the Internet.
Last week a delay occurred in the new version of Windows Mobile 7.
It won't be released at the beginning of 2009 but towards the end of 2009 or see in 2010.
Microsoft will surely try to launch at the same time Windows 7 and Windows Mobile 7.
The phone, after all, increasingly resembles a computer.
But the launch of Android being late and the iPhone is already on the market, it will cost them dearly.
Fabulous Marcel
Pablo Picasso endorsed it on the French Riviera, Leonardo di Caprio wore one in Edward Zwick’s ‘Blood Diamant’, as well as Al Pacino in Francis Ford Coppola’s ‘The Godfather’ and is part of the holding of the Muscled Gay Association.
And if we begin to enumerate all his appearances, we could also quote this strong mustachu who lifted weights in a traveling circus.
It's not just a men's bodysuit made of cotton, molding and sleeveless.
The English call it 'tank' but the Fabulous Marcel of Brussels stands out among all the others.
First of all, by its flamboyant name - although it is known as 'Marcel' - which has to do with the sport since it takes the name, according to legend, of boxer Marcel Cerdan, a lover of singer Edith Piaf.
The 'Marcel' is distinguished from the others by its excellent cotton, by its unisex cut, by its distinctive logo and by the small Belgian flag (barely recognizable) that the best-knowing bodies have in the neck.
But be careful, because the Belgian brand does not only produce top tanks.
But it also produces sweatshirts, retro boxers, cashmere cardigans, cotton pants, babies, mini-shorts...
Apparatus designed for the comfort of underwear.
Even though it's a shame to only wear them as underwears.
They're too charming.
"I want to become a young mother; the candidate doesn't worry me"
His enormous resemblance to his sister did not prevent him from developing his own personality.
Monica Cruz is small but full of vitality.
Currently the Cruz sisters take their new activity in fashion very seriously.
According to Monica, they’re not just beautiful and famous girls lending their names.
It was present at the promotion that the Mango brand made in Paris at its new headquarters but as well as at the presentation of the most important fashion prizes that the Spanish house sponsors.
Monica is warm and shy.
Tell us what you're doing in Paris.
I came to present the new collection that my sister Penelope and I created for Mango for the new autumn-winter season, but also to attend the presentation of the second edition of the 'Mango Fashion Awards ' and the dinner of the Opera in honor of Valentino.
A very intense day but, at the same time, exciting, awaits us.
What’s your new collection created for Mango for this fourth season?
The cinema of the 50's and 60's delights us and we are very inspired by it for our collections.
I really like to walk around the city and observe women to see how they are dressed, how they combine their clothes, I do it often.
Dancer, actress and draftswoman, you're a very versatile woman: right?
“I think yes, but in the end everything comes together and these activities have a lot in common.
Since I was very small the clothes, I wanted to go to my grandmother's house to look into my aunt's wardrobes and to see her clothes.
It was great, almost better than going to the amusement park.
Then, when I became a dancer, I was always trying to take care of the way I dressed.
Dancers like to dress well for classes and tests, to look at each other in the mirror and feel good.
And in the role of an actress, when you take on the clothes, that's when you really put yourself in the skin of the character.
As a result, it's all connected.
You didn't have 30 years ago: was it hard for you?
Not at all.
I'm feeling better than ever, I'm thrilled 30.
I find that the woman is more mature, has more experience and enjoys life with less problems.
The biological clock doesn't scare me.
I've done all the things I've wanted to do since I was a kid and it gave me a lot of internal happiness.
So do you think you're becoming a mom?
Of course, I want to become a mom because my body asks me and, in addition, I want to become a young mother; I don’t want to lose that.
As for the candidate, I'm not worried.
What I will do is give a lot of happiness to my son and raise him with a lot of love.
Let's come to the movies, you're waiting for the various movies you shot last year to come out.
That's right, I can't wait for them to come out.
Me who is a woman from the theater, I am very impatient and worried, I would like to have everything now but of course it is not possible.
I can’t wait for the films ‘La última hora’ and ‘En busca de la tumba de Cristo’.
Seeing what you're thinking...
A few days ago you started on the bridge with Naomi Campbell, didn't you?
Don't say that!
I didn't get on the bridge to make the model.
Quite simply, Naomi Campbell called me, I have known her for quite some time, to invite me to participate in London in a charity parade for her association in favor of supporting women and the clothes that we exhibited - I exhibited two clothes from our collection created for Mango - were auctioned to raise money for Naomi's NGO.
The people that Naomi is a person with a tender heart.
They say so much about her, I know her, a lot isn’t true.
In addition, this good side of Naomi fascinates me.
She asked me to march for such a noble cause, but if she'd asked me to make the pear tree, I would have done it anyway.
The important thing is to make life better for women in the Third World.
Recurrers of driving alcohol will have to install the 'alcolock' system
The Minister of the Interior wants the driver who has had more than three administrative penalties for blood alcohol, to have to install in his vehicle a system that prevents starting the vehicle intoxicated, or if it does not blow.
The initiative has already been announced last June and it now appears in the report of the Minister of State in which this initiative is considered as a pilot project that will last one year.
Its goal is to reintegrate the driver by offering road rehabilitation courses.
The Minister of State in charge of Road Safety but also the General Directorate of Road Safety and the Technological Institute Foundation for Automotive Safety will participate in this initiative.
According to the statistics, between 30% and 75% of motorists arrested for having driven more than once intoxicated, the car resumes.
The alcolock would avoid this since it is a system connected to the vehicle that measures the level of alcohol in the breath and prevents starting the vehicle if the driver does not blow before; or when the driver's alcohol level is higher than that allowed.
Its cost is more than 1,000 euros and is the responsibility of the driver.
In this regard, a study conducted in the United States in 2007 showed that vehicles equipped with this device reduced up to 65% of infringements during the first year of installation.
And in Spain it is estimated that this could save the lives of 115 people every year.
This measure sticks only to administrative penalties for two reasons: to deal with these behaviors before they turn into criminal offences.
And to facilitate its implementation, since the legal reform can be carried out by ordinary means, but, however, the reformation carried out by criminal means requires a reform of the organic law.
The device is not yet available on the Spanish market in any commercial form, although some industrial and functional vehicles already have one on an experimental basis.
Precisely tomorrow, the Congress of Deputies will vote an initiative of the Spanish Socialist Party which proposes to install on school buses this system as was done in France.
In Mexico without 'peep-toes'
At 17h40 precise, official time planned, the plane of the Spanish air force in which the Princes of Asturias traveled landed in the Presidential Hangar of the Benito Juárez airport of Mexico DF.
The sun was on the eve of sunset but its rays were still forced to put their hands as a visor to be able to see the royal couple get off the plane.
At the foot of the stairs, the Spanish ambassador and other authorities were present.
A few hours earlier I had asked in the street, to the taxi driver, if he was aware that the Spanish princes were visiting the city to finish their official trip.
- Ah, all right, we won't be able to go to the airport to pick up customers at this time... are you from here or...?
"No, no sir, I'm Spanish, but I've lived here thinking for two years.
- Ah, all right...
But the Mexican press knows very well who the Princes of Asturias are and knows the importance of the trip.
They are several to wait in the hangar of the airport near the red carpet and to a military row composed of 27 navy soldiers.
The Prince is smiling and dressed in navy blue, the princess, as for her, has hair in the wind, is dressed very soberly and does not wear her usual 'peep-toes'; perhaps they had told her that a thunderstorm threatened.
They make a brief stop with the photographers before passing into the room where the official procession awaits them.
At the door of the hotel where they live, one of the four largest hotels in the city, a funny Mexican television journalist is waiting for them.
The Most Prestigious Fashion Award
There is surely no more suitable place in the world to present a fashion prize than Paris and there is surely no living cartoonist more extraordinary and good live than Valentino.
Mango has just presented the second edition of the Botón-Mango Fashion Awards, in order to strengthen the international image of new fashion talent.
The winner will be rewarded with a prize of 300,000 euros.
Yesterday Sandrina Fasoli, the Belgian outing winner of the first edition, looked at her models in Mango's showcase at 54 Boulevard Haussmann in Paris and she cried almost with emotion.
Admittedly, it is the brand's second largest store in Europe that has just been inaugurated with 1,335 square meters of area.
The first shop is located in London.
The events, as well as the desire to thank the stylist Valentino, president of the jury of the competition, made that we met in a luxurious dinner at the Opéra Garnier in Paris.
With as a table next door that of the extraordinary Chagals family, the presidential table prepared by Mango, seemed even more spectacular.
By having big on his heart and doing against bad fortune good heart, because of the recent death of her brother, Nati put herself a garment of Mango and some ornaments of ELAH, who cut his breath, and arm on it, arm under with his son Luis – a handsome man almost two meters high – she accompanied Valentino to his dinner prepared in her honor.
Andik, the owner of Mango, a gallant man, as well as Baron de Rotchil ended the evening without a jacket because they had lent them to two ladies.
Monica Cruz, who showed up at the store with pants and a shirt – from her own collection – and who for the dinner metamorphosed by wearing a sexy and black long dress, was moved when during the nocturnal tour of the opera, accompanied by Goya Toledo, they showed us the rehearsal room of the ballets and remembering the good old days, she grabbed one of the bars
Inés Sastre did not stay for the visit of the palace outbuildings, but she showed us before on her mobile phone pictures of her son.
The next morning, we had the pleasure of seeing Olivier Martínez – another handsome man who travels by motorcycle – and John Travolta, who arrived at the intercontinental hotel in Paris, with his sheets, his towels and having asked beforehand, that there be no smells in his room.
Stars under the starry sky of Paris.
Oh there, that's life.
1001, the anti-stress number
There is not only 1000 but 1001 ways to forget the chaos and frenzy of everyday life.
And to top it off, they’re all listed in a book that’ll brought relaxation to booksellers on September 30.
The waking-morning saddens us almost when it rings every morning at 7 a.m., but it's even worse when we think about the restless rhythm that the current life imposes on us.
The desk is synonymous with tension and the computer has back pains.
Stress is the evil of the 21st century.
But there are neither one nor two methods to combat daily fatigue and problems related to the world of work.
But 1001, neither one more nor one less.
Apart from the positions of yoga, the classical massage or 'spa' treatments, Susannah Marriott brings together in the pages of a book a whole 'menu' of methods adapted to the taste and time of everyone.
The anti-stress work is called ‘1001 Ways to Relax’ and Will Arrive at the Librarians Directly from the publisher Pearson Alhambra and DK.
If someone believes that it is not possible to obtain tranquility in places such as work or through personal relationships, this 'manual' denies such a belief.
In addition, this manual offers other methods such as contact with nature or 100% natural recipes that help you relax.
If adrenaline doesn’t run through your veins to restore your body’s balance and stress has become a problem, it’s time to sit in the couch, disconnect from your mind and focus on reading.
Talking While Sleeping
A few kids speak during the night.
I don’t relate to those who wake up very awake telling us something that happened the day before, but to the children who talk during their sleep.
The first time this happens it may surprise you, but in reality it doesn't matter.
It is a benign alteration of sleep that is called somniloquie.
This is quite common, especially among children and adolescents.
Of course there's more than one talking during the night.
This phenomenon can appear very sporadically (once in life for example associated with an episode of high fever), or more periodically (every days, once a week, once a month ...), the day after the child does not remember anything anymore.
There is no one-size-fits-all cause that determines its appearance
They are often linked to other sleep alterations such as nightmares, nocturnal terrors, nocturnal bursties (pee in bed) or sleepwalking, but this is not always the case.
As we have said before, they can also be associated with very high fevers, situations of stress or overexcitement.
Either way, it's an unstudied phenomenon.
The intensity with which it occurs is also highly variable.
It may be that he speaks with a quiet little voice, with isolated words, whispers or on the contrary with screams, tears, long speeches ...
In the event that somnilody becomes very frequent, it would be wise to observe the child and to consult a specialist, to check if there is a problem that causes a lot of anxiety and in order to obtain an explanation for this problem.
We have said that it is a benign alteration, which is why there is no treatment, unless it is related to another sleep alteration that can be treated (for example, if it is related to sleep apnea or stress).
What can be done is to take into account a number of very simple preventive measures such as:
Try not to excite the child before going to sleep.
Therefore look for quiet games and avoid programs or television series that are more or less violent.
With very nervous children who are struggling to relax, relaxation techniques can be used to help them calm down internally.
This will allow the child to have a more peaceful sleep.
As soon as the somniloquies appear try to remain calm.
Remember that they are harmless to the child and that the next day he will no longer remember what happened.
Useful Advice
Don't wake him up.
It's useless because he's unconscious and you'd wake him.
Even if he talks, he sleeps and rests.
Remember that if it is very anxious several times, it is advisable to consult a doctor or check what causes so much anxiety in the child.
If you understand what he’s saying (which isn’t usual), don’t have the temptation to question him for information.
You won’t get anything, because by sleeping, he’s not conscious to be able to organize his thinking.
In addition, you will hear what he is saying, but you won't know what he's dreaming about, so pay attention to how you interpret the information and the conclusions you draw from it.
Never tell the child that you can interrogate him during the night to know his secrets.
Not even joking.
It causes a lot of anxiety.
In conclusion, don't worry, it's nothing very nasty, it's an episode that lasts only a few minutes.
Illhaeusern... finally
Illhaeusern has finally found his way back to victory on his field after a long period of scarcity.
Without making a big game, the Yellows and Blacks created the greatest number of opportunities and managed to transform one for the gain of the match.
