Sarkozy meeting with angry fishermen
Before flying to the United States, Nicolas Sarkozy will make a detour through Brittany.
Faced with the show of strength of the fishermen who strongly disturbed Monday the accesses of several Breton and Norman ports, the President of the Republic decided to go Tuesday morning to the port of Guilvinec, in Finistère, to meet their representatives.
The latter have promised to welcome him "in a constructive spirit" and hope that he "will come with concrete proposals".
A presidential visit that falls at the same time as a general assembly of the "crisis committee" created by the Breton fishermen to obtain measures in the face of the surge in diesel prices, which reached a record of more than 1.14 euros per liter on average.
According to the spokeswoman of the Elysée David Martinon, the president "will discuss with them solutions that can be devised to help their companies regain competitiveness and to secure the conditions of employment and remuneration of sailors".
Nicolas Sarkozy is awaited firmly by the fishermen.
On Tuesday morning, they carried out snail operations in Morbihan and Calvados, causing plugs of nearly 10 km.
Quiberon's marina was also blocked, but connections to Belle-Île not stopped.
A filter dam has been underway on Quiberon Peninsula.
Sailors have already shown their determination on Monday by burning tires and pallets in front of the fuel depots of the ports of Brest, and Lorient, while demonstrations took place in Concarneau or Saint-Brieuc.
The movement has spread to other ports on the Atlantic facade and the English Channel, such as Les Sables d'Olonne, in the Vendée, where trawlers have remained docked, or in Le Havre, where about twenty boats have blocked the movements of commercial ships.
The fishermen of La Turballe and Croisic, in Loire-Atlantique, stopped in the evening the blockade of the important refinery Total of Donges.
Those of Boulogne-sur-Mer have decided to wait until Wednesday's meeting at the ministry to decide on possible actions.
In Guilvinec, the "crisis committee" must indeed decide on Tuesday the follow-up to the movement and its participation in the interview scheduled at the Ministry of Agriculture and Fisheries in Paris.
But some Breton fishermen do not want to be represented by officials of the National Fisheries Committee, which did not support their movement.
"The strike will continue" because "the fishermen won't let go" and "do not want to move to Paris", for example announced Liliane Cariou, the president of the fisheries committee of Lorient.
The professional fishermen, who do not pay taxes on diesel, are currently paying about 50 cents per litre but they estimate that the profitability threshold of a boat is only reached with a gas oil of less than 0.30 euros per liter.
Paul Newman The Beautiful
Unforgettable “Butch Cassidy and the Kid” died from cancer at the age of 83 in his Connecticut home.
Hillary and Bill Clinton, Nicolas Sarkozy, George Clooney, Julia Roberts, Robert Redford and Sam Mendes hailed the film legend and committed actor Paul Newman, who died at the age of 83.
Embraer Maintains Delivery Forecasts in 2008
The Brazilian aircraft manufacturer expects 215 aircraft delivered this year despite the threats of recess that hang over the global economy.
The world’s fourth-largest aeronautical manufacturer, Embraer, maintains its delivery forecasts for 2008 with 215 aircraft delivered, reaffirmed Fredrico Fleury Curado, its president.
In the first half of the year, the aircraft manufacturer delivered 97 aircraft.
The Brazilian manufacturer expects a lot from the Chinese market especially for its aircraft of the range from 30 to 120 seats.
To date, Embraer has delivered 41 devices to Chinese customers in eight years.
AIG would consider disposinging more than 15% of its assets
The insurer American International Group, which the U.S. monetary authorities have bailed out, plans to divest more than 15% of its assets in order to repay the US$85 billion that has been advanced, according to the Sunday edition of the Financial Times.
AIG is ready to study the sale of most of its activities beyond its international life insurance and retirement activity in the United States, says the newspaper which quotes sources close to the file.
AIG's board of directors meet in the evening on Sunday to consider possible assignments, according to the FT.
No confirmation could be obtained from AIG.
Assets that AIG plans to split from include its Air Leasing subsidiary International Lease Financial Corp., its 59% stake in reinsurer Transatlantic Holdings as well as its real estate portfolio and private equity investments.
No final decision has been made on these assignments, continues the FT.
AIG must quickly realize liquidity to repay the 85 billion loan granted to it by the Federal Reservance at the risk that the U.S. authorities will take 80% of its capital, diluting the current shareholders in proportion.
Analysis Paris History of the session Morning Meeting Historical Morning Meeting
The New York Stock Exchange blamed a historic fall on Monday after the House of Representatives rejected the Paulson bank bailout plan: the Dow Jones lost 6.98%, a loss never seen approaching 800 points, and the Nasdaq 9.14%.
Dow Jones Industrial Average (DJIA) dived 777.68 points to 1.365.45 points.
In terms of points, the largest fall in its history (684.81 points) had previously been recorded on 17 September 2001, the day of the resumption of exchanges after the terrorist attacks of 11 September.
The technology-dominated Nasdaq index yielded 199.61 points at 1,983.73 and the expanded Standard & Poor's 500 8.81% (or 106.85 points), to 1,106.55 points, according to final closing figures.
Already depressed at the beginning of the session, Wall Street sank after the unexpected rejection by the House of Representatives of the bailout of the banks which was intended to regain some stability in the markets.
By 228 votes against and 205 in favour, the representatives denied Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson the means he asked for.
Nearly two out of three Republicans rejected their plan.
"It's a bad signal sent to the market, which sees Congress unable to pass a plan," said Lindsey Piegza of FTN Financial.
"This shows how much the market is counting on this plan," she added, fearing that a project adopted in several days will come "too late".
“If the project is indeed daunting ... then the baton will quickly pass to the Fed and other central banks to manage the crisis, which would further tighten credit conditions,” said Brian Bethune, chief economist at Global Insight.
And accentuating more the sense of urgency, "every passing day brings the fall of a financial institution" to the United States, Piegza stressed.
Following the bankruptcy of Washington Mutual on Friday, the authorities organized the acquisition of Wachovia’s banking activities by rival Citigroup.
In Europe, the crisis has accelerated sharply, prompting emergency response to banks in the United Kingdom, Benelux, Iceland and Germany, a sign that markets are facing "not just a U.S. crisis, but a global crisis," the FTN Financial analyst added.
The bond market has risen sharply.
The 10-year Treasury yield fell to 3.632%, from 3.827% on Friday night, and 30-year yield at 4.161%, from 4.357% the day before.
Obama, winner at the points of the first televised duel
At Friday's first debate between the two candidates for the White House succeeded another to find out who, from John McCain or Barack Obama, prevailed.
Unsurprisingly, Republican and Democratic campaign headquarters have proclaimed their own victory in this televised duel that would have been followed by one-third of U.S. families.
Political analyst David Gergen believes John McCain's performance, while honorable, has not been "brilliant" enough to allow him to halt his drop in the polls to five weeks of the presidential elections.
The Gallup Institute, whose opinion poll published on Saturday grants a five-point lead to Obama (49% compared to 44% to McCain), considers that it is the Democrat who pulled his weight.
Among undecided voters, who matter, Obama emerged victorious for 39% of respondents, while 25% of them gave McCain victory, according to a CBS poll.
Obama should be able to confirm his lead on Thursday with the expected good performance of his running mate, Joseph Biden, during the first and only public debate with his rival, Sarah Palin.
“It would be a mistake to underestimate it.
She’s having good instincts,” Clinton warned this weekend.
The seventeen-year-old McCain kept putting forward his indisputable experience in foreign policy, while stressing the "naivety" of his opponent on these issues that were at the heart of this first face-to-face.
“Senator Obama doesn’t seem to understand that...” he accused repeatedly.
“I don’t believe that Senator Obama has the experience and knowledge to be president,” he said.
An angle of attack hammered since yesterday in republican television commercials.
Obama stressed that he represented the future.
Without dismantling himself, he was concise and precise.
He highlighted his ability to make the right decisions, and to restore the image of the United States in the world.
"McCain still hasn't figured out, while Obama doesn't," a Democratic spot criticised the Republican for failing to appreciate the impact of the crisis on the "street-as-you-gourned."
“During the ninety-minute debate, John McCain had a lot to say about me, but nothing on you,” Obama said yesterday in front of 20,000 people gathered for an election rally in North Carolina in heavy rain.
He didn’t even utter the words “middle class” or “workers.” The financial rout clearly benefits Obama, which is seen by Americans as most likely to save the economy at the edge of the precipice.
"The coming week could be a turning point," said influential Democratic Senator Charles Schumer.
Democratic Representative Rahm Emanuel, a former of the Clinton administration, compared Obama’s “good economic instincts” to those of Bill Clinton during the 1998 financial crisis.
For the Democratic nominee, the current debacle “is the eight-year final verdict of a bad economic policy implemented by George Bush and backed by Senator McCain.”
Reaffirming that he would still succeed in reducing taxes by 95% of Americans despite the upheavals of Wall Street, the senator from Illinois also reported that the achievement of a possible energy independence of the country through the promotion of renewable energies was his first priority, with the establishment of a health insurance system.
McCain wanted to build a fleet of 45 nuclear power plants by 2030.
Prudent, the two candidates dodged the questions relating to the financial rescue plan of 700 billion dollars (470 billion euros) adopted last night by Congress and extremely unpopular.
In Minsk, "have you noticed that there is an election?"
Young people and opponents demonstrated Sunday evening in the main square of the Belarusian capital to reject in advance the results of the legislative elections.
A few hundred Belarusians gathered on Sunday evening as soon as the polling stations closed in the central square of Minsk to protest against the fraudulent nature of the legislative elections.
While no results had yet been announced, many young people had come to demonstrate against the power of President Alexander Lukashenko: "the dictator, resignation".
Somes admit they didn’t even vote: “What’s the point, since they’ll do what they want with my voice,” says a kid.
They do not believe that the regime, which has done well for foreign observers, with the aim of regaining language with the European Union, is ready to make a democratic turning point.
Sanctions, including the visa ban for 40 Belarusian leaders and the freezing of certain assets, could be lifted if these elections were considered clean by observers.
They have not been according to the opposition.
"We have been kept away from the electoral commissions and our observers have not been able to participate in the recount," said Yuri Potemkine, spokesman for the Union of Democratic Forces, a heterogeneous regrouping of twelve parties.
"This is a cosmetic change," said Alexandre Milinkievich, a former candidate of opposition to the 2006 presidential election and leader of the pro-European movement, "because the important thing is who counts the voices, and at home, it is the executive power.
If members of the opposition enter parliament, it is because they will have been appointed, not elected.
But their entry into parliament can mean space on television, meetings in the regions, and legislative proposals.”
The opposition must therefore enter the political game because "we have to start the dialogue with the European Union.
For if our economy, which is heavily indebted vis-à-vis Russia, comes into crisis, and we do not reform, we risk becoming a protectorate of Russia.
We want to be independent.”
The opposition fears that the Organisation for Security and Cooperation in Europe, which has followed the electoral process, will recognise the validity of the vote.
She points out that turnout figures seem too high, and that the generalization of advance voting (26% of voters in Minsk) has opened the voice to all manipulations.
"Have you noticed that there was an election, you?" asked Social Democratic leader Alexandre Kozouline, one of the last two political prisoners released in August this year.
There wasn't anyone in the polling stations.
And there will be a maximum of five opposition deputies in parliament, those who best suit power.”
The silence of the night falls on the October square.
On television, President Lukashenko and the members of the Central Electoral Commission welcome their success.
The interview - Blood becomes safe again
Héma-Québec was celebrating yesterday the 10th anniversary of its foundation, and its president, Dr. Francine Décary, was right to rejoice because Héma-Québec has become one of the safest and most effective blood suppliers in the world.
And above all, he regained the trust of the public who had been seriously shaken by the scandal of contaminated blood in the 1980’s and 1990’s.
As is well known, national blood supplies managed by the Canadian Red Cross Transfusion Service have been contaminated with acquired immunodeficiency virus (HIV) and hepatitis C virus in the 1980's.
Faced with this tragedy and the growling of the public, the Government of Canada instituted in February 1994 a public inquiry, chaired by Justice Horace Krever, which aimed to shed light on the circumstances leading to such a tragedy and to make recommendations so that such a disaster does not happen again.
As Justice Krever was slow to table his report, some provinces set up a committee to plan a new blood management system in Canada.
In Quebec, the Gélineau committee fulfilled this mandate.
As a result of the Gélinau and Krever reports, two suppliers were created in Canada: Héma-Québec to serve Quebec and the Canadian Blood Services to supply all other provinces and territories.
"In 1998, when Héma-Quebec began operations, barely 38% of people still had confidence in the blood system in Quebec," recalls Dr. Décary.
The situation was daunting.
Through efforts to improve the safety of blood products and work in the field in the years that followed, we regained public trust," she said before proudly affirming that at the beginning of 2008, nearly 94% of the Quebec population now had confidence in Héma-Québec.
Dr. Décary, however, keeps in mind that "confidence is something extremely fragile."
From its inception on September 28, 1998, Héma-Québec applied the precautionary principle.
At that time, many cases of the human form of mad cow disease, a new variant of Creutzfeldt-Jakob's disease were hatched in Europe, recounts the one that has held the fort since its foundation in 1998.
"And we didn't know at all if this disease could be transmitted through blood.
We then relied on one of the main recommendations of the Krever and Gélineau reports, according to that when we do not know and that a serious disease can result, in this case Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease which is characterized by degeneration of the brain inevitably leading to death, measures of exclusion are introduced.
In April 1999 people who had stayed in the United Kingdom since 1980 were therefore denied blood.
However, a few years ago, it has been proven beyond any doubt that this disease is transmitted by the blood.
This episode has shown us that the precautionary principle that has been applied makes sense."
In 1999 appeared on the market a much more sensitive technique for HIV testing: nucleic acid (NAT) tests that make it possible to detect directly in the blood of donors the presence of the virus itself and not that of antibodies against the virus, on which the tests used until then.
NATs have shortened the mute period, which extends between when the person was infected and when the first viruses can be detected in the blood by our techniques, explains the specialist.
This period has been reduced to less than one week.
As a result, there has been a substantial decrease in the risk that some donors may be infected without knowing it and that they may not be detected.
These improvements have significantly lowered the risk of contracting AIDS through a blood transfusion.
Today, one in 12 million people runs the risk of receiving HIV-contaminated blood.
“In other words, this means distributing 12 million blood products before someone is infected with HIV.
As Héma-Québec distributes between 400 000 and 500 000 blood products each year, it will take more than 24 years before a case of transmission of HIV by a blood product occurs.
I think we can now feel comfortable receiving or prescribing a blood transfusion to recover health, "says the CEO of Héma-Québec.
"Our challenge for the next decade is to maintain the safety of our products.
You never know what pathogen will emerge in the system.
Just last year appeared on Reunion Island the Chikungunya virus which, like the West Nile virus, is transmitted by an insect in the blood.
We must be constantly vigilant, "continues Dr. Décary before stressing that in addition to a safe supply, Héma-Québec must also provide an adequate supply.
"Needs are increasing because the population is aging.
Since anti-cancer treatments are more aggressive, patients often need a recharge of red blood cells or platelets.
The ageing of the population also has consequences on the network of blood donors, which includes on average only 3% of Quebecers eligible for blood donation."
Any healthy person, aged 18 and older who meets the eligibility criteria, can donate blood every 56 days, i.e. six times a year.
On average, 14% of people who offer their blood are unaccepted because they have AIDS or malaria, for example, and if they have traveled to a malaria-affected country, they will be excluded for a period of one year.
"With these 3%, we manage to maintain an inventory that meets the needs of hospitals.
Shortages are very rare.
But it is necessary to renew the members of this network of donors as they age and can no longer donate their blood.
But it’s hard to convince people to donate their blood, but above all to hold them back and encourage them to become a regular donor.
“Today’s life being so frantic, we rarely find the time to spend an hour donating his blood,” says Francine Décary.
With an annual budget of US$297 million, Héma-Québec provides not only blood products, but also human tissues, including bone grafts, heart valves, eyeballs and skin that is used almost exclusively for burn victims.
These fabrics are analyzed, transformed and frozen before being stored at Héma-Québec which also manages the only public cord-blood bank in Quebec.
The umbilical cord that was once considered a biomedical waste is full of blood rich in stem cells that can be used to treat serious diseases, such as leukemia.
"A few weeks ago, we celebrated the banking of our thousandth sample of cord blood and we are planning to collect more than a thousand a year.
We should start offering some for grafts at the beginning of next year, "says Dr. Décary who describes this cord-blood sampling program as very popular.
"Several mothers are disappointed to not be able to access it", because Héma-Québec does not have the means to offer this complex and expensive program in all hospitals in Quebec.
Soccer - The Impact in the semi-finals
The Montreal Impact qualified for the semi-final round of the USL First Division playoffs, thanks to a 3-1 win (4-3 overall goals) at the expense of the Seattle Sounders yesterday, in the second-and-decisive quarter-finalist stream in total goals at Saputo Stadium.
Midfielder Antonio Ribeiro scored the deciding goal in the 90th minute of play.
After a first half without goal on both sides, midfielder David Testo and Rocco Plancentino scored the other nets of the Montreal team.
Roger Levesque was the only one to outsmart Matt Jordan for the Sounders.
Recall that during the first game of the round-trip series on Friday, the Impact suffered a 2-1 loss at the Starfire Complic, in Tukwila, a suburb of Seattle.
The Impact will compete against the Vancouver Whitecaps in the semi-final.
On Saturday night in Minnesota, the Thunder had a 3-1 deficit in the return game to win 4-3, but the Whitecaps still made it to the semi-final 5-4 round in total goals.
The Impact is expected to face the Whitecaps on Friday and Sunday, but the details of the series will be confirmed today.
In Brief - Kovalev's Victorious Goal
The number one trio of the Canadian is ready to begin the season.
The unit composed of Alex Kovalev, Tomas Plekanec and Andrei Kostitsyn animated the attack in a 3-2 win of the Canadian in the shootout against the Florida Panthers, last night, at the Bell Centre.
Kostitsyn, the first star of the game, scored a goal, Plekanec had two assists, and Kovalev had an assist in this win, the fourth of the Habs in six preseason games.
Kovalev gave the victory by scoring the only goal of the shooting.
“I’m not ready yet,” the Russian warned.
I need another game to feel comfortable.”
"He tried to do too much in the first half, noted Guy Carbonneau.
But I think he enjoyed the end of the game.
He had a great ovation.”
Charest bet on the North
The Prime Minister does not rule out triggering elections before the end of the year
As soon as the general council of the Liberal Party is over, Jean Charest went yesterday to Jean-Talon in Quebec, where the new Minister of Health, Yves Bolduc (left), tries to be elected on the occasion of today's by-election.
"Pushing the limits of our last major northern border": this is the long-term ambition that Jean Charest presented with visual force while remaining in outline, at the end of the general council of the Liberal Party of Quebec, in Lévis, yesterday.
Behind him, during his speech, was projected a kind of Mill with images.
Rotated in a loop, on three huge screens, spectacular shots of hydroelectric dams, mining, Quebec seen by "Google Maps", maps, etc.
Great means, such as during an election period.
In a press briefing, moreover, the Prime Minister refused to rule out that he would trigger the elections this fall, even though he pointed out that his schedule was very busy.
The "Plan Nord" is the fourth part of a "vision" that the Prime Minister claims to have for Quebec, that of a "new economic space", which includes an agreement on the workforce with France, an agreement between Canada and Europe and agreements with Ontario.
Often mentioned in the Prime Minister's speeches recently, the opening of the North - the northern territory in the 49th parallel - had never been presented.
However, the Prime Minister limited himself to explaining why the North was "a jewel with immense potential", but did not give any maturity, amounts of investment or precise priority in future projects.
At most a few avenues of what could be further developed: including hydroelectricity, the forest, but especially the mining sector, boosted by the growth of emerging countries and the reconstruction of public infrastructure.
The North contains "nickel, zinc, copper, platinum, gold, silver, iron, titanium, and even diamond", according to the enumeration of Mr. Charest.
This "territory twice as big as France", "it's at home, it's in Quebec.
Not only is it at home, but it’s in us,” he insisted, as if to show that he was asking for a national founding myth.
Quebec has built a lot in its relationship with the North, he pointed out: the gold rush in Abitibi, Quebec engineering, James Bay.
"It's up to us, it's our future," he added, after taking a theatrical break to contemplate the huge map behind him.
The North, 70% of Quebec territory, includes a portion of Labrador that Quebec has always claimed, said the Prime Minister during the press briefing.
"This is a traditional stance that all governments have reiterated.
“There’s been a border line that hasn’t had an agreement for a very long time, and when the opportunity arises, I reiterate that position on behalf of the government,” he said.
Moreover, "with climate change, we need to rethink our vision of the North," he said, referring to the north-west passage, which will open throughout the year due to melting ice.
In this new geopolitical context, the Prime Minister believes that occupying the territory is a duty.
What about the inhabitants of the territory, the First Nations and the Inuit?
The Prime Minister said that the Government of Quebec would be very attentive to them and that the project would benefit them, especially young people.
Two former ministers from two different parties, Pierre Corbeil and Michel Létourneau (former PQ MP of Ungava), have already begun consultations with some thirty communities.
An interdepartmental committee chaired by Benoît Pelletier and in which Julie Boulet (Transport and Natural Resources) has an important place has begun work.
The Prime Minister does not exclude a great meeting, "not necessarily a summit", but a moment when "we will give ourselves a common vision".
The development of the North would also take place in accordance with the principles of sustainable development.
The Plan Nord will be "concerted, ordained and respectful of the environment", he promised.
Jean Charest described this "vision" as being in continuity with that of Liberal prime minister who preceded it.
"Like the Liberals that preceded us, we thought differently to turn the challenges into new opportunities for growth and development."
This was also one of the recurring themes of the weekend, which began Friday with a big cocktail to mark the 50th anniversary of the arrival of Jean Lesage at the head of the party.
One of the adopted resolutions proposes to rename the Beauharnois Power Station as an Adélard-Godbout Dam.
"De Godbout, who created Hydro-Québec, in Lesage who made the Quiet Revolution and the nationalization of hydroelectricity, in Bourassa who made James Bay, our party was able to forge the future," said Jean Charest.
In a press briefing, he invoked another reference, conservative, John Diefenbaker's "Roads to the North", Prime Minister from 1957 to 1963.
Addressing the Eastmain1a-Ruper hydroelectric dam project, Mr. Charest stressed that its commissioning was planned for 2012.
“It stays between us there, but I’m planning to be there,” he told his activists, sparking applause at home.
Indeed, the Prime Minister, who said in April that there would be no election in 2008 and probably not in 2009, did not rule out the possibility of triggering an election this fall, what the law allows him.
He points out, however, that his schedule is fulfilled, in particular by a mission of the council of the federation in China and a Quebec-New York summit.
The General Council of the weekend provided the Liberal leader with a platform containing nationalist resolutions, including "constitutional aspirations" that have passed as a letter to the post: the PLQ calls in particular for the recognition of the "specificity of Quebec" in the Constitution.
He also hoped that Quebec would appoint members of the Canadian Radio-television and Telecommunications Commission and that they would have the right to veto the decisions taken in Quebec.
On the side of the opposition, we welcomed the outlines of the Northern Plan with skepticism.
The PQ leader, Pauline Marois, revealed the Northern Quebec Development Policy that the Landry government launched in 2001.
According to her, the Liberal government, coming to power in 2003, renounced to enforce it.
If he did, "we'd be more advanced today" commented Mrs. Marois alongside her candidate in Jean-Talon (scrutine that takes place today), Françoise Mercure.
The ADQ said it supported the government's adoption of a Northern Plan.
"But what we want is that it's not just a show," commented leader Sébastien Proulx, replacing Mario Dumont, who is returning from vacation today.
Letters - Disgraced Country
It was Mr. Vic Toews, former Minister of Justice (oh irony) in the Harper cabinet, who allowed himself last summer to tell Louise Arbour that she was a "shame" for Canada.
This reproaches this woman, who has waged relentless struggle for justice and who deserves all our admiration, says as much about the Prime Minister's entourage as about Stephen Harper himself.
Here we are in front of a government that is the onlone, among all the Western countries involved, not to have repatriated its national locked up in the Guantánamo concentration camp.
A government that ignores the fact that Omar Khadr was a very young teenager at the time of the charges against him.
The only government which, despite the fact that Canada has signed the Geneva Convention, denies its word and leaves a child-soldier languish in inhumane conditions.
The only one who relies on a parody of justice to assess the guilt of the accused.
Recall, as a reminder, that UNICEF, Amnesty International and the Canadian Bar Association, among other things, call for the repatriation of Omar Khadr to receive a fair trial in Canada.
It is this same government that turns a blind eye to torture: it doesn't matter if a young adult is now psychologically destroyed, probably forever after so many years of abuse.
We don't realize it yet, but we're witnessing an insidious erosion of our rights, our freedom and our "democracy", the word that Harper keeps brandishing and sounds so hollow in his mouth.
Now is the time when no more morals exist: we can abuse, torture, arbitrarily imprisone a Canadian citizen according to the goodwill of a hitherto minority head of government (what will happen after October 14...).
Many voters believe that they will find salvation in a party that advocates Virtue, the Law and the Order: they do not see that this government has renounced what made the reputation and dignity of our country.
Shame, Mr. Toews, it's there, in this renunciation, and not elsewhere.
Free opinion - Barrick Gold's Orwellian discourse
In its September 17 edition, Le Devoir published a letter from Mr. Patrick J. Garver, Executive Vice-President Vice-President of Canadian Mining Barrick Gold.
Pursuing an Orwellian logic, he claims that the world's largest gold-bearing company filed a six-million defamation lawsuit against three perpetrators and a penniless publishing house in an attempt to launch a "transparent public debate".
If it is true that judges are independent and impartial, this is not the truth they judge, especially in the case of defamation prosecutions, but the law.
Unsurprisingly, this fundamental difference is completely obscured by Mr. Garver.
Because, for Barrick Gold, is the best way to conduct a public debate to lead to ruin anyone who dares to support a position contrary to his?
Regardless of the outcome of the trial, the authors and editors of the Book Black Canada will have to spend considerable sums of money to prepare their defense and fight, with very unequal weapons, against an army of lawyers supported by the almost bottomless pockets of mining.
The tactic, in these unequal struggles, is often to ruin the defendants in order to impose an amicably settlement that will gag the opposition in addition to scaring anyone who dares to address the issue again.
If Barrick Gold really wanted a public debate, it was in front of public opinion that she should have presented her vision of the facts.
Contrary to what is done in several of the countries where Barrick Gold works, debates in Canada are normally done through the exchange of ideas and not with batons.
It was therefore enough for the miner to open her books, his files and to establish a real debate, through the newspapers, the media, publications and the funding of independent research teams that could have shed light on the issue.
But Barrick Gold does not seem to trust public opinion and rejects petitions and letters of support for the authors of Black Canada.
Possibly Mr. Garver could explain how he can at the same time ask for a "public" debate while despising the opinions of the same audience?
In the vice-president's world, it seems that this word refers only to hard-paid avocados and not all Canadians; this is a rather narrow view of the concept.
In a democracy, even detractors have the right to speak.
Which does not understand - or does not want to understand - Barrick Gold is that the public who supports the authors and publishers of Black Canada does it above all because it rejects the brutal way of mining.
This public is ready to hear Barrick and to judge, but on the basis of civilized exchanges of arguments, and not in the context of what many perceive as a gag lawsuit (continuing the "public debate", Barrick Gold has just filed a formal notice against the authors of Black Canada telling them to stop the use of this term...).
Beyond Mr. Garver’s surreal discourse, Barrick Gold’s pursuit is a direct attack on academic research freedom and the pursuit of truth, essential to any democratic society.
It denies, as a whole, the right to citation of credible sources and the debate on facts and interpretations, which represent the very basis of intellectual work.
If it is impossible to study and discuss topics that displease the rich companies of this world in a country like Canada, under penalty of repeated prosecutions, who can do so?
If Barrick Gold really wanted public and transparent debate, she could do so by following the scientific standards used by the authors of Black Canada.
She's got a right of reply.
The academic environment, to which we belong, has long been able to manage debates and disagreements and resolve conflicts.
Barrick Gold’s disproportionate pursuit clearly shows that, contrary to what she claims, she has no desire for a transparent debate.
It's a loss for academia, as well as for public debate in Canada, and it's time for governments to put in place mechanisms limiting such abusive lawsuits.
Wall Street Rescue Operation - Democrats and Republicans Get Along
The Bush administration and congressional leaders, both Republicans and Democrats, have finally come to an agreement yesterday on the biggest economic rescue plan since the Great Depression of the 30’s.
The new version of the US$700 billion plan to curb the financial crisis that has shaken Wall Street for months has yet to be endorsed by both chambers of Congress.
In particular, it provides for better control by elected officials of its implementation, new mechanisms to ensure the reimbursement of engaged public money, new provisions to contain the number of houses seized for non-payment, as well as a right of scrutiny over the salaries paid to the managers of rescued companies.
U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson, House Speaker Nancy Pelosi and Republican Representative at the negotiating table Senator Judd Gregg announced the good news a few minutes after midnight yesterday after a two-day marathon of negotiations.
“We’ve made great strides towards an agreement that will be effective in the markets,” Bush administration representative Henry Paulson said.
“Everything has been done in such a way as to shield the real economy and ordinary Americans from the ongoing crisis on Wall Street,” she said.
“That’s the fundamental point: If we don’t do that, the trauma, chaos, and disruptions to Americans’ daily lives will be immense,” Republican Judd Gregg said.
Negotiations had sprinted in the last few hours for fear of the effect that a new failure would have on the mood of investors.
The limited time that had been granted was the opening of the markets this morning.
Lately associated with the negotiations, the two candidates in the U.S. presidential election were rather in favor of the agreement, without failing, however, to express their dissatisfaction that one is obliged to reach this end.
Democratic Senator Barack Obama has agreed to add certain provisions to the agreement, such as allowing homeowners to retain their homes and the salary of state-survived corporate executives.
"When taxpayers are asked to take extraordinary measures because of the irresponsibility of some, there is no reason to welcome.
But these measures are necessary,” he said.
Asked on another channel to find out if he would vote for the proposed plan, his Republican opponent, John McCain, replied: "I'd like to see the details, but I hope so."
"The outlines I've read [show] is something we're all going to have to digest and that we're going to be able to move forward with.
The option of doing nothing is simply not acceptable,” he said.
The 700-billion plan aims to build with taxpayers' money the bad, risky and unsaleable assets of U.S. financial institutions.
It comes after 14 months of a crisis triggered by the bursting of a housing bubble that first led to the collapse of subprime mortgages before spreading to other more widespread forms of credit securities (PCAAs) and leading to a freeze on liquidity and disbursing banks and financial institutions.
It is a group of elected Republicans who had collapsed the first draft agreement, last week.
It is believed to have come out of this impasse since the Democrats have accepted that certain "toxic" assets of companies in difficulty are only insured by the government, rather than purchased.
In order to round the corners for everyone in Congress, it has also been added a provision stating that if the government were to assume losses for five years with this program, it could put in place new taxes to help it recover.
The new plan provides for the payment of a first tranche of 250 billion to which will be added another 100 billion if the president deems it necessary.
The payment of another 350 billion is expected to be endorsed by another Congressional vote.
In exchange for his help, Washington will obtain stakes in rescued companies that will earn it dividends in case of profit and that it will be able to resell when the turmoil has passed.
The federal government will have the opportunity to delay seizure procedures for housing related to real estate loans covered by this plan, as well as to set limits on compensation and severance pay paid to the managers of the affected businesses.
The architects of the plan would like to see him submitted to the vote of the House of Representatives today, and that of the Senate on Wednesday.
Despite all their efforts, it is not yet certain that their plan will have the green light.
Voices have indeed continued to rise yesterday against this project, both in the republican and Democratic camps.
“Obviously, Washington doesn’t hear from you,” Republican Scott Garrett, a member of the House Finance Committee, told its constituents.
"Yes, we have a serious financial problem.
Yes, there are solutions.
But not that of taking advantage of taxpayers.”
Facilitator of a small group in Congress nicknamed "the caucus of skeptics", Democratic Representative Brad Sherman presents things differently, but comes to the same conclusion.
“Four hundred prominent professors of economics, including three Nobel Prize winners, have written to us to tell us: Congress is asked not to rush and to carefully examine the path to be taken,” he said.
At the same time, in Europe there was once again evidence of the global nature of the crisis.
At the end of an emergency meeting, the three Benelux countries announced the partial nationalization of the Fortis banking group for 11.2 billion euros (16.3 billion U.S. dollars).
The intervention of the governments of Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg had been made necessary after the French bank BNP Paribas announced that it was giving up acquiring its competitor in the middle of the shipwreck since it lost 2 billion euros (2.9 billion U.S. dollars) in the crisis of risky mortgages in the United States.
Fortis employs 85 000 people and is one of the twenty largest European banks and the ten largest insurers.
"It's not lost money," Dutch Finance Minister Wouter Bos said yesterday.
In exchange, a right to vote is regained in the bank and influence.
It’s precisely something that savers and households appreciate now that times look uncertain.”
Meanwhile, on the other side of the English Channel, the British government seemed in the process of nationalising another bank plagued by the financial crisis.
According to the English press, the government is taking over all outstanding loans from Bradford and Bringley, including more than 41 billion (75.5 billion U.S. dollars) of home loans.
The Spanish bank Santander said yesterday that it would buy the branches and customer accounts of the British bank.
The Bradford and Bringley would be the second bank, after Northern Rock, that the British government would have to nationalize since the beginning of this financial crisis.
Paul Newman (1925-2008) - Death of an engaged icon
His beauty in the eyes of azure was for a long time his asset card, some will say his weakness.
Had he freed himself from the narcissistic mirror, the American actor with a charming smile?
Yes and no.
His modesty, his sensitivity, and a talent that was flowing from the source will also have distanced him from the register of power that gives birth to the true sacred monsters.
But what a great background runner.
Paul Newman, missing Friday, at the age of 83, from lung cancer on his ranch in Connecticut, remains one of the last icons of American cinema in the pompous years.
Less monumental than Brando, to whom he resembled and of which he was for a long some kind of rival on the screen, more cautious in his game, certainly more fragile, but also an apostle of freedom and the break-up of dramatic conventions.
Mingled blood often makes wonderful children.
He was born in Ohio in 1925, from a German Jewish father and a Hungarian mother of origin.
From these European roots will be born one of the glorious faces of the American dream.
Without disproportionate ego, the ultimate mark of quality.
Because in him projected a whole country in love with the self-made man, of the fighter of the Pacific war, wounded in battle, which in the early 50's studied drama at Yale then frequented the Actor's Studio of New York, before triumphing in the theater, on television, soon in cinema.
The same man who campaigned for civil rights was also the racing driver who placed second in the Le Mans 24 hours in 1979, coupled with a sex symbol, although a member of a model couple formed since 1958 with actress Joanne Woodward.
Disappear with him also the areas of shadow of this secret star, discreet, committed, allergic to the shards of the show business, whose enigma will not quite have been pierced.
An honest man in the noblest sense.
Paul Newman's disappearance gave the moviegoers a dizziness this weekend, as his great roles clash on the black screen of memories.
More than half a century of an ever-changing seventh American art marches through it.
Coming back and jostling in remembrance Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid’s charming cowboy (1969), the young man rejecting his sensual wife (Elizabeth Taylor) in The Pussy on a Burning Roof adapted from Tennessee Williams in 1958.
Closer to us (1990), we evoke his sensitive Mr. Bridge, troubled in his old age by a marriage that did not fulfill all his promises.
So many other Paul Newmans have made history.
Rocky Graziano's delinquent, tormented, violent, unforgettable boxer in Somebody Up There Likes Me (Hate-Marked), by Robert Wise, a role destined for James Dean, picked up after his death, which really launched Newman in 1956.
This sympathetic thug in the brilliantess The Sting (L'Arnaque), Oscar for best film in 1973.
Bitter and avenger in Robert Rossen’s magnificent The Hustler (1961), he will pick up the character on Martin Scorsese’ The Color of Money, which earned him his only nine-nominations Oscar in 1987.
That face through the ages was him.
He's still.
Eras jostled through his journey.
Young people know him mainly through George Roy Hill's cult Slap Shot (1977) in Reggie Dunlop, coach of an unorthodox ice hockey team.
His fans found him in 1994 in The Hudsucker Proxy of the Coen brothers.
With a final role as Irish mafia godfather in Sam Mendes’ Road to Perdition in 2003, the loop was completed.
Contrary to several film legends that dropped the trade early enough, Newman remained in the saddle for a long time, while denouncing Hollywood, its golds, its pomp and its concessions.
The French filmmaker Bertrand Tavernier considered him, not without humor, "as a living challenge to "underplaying" judging his performances too strong.
The profession has not always been tender for the handsome Paul Newman to whose the roles fell for a long time all cooked in the beak.
He, however, played less often the young first than the fringes: neurotics, alcoholics, bastards or hopeless, whose perfect features reflected drifts and revolts.
As a director, Newman revealed poorly known facets of his true modest sensibilities.
Her Rachel, Rachel, in 1968 (based on the novel by Canadian Margaret Laurence), with his wife Joanne Woodward in the lead role, drew a fine feminine portrayal of melancholy.
He who lost his son Scott by overdose dedicated to him his film Harry and Son in 1984, a work of modesty and sadness.
Among the rain of homages breaking yesterday on Newman, his great boyfriend and former comrade Robert Redford (they had performed together in The Sting and Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid) claimed, very saddened: "His presence made my life and this country better."
Generosity, love of existence are vibrating features of the disappeared actor.
Beyond his legendary career, a great activist for civil rights, ecology, independent cinema, a protester of the drifts of Hollywood also disappears.
We will have seen a lot of his binette throne on jars of vinaigrette, sauces, biscuits and other foods, judging the process of quite bad taste, although gag initially.
However, the proceeds from these sales were donated to his Children's Foundation in difficulty.
Newman, rather than sitting down on his myth, will be committed to the end.
He left his five daughters a letter containing these sentences: “Always and until the end, your father has been incredibly grateful for his good star.
It was a privilege to be there.”
And a privilege for us to have followed the journey of such a lucid and generous American artist.
Citizen model, in sprey and in addition.
What could Zuschlag &amp; Company manufacture in their bill factory?
Soon, on 8 October, hearings relating to the ongoing criminal proceedings against János Zuschlag and his accomplices will begin.
The politician and his accomplices are accused of scamming, in the form of a criminal organization, on a permanent and regular basis, resulting in particularly significant damage. Other charges have been brought against them, such as, for example, false and use of forgery.
According to the prosecutor's office, money from subsidies was spent for personal purposes, while the benefits were justified by false bills.
The money of the state and the municipalities, about 65 million HUF in total, went on the one hand in the coffers of the Fiatal Baloldal movement and on the other hand, in the pocket of some people.
Their method, also made public by the investigating authorities, was to receive the money from the subsidies obtained by issuing invoices of expenses, behind which no real benefits were provided and thus the associations never complied with their commitments.
According to the indictment, many of the invoices were false.
For the latter, they procured invoice notebooks at Józsefváros market, while they made the stamps in Kiskunhalas.
For the company names that would appear on the invoices, it was decided, among other things, during tours in the northern province of Lake Balaton, carried out by István Lados, at the request of János Zuschlag.
According to the latter's deposition, he had to spot pensions and restaurants.
For his personal consumption, he asked for an invoice, on the model of which they carried out the other invoices that they would introduce, among others, to the Ministry of Youth and Sports, the Ministry of Social Affairs and Labour, as well as the Municipality of the Capital to access subsidies.
The majority of invoices were created by István Lados and his accomplice András Őri, who, according to our information, was mainly concerned with drafting the files necessary to obtain subsidies.
During a "locking circuit", one of Lados' choices was a bistro.
According to the two invoices issued by the Zuschlag in 2003, this place ensured the restoration of a group of 90 people, members of the association Biztos Pont Egyesület.
However, according to the manager, it would have been impossible for so many people to occupy this place at the same time.
In the same way, in the summer of 2002, the invoice drawn up on behalf of the Jobb Jövőért Foundation, which would subsequently bear the name of Együtt a Harmadik Évezredért, swarmed with form and background errors.
A pension, located in Badacsonytomaj, actually had only three rooms, and a maximum of ten people could spend the night there. Yet, the bill mentioned one hundred and thirty customers.
Issuers of invoices with an overflowing imagination have also fallen into excess, when they mentioned the company Pigal Kft, also belonging to the Horeca sector. In their accounts, neither the name of Jobb Jövőért nor that of the Hoppá appeared, according to the information given to the prosecutor.
Győző Farkas, the company's manager, told hvg.hu that when the prosecution summoned him and showed him the invoices, he knew, right away, that they were false documents.
“The name of our company and our tax number were placed in the wrong place on cachet.”
"Beyond the form, the fact that according to the invoice, a hundred people stayed there, did not stick either, since this establishment has only eleven rooms, which do not have as much space.
In addition, the bill also mentions meals, and our restaurant has been closed for a long time."
Zealous invoice issuers have also not inquired about Budapest's travel agencies, being part of their choice, but organizing only trips abroad. Thus, naively, they have indicated stays in Hungary on invoices to be paid in cash, which also goes against the accounting practices of the agency.
Moreover, as appeared during the investigation, they also added VAT on these documents, while services concerning travel activities, whose destination is outside the European Union, are invoiced without VAT.
Similarly, the VAT rate of the four invoices drawn up on behalf of the Hotel Révfülöp payable to Jobb Jövőért was incorrect, since the company's services do not support 25% VAT as indicated, but a VAT of 15%. In addition, the hotel's employees issue only printed invoices, while the fakes were written by hand.
The prosecution's investigation reveals that the "issuers" also made mistakes in mentioning the transport of goods by road, which the company issuing invoices could not exercise, since its profile was investment advice.
Moreover, it is also for defects of forms, such as types of inadequate invoices and false stamps, that the invoices issued to the order of Együtt a Harmadik Évezredért Alapítvány, of XXI. Századi Kulturális Egyesület and Biztos Pont have proven to be false.
As in the case of the company Ravenna Bt. or the Kunfehértó sports course, which both deny being the authors of the invoices issued on their behalf.
The forgers also did not pay attention to the fact that HIB Kft., based in Nyíregyháza, was in liquidation at the time (in 2001) the Fiatal Városért Egyesület group was supposed to have a meal at home. According to our information, it is for this reason that the liquidator of the company denies having issued this invoice.
Deliberately established invoices
Another category of invoice issuers is those who were fully aware that they were being asked to issue fictitious invoices.
The Esély Baudért Alapítvány Foundation, at the request of the Fiatal Baloldal-Ifjú Szocialisták consortium, established an invoice payable to the Együtt a Harmadik Évezredért Foundation, for a sum of four million HUF.
According to the indictment, the manager of the company Collab Kft. had to issue false invoices by one of the defendants, Ludovic Györfi.
Similarly, co-operated with the Zuschlags, Biogate Kft., which is currently under liquidation and which in 2003 issued invoices payable to the Jobb Jövőért Alapítvány Foundation, Atlasz Egyesület and the Vörösfenyő Egyesület association, for an amount of about five million
No financial movement took place, either, since the invoices issued were subsequently cancelled, but this, the organizations avoided mentioning it in their accounts.
The next category of companies issuing invoices is that of companies directly related to defendants.
For example, the administrative representative of the managing director of Delta Trió Kft., dealing with the fuel and motor vehicle trade, headquartered in Budapest, but where he was nowhere to be found, was Iván Kubatov.
In October, November and December 2005, this company issued several invoices concerning the sale of computer equipment, amounting to approximately 18 million HUF.
According to the investigation, this equipment was purchased by the company Cash Investment Kft. of Kiskunhalas, which had been in liquidation since 14 September 2005.
The director of this company was a man of Romanian origin, to whom Iván Kubatov would also have sent documents related to the company, between 2002 and 2005.
According to what we know, Kubatov acknowledged during the investigation that he issued invoices to Atlasz, Hoppá and Együtt in Harmadik Évezredért Alapítvány, and that he pocketed their value.
It is precisely at Iván Kubatov's request that Romanian masons created the companies Fa-Bani Jos Faipari Kft. and Jos-Investment Bt.
In fact, none of these companies worked, which the accused explained by the fact that they did not receive work.
He still used these companies, since at the request of Maître Zsolt Szente, co-defendant as well as editor of the founding acts of the companies, he established invoices in their names, at the order of associations relating to Zuschlag, such as the associations Atlasz and Y-Generáció.
Kubatov has donated to organizations, 15 invoices in total, issued by Cash Investment, Delta Trió, Fa-Bani Jos, Jos Investment and Linaxtrade, for a value of 14.2 million HUF.
The prosecution believed that 10% of the sums indicated on the invoices as well as VAT went into Kubatov's pocket.
The man even used Szi-Beri Bt. to establish fictitious invoices that were used as part of Biztos Pont's grant application.
In January 2006, the Association Y-Generáció and Szövetség a Modern Magyarországért submitted an application for a grant to the public utility organization of the Socialist Party, Táncsics Mihály Alapítvány (TMA).
They obtained this grant for twice 500,000 HUF, but these amounts have not been used for the objectives indicated in the application.
To prevent this from being known, five fictitious invoices received from Works Team Kft. have been submitted to TMA.
Moreover, it is these invoices that were found during the search carried out in Árpád Kemecsei's office at the Ministry of Education.
During the hearing, some of the defendants will relate the method of manufacturing the invoices, as it had already done before the public prosecutor's office previously.
What could be really interesting would be that the main defendant, János Zuschlag himself, decides to lift the veil on the whole history.
So far, his confessions are not very detailed, and although he has repeatedly promised to tell the smallest details, he has not yet done so.
The question is whether it is in his interest, or, eventually, the Socialists would have reached an agreement according to which in exchange for the repayment of a large part of the fifty million HUF, Zusi would wear the hat alone.
Today, the latter is probably offended, since applications for his house arrest, on the basis of a lightening of his conditions of detention, fall continuously in the ear of the deaf.
While those who have caused much greater damage, and the perpetrators of non-reparable economic offenses, walk freely or at least wait at their own home for the end of the proceedings. He does not have this possibility.
The psychology of bidding: why are we spending more money?
At auctions, buyers tend to spend more, and overestimate merchandise.
Researchers in neurology and economics at New York University have succeeded in updating the neurological and economic foundations of this known and seemingly irrational behavior.
According to their study published in the journal Science, humans don’t want to be distanced during this type of competition, such as an auction on eBay, and that’s why they even prefer to pay too high a price.
This research has attempted to draw up a neurological pattern of this kind of "game" using experimental auctions.
One of the groups of volunteers played the lottery, while the other group took part in a playful auction, while using a functional magnetic resonance (fMRI) process, the researchers analysed the active brain domains of the experimental subjects.
In both games, participants could earn money, but during the auction, an essential element of which is the outbidding of one-third, the set under the cerebral stem, called striatum, moved intensively during the loss of the game.
This part of the brain, alongside several other functions, plays a role in the "self-stimulation" mechanism of the nervous system.
This is why, according to the researchers, the rise in prices can be explained by the flight in the face of the violent reaction of the striatum.
In support of their theories, economist members of this group of researchers pursued financial surveillance analyses.
During these, three groups outweighed against a partner, under various circumstances.
They simply informed the control group of the actual value of the product in question, and thus carried out their betting.
To another group, they promised that if, in addition, they came out the winner of the game, then they would receive an additional 15 dollars as a reward.
While the third group received these 15 dollars before the auctions, but the members of the group also lost that money, in case they lost the auction.
The difference between these two groups is in fact that the focus was in one on victory, while in the other, on failure.
By thus proving the hypothesis of neurologists, members of the team "against failure" were willing to higher bets than those made by the control group and the group "for victory".
“These results cannot be demonstrated by any existing economic theory.
Indeed, each of them explained the phenomena observed during auctions only by the pleasure of winning and the parade in front of the risk.
Only neurological imaging methods have helped remove the contradiction between the two theories by leading, now, to a third explanation.” – Andrew Schotter, the professor at New York University’s Faculty of Economics.
Hepatitis: when should we consult a doctor?
Around half a billion people worldwide suffer from chronic hepatitis B or C, while in Hungary, chronic liver disease is mentioned among the leading causes of death.
It is estimated to be one hundred thousand patients carrying the hepatitis virus in Hungary, but only seven miles undergo treatment. This is due to the fact that patients often do not know that they are carriers of the virus.
Hepatititis is a generic name given to infections affecting the liver caused by different viruses.
There are many similarities between their symptoms. Inflammation of the liver and jaundice are characteristic, but pathogens and diseases generated differ from each other.
Hepatitis viruses, according to pathogenic genes, spread mainly in two forms: hepatitis A and E through the digestive tract (e.g
For travellers, it is in any case recommended to be vaccinated against viruses A and B. There is no Vaccine against C.
Symptoms of hepatitis A appear two to three weeks after the consumption of contaminated food, in general, accompanied by poor general condition, weakness, stomach upset and nausea.
Then develops on the skin and white of the eye a visible jaundice, the stools become lighter and the urine takes a darker color. Healing can take two to three weeks.
Around the world, every year, one million people die from the most serious form of this pathology, such as cancer or liver cirrhosis caused by hepatitis B.
The main reason for this is that according to estimates, only one in ten patients receives medical treatment.
Hepatitis C can also have serious consequences: if it is not treated, it can lead to cirrhosis or liver cancer.
Most patients don't know themselves with the virus, because the pathogenic gene sneakily destroys liver cells. In many cases, the first symptoms appear only after 20 to 30 years.
As a result, early prevention is very important, as well as regular screening tests.
Contamination is much higher than the average among a population of three groups: people who underwent a blood transfusion prior to 1991, drug users and tattoos.
Currently, there are 200 million patients with chronic hepatitis type C around the world.
Since the disease can be detected by a routine laboratory examination, it is advisable to consult with one's treating physician as soon as symptoms of unknown origin.
The disease can be cured, if it is detected in time. The virus can be eradicated from the body, this way the risk of contagion disappears and the development of cancer is prevented.
September 30 is World Hepatitism Day, organized for the first time in 2004 by organizations representing patients around the world.
Its objective is to draw attention to cirrhosis of the chronic liver, caused by viruses, the causes of its development and the importance of the early diagnosis of the disease.
Is anyone who grows in God feel less pain?
According to the results of recent research, religious belief would function as a kind of analgesic, which would have already contributed to making the death of Catholic martyrs more bearable.
The British newspaper Telegraph published an article that researchers at the University of Oxford conducted an interesting experiment by analyzing at what level the belief in God could influence tolerance to the pain of the human being.
As part of this strange experience, 12 Catholic believers and so many atheists were subjected to electroshocks, while each of them observed a painting representing the Virgin Mary.
According to the results, Catholic believers have been able to rule out much of the suffering.
Thanks to a palpation technique of the human brain, it has also been discovered that Catholics have been able to activate the part of their brain responsible for regulating pain.
This analysis is the first in a series of experiments, in which academicians, scholars, philosophers and theologians participate from different sectors of the university.
More precisely, the experiment unfolds in the following way: an instrument emitting sparks and diffusing the electroshocks is fixed to the left hand of the experimental subject.
The researchers asked the participants of the experiment to look in a sustained way two paintings, one being the Virgin Mary painted by Sassoferrato in the 17th century and the other, the Lady with ermine painted by Leonardo da Vinci in the 15th century.
The researchers at the origin of this experiment hoped that the sight of the Virgin Mary evokes a kind of state of faith among the believers, while the choice of Da Vinci's painting, not very different from the former, was about its soothing effect.
The experimental subjects spent half an hour in an MRI scanner, and meanwhile received 20 electroshocks in four parts succeeding each other, while looking at either religious-themed painting or the more general-themed painting.
The Catholics stated that at the sight of the Virgin Mary, they felt like "safe", as someone "cared for" and they "calmed down, and peace invaded them".
At the sight of the painting of religious theme, they accounted for 12% less pain than when they looked at Léonard's work.
The psychologist Miguel Farias, who himself took part in the experience, thinks that one can achieve the same effects with any other strong image, in atheists, provided you find the appropriate photo.
“We should find an image against which they react very positively, such as for example a mother or father,” the psychologist said.
Tom Wright, Anglican bishop, praised the results of this research.
“The practice of faith should change, and in many cases, actually changes our personality,” Wright added.
After these excellent results, it remains only the question of what would be the effect on the regulation of the pain of the subjects if we show believers a Madonna painted by Léonard or by a painter of the same caliber?
Is the sight of a Virgin Mary of better quality causing better results?
Are you what you divulge about you
On the occasion of its tenth anniversary, Google says that it can be found in the bottom of people's pocket in the same way as the sky above their heads. It knows where they spent their holidays, what specialists they consult and what are their interests.
However, that's not enough yet, according to Eric Schmidt, who is one of the influential people in society.
While as far as skeptics drawing a parallel with Orwell’s horror vision, they don’t see the essence of Web 2.0.
Eric Schmidt recently told the Financial Times that Google’s main ambition is to gather even more information about its users. This is necessary so that the founders of Google can respect their initial goal that sounded: “Let’s gather in a system all the information in the world!”
According to the leader of Google questioned, after ten years of a global upheaval work, they are still only at the beginning of their objectives to be able to process all the information.
To the question: what will be in five years? Schmidt replied that he would like Google to be able to answer questions like "What could I do tomorrow?" or "Which is the work that would suit me the best?" However, the software will only be able to answer this kind of question, simple at first glance, on the day when they will know enough about users.
It is not perfectly clear to all Internet users that the words used during searches on the internet, the IP address of the computer that launches the search and the pages then consulted by the people concerned are precisely listed.
Through the Personalized Search program launched two years ago, users can, for example, store their own search history and can view what they have looked for, which they have clicked on. Thanks to this, in the long term, Recommendations, program based on the same logic, will be able to give much more personalized answers, and will submish products and services on the basis of our previous statements and our registered preferences.
What happens if someone uses my data against me, if they look at what my work is? ask users, who are worried about Google's monopoly of information.
In most cases, it is the potential power represented by society that can arouse fear, and it is not remorse, the fear of punishment, the desire for freedom or possibly the revolt against power that can arouse a counter-reaction among Internet users.
It is, however, a confirmed fact that the terms sought are not only recorded, but in justified cases they are transmitted to a third party, and in this kind of legal case, nothing protects the personal rights of Internet users.
The most resounding example in recent times is probably the coordinated action against Brazilian paedophiles, during which Google transmitted to the Brazilian government the information it had collected on the public page called Orkut, thus allowing identification.
Human rights-conscious Internet users were able to breathe a sigh of relief last May, when Google, armed it with the electronic legal protection agency EFF, has proven its right in court against the FBI. According to the ruling, in the future, Google will still not be able to be obliged by any point of the constitution to disclose information about the FBI's secret request of any kind.
(At the same time, the federal government has successfully obtained some 50,000 web addresses and 1,000 search terms that it had won in relation to the strengthening of the law on the protection of children, voted several years before. But in the end, it does not manage to obtain the lists of searches of private users, or the entire list of websites and keywords of the last two months.)
The judgment of Google is therefore quite contradictory: it is criticized by those who fear the transmission of their personal data, because they think that their intimate sphere and their private domain suffer when they begin to use Google services. Therefore, they attack them in the spirit of revolt that is manifested against multinational corporations proud of their conservative image.
Meanwhile, the U.S. geek culture and its “savages” bringing together enthusiasts, activists, and computer-enthusiastic users appearing in many countries see Google as the next generation of large companies, the “connected corporation,” as opposed to its rival, Microsoft, cataloged as an invader of the world because of Windows, even if both are fighting for the same goal.
This can be explained by the fact that Google, unlike Microsoft, does not ask its users for identification code, does not ask for money for its applications. The documentary code and the package of programs, continuously refreshed, has a much more accessible effect, younger and cooler, in front of the practice of the official software giant.
The reason can also be that geeks see very exactly the problematic relationship between human rights and the internet, and what Web 2.0, the most proclaimed keyword of recent years, stems precisely behind it.
Indeed, web 2.0 does not concern anything other than collaboration, that the fact that we intentionally leave traces behind, and that we use the communication channels at our disposal, and while the dimension of them changes one by one, the essential remains the same: we expose ourselves.
A young person, in general, can hold a profile on several public pages, from Facebook to Xanga, and he can even link them to other performances, such as Twinter, or last.fm, listing his musical habits. Then, he can link them to a tracking system, such as FriendFeed or Blip.fm. These young people can also keep blogs, in which their most recent photo galleries appear.
While we can read many examples, such as that of the girlfriend who understands easily at the sight of the photos of the morning, taken by the circle of common friends, with whom his unfaithful friend spent the evening, or that of the company that dismissed one of his employees, just for the sincere opinions that he wrote on his blog about top management, we can be on one thing: the term
Whoever lives an online life, consists of what he shows of himself, and this is how we can define the principle hiding behind the behavior of today's young people.
He wants to hide something, or at least, wants to try around Google’s monopoly, can do so (starting, for example, with Scroogle, using the Google search system, but, at the same time, intentionally leaving no trace).
The number of people who, deliberately, do not register on public pages so that it is not possible to find them, is not the least. The services of Google online, through word processing to image processing, reading information can be replaced by alternative versions, which are not attached to huge server parks and that do not assemble our best profile
Public opinion waits, without really hiding from it, the moment when Google will reveal its true nature.
The company, with its current value of US$156 billion, is worth more than IBM (US$140 billion), Intel (US$124 billion) or Coca-Cola (US$110 billion). AdWords/AdSense advertising domains threaten the turnover of advertising agencies, while services introduced at an increasing speed cause serious migraines not only to Microsoft, but to many other IT program creators.
However, critical observers find, alongside all this, contradictions having disturbing effects: for example, the top management of the company refused, in its entirety, the protests of the employees, after it has doubled the price of corporate kindergarten and after the company has judged to be too expensive the daily multiple catering, taken at any time. All this seems derisory to the fact that already in 2006,
According to one of the internal rules of the company, the new services are not announced only when they can be used. What is sure is that in the coming months and years, they will move the world on new points. While some think that the project of scanning and archiving books and magazines of Google takes on such important dimensions that the company will soon find itself in front of the Supreme Court of the United States.
The fact that Google is mounting a genetic database can give a good raw material to the next generation of sci-fi authors. The novel of the famous Cory Doctorow, written with Orwellian halftones, describes the future in which a man could be approached appallingly just through Google’s data.
Two minors helped to remove traces of Ózd's double homicide
"The police summoned two minors to interrogate them in Ózd's double homicide case. According to well-founded suspicions, they helped to remove traces of the crime."
The young people have acknowledged the charges against them, and for the time being they are accused of complicity.
The two minors, who, according to MTI (Hongriese Telegraphic Agency) are young girls, defend themselves free.
Complicity in murder can result in detention of up to 5 years.
The suspect in the double homicide of Ózd, a 23-year-old young local man, was remanded in custody Sunday.
The charges against him are multiple homicide, committed with premeditation and by interest.
During the investigation, it turned out that the 23-year-old owed a large amount of money to a 53-year-old man.
The suspect did not want to settle his debt and it is for this reason that he attracted his creditor last Wednesday to a site in Ózd where he killed him, as well as the little girl of the 9-year-old man.
According to MTI information, the young man premeditated his crime. The weapon with which he was going to commit his hidden crimes at the scene of the crime is proof of this.
The police examine the weapon and the circumstances of its use with the help of specialists.
The Warsaw Stock Exchange makes a proposal for the acquisition of the Prague Stock Exchange
The Warsaw Stock Exchange offered two hundred million euros, about five billion Czech kroron, for the redemption of 100% of the package of shares of the Prague stock exchange.
Ludwik Sobolewski, the chairman of the Warsaw Stock Exchange, confirmed this proposal that the Poles made although, previously, the Czechs excluded the Warsaw Stock Exchange from the circle of potential new owners of the Prague Stock Exchange.
The cause of this exclusion is that the Warsaw Stock Exchange lies in the hands of the state.
99% of the shares are located in the hands of the Polish State, which wishes to separate by more than 60% of its shares in a few months of activities.
According to the Czech economic daily newspaper E15, the Polish proposal represents the highest offer that has been made so far for the acquisition of the Prague Stock Exchange.
According to the Czech press, the Vienna Stock Exchange, the Nasdaq Transatlantic Stock Exchange, the New York Stock Exchange, the London Stock Exchange and Deutsche Börse have made an offer to date.
Petr Koblic, the director of the Prague Stock Exchange, said last week that among those concerned, the Warsaw Stock Exchange would be the worst new owner, and did not rule out the fact that this could lead to the disappearance of the Prague stock exchange in the future.
The value of the Prague Stock Exchange is estimated between 5 to 10 billion Czech kronor by market specialists.
Based on an agreement between the shareholders, some 90% of the papers would be sold.
The largest owner of the Prague Stock Exchange is Investment Bank Patria Finance, with about 25 percent stakes, followed by 14.7 per cent of the Ceská Sporitelna Bank and Tiger Holding Four a.r.l. with 13.5 percent.
The financial crisis from the point of view of the European press
In their Tuesday commentary on the international financial crisis, worsening day by day, the leading newspapers of the European press are already referring to a "Second 11 September".
"The global financial catastrophe, we owe it to the American president" - accuses the daily of Rome, La Repubblica. "The president has not even been able to block with his own party behind a bill, improvised hastily, aimed at providing relief to a desperate situation..."
In place of a new Pearl Harbour, as the billionaire Warren Buffett evoked, it is more appropriate to talk about a second 9/11.
Part of the Madrid press also sees the scapegoat in Washington.
According to the vision of the daily El Mundo, it is the shortcomings of the political leadership there that brought the global economy to the brink of collapse.
The danger of the collapse of the U.S. financial system is increasing every day, and subsequently the whole world is going to pay the high price.
Bush’s plan was far from perfect, however, according to the congressional vote, a real threat danger, one that Washington does not propose as a political tool to deal with the crisis.
The world economy hangs in a vacuum, just when in the history of capitalism, one of the greatest transformations takes place.
We can’t ignore the threat longer – says the Tages-Anzeiger in Geneva.
Some statements can still provide a lifeline for their staggering financial organizations, and thanks to the money of their taxpayers, they can save them from bankruptcy.
Of course, if even more banks are collapsing like dominoes, then neither financial means nor new improvisations will be enough.
In the long term, we need the states of the European Union to take little account of their national selfishness, and to centralize control over divided financial markets.
On Monday alone, weakened financial organizations received more than 100 billion euros - recalled the Viennese newspaper, Der Standard, which adds that fears that European taxpayers too will have to put their hands in the pocket, very deep, similar to the Americans in order to remedy the crisis, are becoming increasingly realistic.
So far, the EU has not really dealt with the crisis, which, like a tsunami, crosses the old continent from side to side.
The "rescuers" should not immediately raise one hundred billion euros, on the other hand it would be wise if they noticed in which case it would be rational to receive taxpayers' money, with the objective of preventing greater damage.
The intelligent car mimics the theft of the bumblebee
What method would be better to develop technologies capable of avoiding an obstacle, if not the imitation of an obstacle detector already existing in nature?
Such as bees.
In the astonishing CEATEC project, taking place in Japan, buzzing bees have given Nissan engineers the initial strategic idea, to inspire them about how they need to design the next generation of obstacle detectors.
In collaboration with the University of Tokyo, Nissan has built a mini robot car, a bio-inspired self-robotized control system of the BR23C type, which it has equipped with the qualities of bees, the ultimate objective being to encourage the development of a system capable of avoiding all obstacles.
During its thefts, each bee constitutes its own oval-shaped space, which in reality is very similar to our secure protective hull - explained, during the exhibition, Kazuhhiro Doi, the director of the brand's research and development and technological public relations department.
The faceted eye of the bee plays an essential role, since it is able to see in all directions, in an angle of 300 degrees.
To mimic the functions of the faceted eye, the engineers approached the idea of the laser rangefinder.
It identifies obstacles in a 180-degree angle and at a distance of 2 meters in front of the BR23C, it evaluates the distances and sends a signal to the on-board microprocessor, which transforms it into an avoidance maneuver right away.
The perception of the obstacle takes place in a fraction of a second. Then, the mini auto robot, imitating the movements of the bee, immediately changes the direction of its wheels by turning it by an angle sufficient to avoid the collision.
Contrary to any currently existing system, this avoidance maneuver takes place entirely instinctively.
"If it wasn't like this, the mini robot car wouldn't be able to react quickly enough to avoid the hurdle," said Toshiyuki Andoh, director of the Mobility Laboratory and the robotic car project.
It must react instinctively and instantaneously, since this technology corresponds to the most vulnerable and inner layer of the protective envelope, a layer where for the moment the collision is considered inevitable.
This whole process should reflect what the bee does to avoid other bees.
The process must take place in the blink of an eye.
Opposite bees, the difference cannot go upwards, downwards or diagonally, it can only be of two dimensions, and only in the direction in which the wheels can rotate.
In this way, in place of the infinite combinations that bees possess to avoid the other bees, in our case, we were only able to use the rotary movement, the acceleration as well as the slothing as maneuvers at the disposal of the mini auto robot
The BR23C uses Lithium polymer batteries that are lighter and whose energy concentrate is greater, which makes their lifetime longer than in the Lithium-ion version.
According to Andoh, it’s the instinctive intelligent reaction that’s paramount, not data processing, much less the ability to store.
This mechanism should process only a few incoming signals per second, and react to them.
It is not necessary to store them and to react with the intuitive logic based on the data of the previous maneuvers. In this way, it uses only a few dozen kilobytes of continuous memory.
We don't need the memory of giga-byte capacity, or even megabytes.
This is the first step towards the designation of an instantaneous avoidance technology. According to Nissan's estimates, this system will make its entry into mass-produced cars over the next ten years.
Another application could be its incorporation into the GPS satellite navigation system, so that it can guide the driver towards his destination by avoiding the obstacles in his path.
We even gave a name to the overpriced baby
The written version of the US$700-billion-dollar economic rescue plan, aimed at repurchasing the banks’ questionable credits, and using several times, is ready.
In the first round, half of that money will be spent.
Reuters announces that during debates extending until after midnight on Saturday East Coast local time, congressional leaders have agreed on controversies that have existed for days and days, around the 700 billion-dollar rescue plan.
“We’ve taken a serious step forward,” said Nancy Pelosi, Democratic Speaker of the U.S. House of Representatives, that all that’s left is to put the agreements on paper.
The compromise, born from the plan, is supported by the two presidential candidates, Barack Obama and John McCain.
U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson said in commenting on the results of the compromise that the program will work and will be effective, however other measures still need to be implemented. According to Pelosi, there is no question, here, of the Wall Street bailout, but of turning the workings of the economy in the other direction.
The written version of the plan was made public ahead of the opening of the Asian markets, Sunday evening at 8pm Washington local time (i.e. Monday at 2am, according to Central European local time), but did not cause a spectacular rise in the stock markets.
Monday, the House of Representatives will be the first to vote on the bill in the name of Emergency Economic Stabilization Law of the year 2008. Then, its fate will be in the hands of the Senate.
On Saturday night, President George W. Bush spoke to Pelosi, then the White House also greeted the news of the agreement.
“We’re pleased with the advances reached tonight, and we’re very pleased with the efforts of both parties to stabilize our financial markets and protect our economy,” White House spokesman Tony Fratto said.
On the other hand, the program diverges from the original proposal made by the Bush administration, which stated that 700 billion dollars had to be spent at once, for the redemption of securities relating to questionable credits.
From now on, a maximum of half the sum of 700 billion can be spent. The first 250 billion will be issued immediately after the passage of the law by Congress, probably on Monday. This first sum will be used by the Secretary of the Treasury to buy bad debts from the banks, while the use of an additional 100 billion dollars will be left to the discretion of the President.
The program will be overseen by an independent body, in which the president of the Federal Reserve will also participate.
Expenditure in the second half of 700 billion depends on a new congressional decree.
According to the law, the Treasury Secretariat will be able to put in place a combined strategy: at the same time it will be able to purchase doubtful debts, securities relating to bad mortgages and mortgages, as well as other types of "toxic" debts.
Competents in the department stated that redemptions may take place as part of an auctury, but also directly.
The rule gives companies the opportunity to take part in an insurance program, in which the Treasury Secretariat guarantees the tools at the origin of the concerns, for which companies will have paid a premium equivalent to foreseeable damages.
Henry Paulson said the law provides the necessary tools to spend US$700 billion for the urgent needs of the financial system, whether it’s the vast redemption of bad debts, their insurance, or avoiding the risk of the system leading to the collapse of one or the other important financial institution.
"We will take the necessary measures, to be ready for the application of the law, as soon as it is signed," he added.
On the other hand, the leaders of the banks in bad positions and waiting for the help of the state can expect that their severance allowances, simply called a golden parachute across the Atlantic, will be capped by the government.
Wage capping is mentioned only in very specific cases. Moreover, the limitations depend on the way in which the Treasury Secretariary proposes its assistance to the company.
The cap principle means that if the Treasury Secretariary redeems in connection with an auction, they will levy a surplus tax on the golden parachute, unless the discredited bank president retires.
Directly after repurchasing bad debts, the government will intervene aggressively to ensure that the amount paid to officers is not exaggerated.
In order for the image to be complete, it must still be added that the existing golden parachutes are not modified. The ceiling concerns only the new contracts, this to motivate participation in the plan.
This is the first time in U.S. history that legal caps will be in place regarding the appointments of the Directors-General - commented the chairman of the committee in charge of financial benefits of the House of Deputies, Democrat Barney Frank.
In addition, the state acquires a right of acquisition of shares in the banks taking part in the plan. In this way, the risk of taxpayers decreases, since they can even win in the deal as soon as the economy and with it the rescued banks will fall back on their legs.
The bill mentions that in the next five years, the President must introduce to Congress a proposal that will rule on how the losses will be returned to taxpayers, starting from the financial sector.
On both sides, there were obviously those who expressed their doubts about this plan.
That's not why U.S. citizens have mandated us, it doesn't protect taxpayers' dollars - said California Republican Congressman Darrell Issa.
Her Ohio Democratic colleague Mary Kaptur thinks it’s the same people who are rushing to take action, as those responsible for the Wall Street collapse.
Industrial prices continued to rise
In August 2008, domestic industrial selling prices increased by 0.1 per cent compared to the previous month, and 12.9 per cent compared to August 2007 - the Central Statistical Office of Hungary said.
Industrial export selling prices increased by 1.1 per cent compared to those in July 2008, while compared to those of a year earlier, they fell by 3.9 percent.
As a result of fluctuations in domestic prices and exports, industrial production prices increased by 0.7 per cent compared to those of the previous month, and by 3.2 per cent compared to those in the previous year.
Domestic industrial selling prices rose only to a lesser extent: by 0.1 percent, compared to the previous month.
Domestic selling prices in the processing industry's sectors increased only a small increase of 0.1 per cent compared to July.
In August 2008, the most significant price increase was observed in the leather and footwear manufacturing sector (2.8 per cent), with in the first place the manufacture of bags and belts with a monthly increase of 4.6 per cent, then the production of shoes with 2.5 percent monthly increase.
The level of higher prices for plastic and chemical raw materials has resulted in a significant monthly increase in prices in the manufacturing sector of products and chemicals (1.6 per cent).
Deflation resulted in the oil processing (1.4 per cent), manufacturing of electrical machinery and appliances (0.9 per cent), as well as a minimal increase in the wood processing sector (0.1 percent).
The prices of paper production, publishing and printing activities have not changed.
Other sectors of the processing industry recorded an increase between 0.2 per cent and 0.9 per cent.
In the sectors of the supply of electrical energy, gas, vapour and water, representing a significant weight in domestic industrial exploitation, prices increased by 0.3 per cent in August, mainly due to the effect of the increase in the price of gas introduced by the authorities since 1 July.
In a twelve-month comparison, taking into account the domestic sale of the processing sectors, it is the prices of the oil processing sector that saw the most significant increase (32.4 percent).
In the metallurgical manufacturing sector, price increases of 13.2 per cent were observed, while in the important area of food, beverage and tobacco production prices increased by 11 percent.
Price decreases took place in the textile and textile production (2.5 per cent), as well as in the manufacture of machinery and electrical appliances (0.3 per cent).
In other sectors of the processing industry, rising prices have changed between 0.6 percent and 9.1 percent.
Across the processing industry, prices increased by 10.2 per cent over the past year.
Prices in the electricity supply, gas, vapour and water sectors exceeded 18.7 per cent in the previous year.
Among domestic trade groups and according to the allocation of sectors, in August, it was the initiating sectors of profit on investment that showed the most significant monthly price increase (0.7 per cent).
In consumer goods production sectors, prices increased by 0.3 per cent, while those in the energy-producing and subsequent processing sectors did not change compared to the previous month.
In August 2008, compared to the same month of the previous year, there was the largest increase in the sectors of production of energy and materials intended for further processing (16.8 per cent).
In consumer goods production sectors, inflation of 6.8 per cent was measured, while for profit-initiating sectors a decrease of 1.4 per cent.
Export selling prices for the industrial sector, in HUF, increased by 1.1 percent in August compared to the previous month, while they experienced a decline of 3.9 percent over 12 months.
In one month, our national means of payment weakened by 1.7 per cent compared to the euro, and 7.1 per cent against the dollar. Compared to August 2007, it strengthened by 7.5 per cent against the euro and 15.9 per cent against the dollar.
Will we still need a visa to go to the United States next year?
Kinga Göncz thinks that there is a good chance that around 12 January, the U.S.-Hungarian visa waiver agreement will enter into force. This is what the Minister of Foreign Affairs said in Chicago, in front of the leaders of the local Hungarian community.
According to her, "Now everything depends only on when electronic entry and control systems across the Atlantic will be put into operation."
All then, she drew attention to the fact that in the new system there will not be an entry visa, but tourists will have to register prior to departure.
The head of Hungarian diplomacy will be in negotiations starting today in Washington.
He will meet the Minister of Foreign Affairs, Condoleezza Rice, to whom he will hand over the decoration of the Commander's Cross with the Star of the Order of Merit of the Republic of Hungary, and will have an interview with the foreign policy advisers of the presidential candidates, Democrat Barack Obama and Republican John McCain.
Barack Obama and McCain both support bank bailout
On Sunday, Barack Obama and John McCain, the presidential candidates of the two major U.S. parties, supported the government’s 700-billion-dollar bailout. However, there are politicians demanding the resignation of the U.S. Treasury Secretary.
Following marathon negotiations that lasted for days and days, the representatives at the Congress and the Administration finally announced, Sunday at dawn, that they have concluded agreements in principle on the bailout of the banks.
This financing plan, aimed at thawing the Wall Street crisis and the paralyzed credit market, has become the central issue of the presidential campaign.
Obama and McCain even present themselves, each as better fit than the other, to drive the country out of the economic crisis.
According to the most recent polls, with the highlighting of economic issues, Obama has again managed to get a little ahead. McCain's lead, obtained with the appointment of Sarah Palin as running mate just after the Republican convention, has melted.
At a campaign meeting in Detroit on Sunday, Obama expressed his hope that Congress would quickly pass the 700-billion bailout law.
He pointed out that the proposal contains the consumer protection passages that he also supports.
"It is now that we must act, with responsibility ... a danger threatens your work, your economies, entire industrial sectors and the stability of the entire economy," he said.
He did not fail to qualify McCain's economic ideas as outdated.
In an interview with ABC, McCain said: “We’ll all have a hard time swallowing it ... but doing nothing isn’t a possible path.”
The Republican senator said that in the proposal, are mentioned the creation of a treasury control commission and the ceiling of severance pay of senior leaders, and that it was, personally, very attached to these points.
Obama told CBS that McCain did not have a substantive role in the negotiations, while he was in daily contact with the Treasury Secretary.
Nancy Pelosi, Speaker of the House of Representatives, said Sunday that there are serious hopes that the House will pass the bill as early as Monday.
Press review of the German linguistic territory
The right-wing camp is intact, it is only at the heart of the party that voters have become more aware - this is how Der Spiegel, the low CSU score in the Bavarian country, evokes.
Haider is ready to govern Austria, to prevent the coalition of the reds and blacks.
Der Spiegel (spiegel.de)
In the internet edition of the German left-wing news magazine, Franz Walter analyzes the causes of CSU's dramatic defeat in his article entitled "Revolution in Bavaria devours his fathers".
According to this well-known political scientist, the erosion of UHC began long before the sad Beckstein-Huber duo took office.
Christian socialists have transformed Bavaria for decades and now it’s these same modern citizens who turn their backs on them.
At the same time, this change of time is the problem of all Christian democracy, and therefore also that of Angela Merker.
For the CSU, the failure was not quite a surprise, but on Sunday came to an end a process that was likely, just ten years ago: the indisputable hegemony of the national party ended.
Ten years ago, in 1998, CSU collected with 47.7 per cent, for the first time since the 1950s, with a score below 50 percent.
This was repeated three years ago in the 2005 Bundestag elections, i.e
The cause can be attributed to the "devouring his parents" modernization, established in the region in a consistent way by the CSU.
Sociologists have been announcing this for 40 years, but they have made great mistakes about the pace.However the secularization, the results of education and the establishment of Germans from the north and the east in the region of BavÁière, in development, could not remain without consequences on the fidelity of CSU voters.
A modernization goes hand-in-hand with increasing opportunities, and the consequences of such a policy are also visible.
The right-wing camp is intact, it is only inside the party that voters have become more conscious, more critical and more stubborne.
It is also astonishing that in recent weeks it is precisely the groups of classical citizens who have distanced themselves from the CSU, such as the independents, the artisans, the holders of the baccalaureate and the graduates of higher studies.
In these groups, support for UHC has fallen below 40 percent.
Die Press (diepresse.com)
In its article entitled "Which? With whom? Party presidents stand on reserve in the face of issues of the coalition", the conservative Austrian newspaper deals with the confusing domestic political situation that took place after the Austrian elections.
The Austrian SPÖ Socialists wish to speak with the ÖVP conservatives.
The ÖVP forbains SPÖ to meddle in its internal affairs.
The populist BZÖ party is being opened for negotiations. The Greens do not yet want to agree on the coalition.
The elections took place, now everything revolves around the coalition; during the debates organized on public television, party leaders have scarcely allowed their cards to be glimpsed.
Werner Faymann, SPÖ president, confirmed that he wanted to talk with the ÖVP.
The SPÖ wants to show that the collaboration of both parties is indeed possible - this is what already exists in some regions and in the socialist partnership.
The question that he would imagine a coalition with Wilhelm Molterer, the current president of the ÖVP, as vice-chancellor, remains open from Faymann.
As he mentioned, Schüssel-Molterer's orientation was, in any case, rejected.
A replica of this combination is out of the question.
Molterer reacted cautiously to the proposals of the reds.
As he has already said, a collaboration will surely be unsuccessful if Faymann tries to interfere in the ÖVP's internal affairs.
In his party, the choice of the opposition or the entry into the coalition is momentarily a completely open question. These points must absolutely be debated within the ÖVP.
Jörg Haider, the president of BZÖ, is once again open to all proposals.
"To avoid the coalition of reds and blacks, we are ready to assume government responsibilities" - Haider said.
Járóka said: We need to break the vicious cycle of poverty and segregation
Today, in Paris, the European Summit for Equal Opportunities, the host of which is France, began as a representative of the current presidency of the European Union.
In her message before the plenary assembly, as one of the main spokespersons of the expert consultation, Lívia Járóka, MEP Fidesz European People's Party, drew attention to segregations in education, because it is a violation of the law that most affects Roma children and therefore, one of the main obstacles to their success in life.
On behalf of the French Presidency, this high-level conference was opened on the one hand by Xavier Bertrand, French Minister for Labour and Social Affairs, and, on the other hand, Vladimir Spidla, European Commissioner for Labour, Social Affairs and Equal Opportunities.
Among those invited to take part in the consultation are the European social partners, as well as the most prominent representatives of legal protection and European civilian bodies, in the field of equal opportunities.
According to the press office of the group of deputy Fidesz People's Party, Járóka warned that alongside the distinctions observed during hiring, the Roma disappear from the labour market, in the first place, as a result of their low qualification.
"An overwhelming majority of young Roma do not have high school diplomas, and as a result of low-quality elementary education, only a tiny part has the opportunity to obtain a graduate degree."
Furthermore, Járóka insisted that education as the basic right of the human being plays a key role in asserting other human rights.
Investment in the early education of Roma children means a strengthening of the economies of the European Union member countries.
According to the MEP, it is necessary to raise awareness of these relations in the widest possible circle, also between Roma and non-Romas.
Indeed, the sooner a Roma child begins school, the more he increases his chances of becoming a productive member of the labour market; by taxes paid from his income and consumption, he participates in the state budget.
"Because of their nationality in the European Union, Roma must be assured of their right to quality education, and be protected from low-quality and sectarian education." - stressed Lívia Járóka.
According to the member, a collaboration of the European Union oriented towards an objective and action is necessary for an immediate acceleration of the desegregation process of schools. For this, compliance with existing anti-discrimination directives and in preparation is of paramount importance.
"We must break the vicious cycle of poverty, segregation and unemployed. We must give the possibilities provided by democracy to Roma children, so that they can participate as full citizens in the future of Europe" - demanded Járóka.
On behalf of the "European Year for Equal Opportunities" guaranteed for all, the objective of the Summit for Equal Opportunities in Europe held every year since the summit in Berlin last year is to provide a single platform to support measures taken in favour of equal opportunities and the fight against discrimination, targeted at European level and at the level of member countries.
CSU : grace for a period of transition
It is in October that the decision will be made on the fate of the fallen duo Huber-Beckstein
Despite this pitiful electoral failure, the heads have not yet fallen, and the debate on personal issues at the summits of the Bavarian CSU has been adjourned.
The president of the party, Erwin Hube and the head of the regional government, Günther Beckstein, can still retain their place. The question is how long will the grace last?
The fate of the tandem, having suffered a catastrophic loss of votes of 17 percent, will be on the agenda of the congress of the exceptional party, held on the last weekend of October.
Members of this much-critified pair, in any case, felt that they did not fit specifically at their headquarters.
Thanks to this, they worked to appease the furious members of the fraction and base, which one day after the "Bavarian tsunami" demanded personal consequences for the loss of the absolute majority.
Five years ago - it's true, still under the leadership of Edmund Stoiber, at the height of his career - socio-Christian union won 60.7 per cent, while today, a year after the coup against Stoiber, there are only 43.4 percent of their strength left.
For 46 years, CSU has been the "single sovereign" of Bavaria giving 18 percent of German GDP.
But the Conservatives are still heavily counted in this region of the south, of key importance to the Hungarian economy. In the future, they will be constrained to the coalition.
As one of the first signs of an internal reorganization of the party, the Vice-President and the Federal Minister of Agriculture, Horst Seehofer, presented as the possible successor of Huber, has also received a place in this modest recognition group, which initiates groping discussions with the FDP Liberals, as well as with the Free Electors, formed along right-wing values, about a possible coalition of the Munich government.
A strange nose of fate was that with the help of the list of Free Electors, enters into the legislation Gabriele Pauli, whose action against Stroiber served as the possibility of putsch perpetrated by the duo Huber-Beckstein, and then deal unceasingly with the very ambitious municipal politician.
Now CSU leaders are putting all of their energy into stabilizing, somehow, Beckstein.
In this transition, Stoiber, who until now pulled the strings behind the scenes, will also receive an active role. The former head of the regional government and former president of the CSU, designated on September 28 as the most bitter day of his political career.
On Monday, Huber made a few sharp remarks towards the CDU. According to him, they deserved much more support from the sister party.
Other CSU politicians interpreted the defeat as a warning to the CDU and also for Angela Merkel.
As a result of the Bundestag elections in September 2009, Merkel will only be able to be a chancellor again if they return to work again in one way or another, with a common force, the CSU, having played an important role in the national success of 2005.
According to Merkel, hardly hiding his disappointment caused by the fiasco, the loss of CSU ground will not negatively influence the Berlin grand coalition.
However, this is relative because it will not be easier to manage the wounded UHC, and the crisis of the socio-democrats always lasts.
Since 1946, the SPD has achieved its worst results with 18.6 percent in Bavaria.
Lukasenko - his rivals : 110 - 0
No members of the opposition came to the Belarusian parliament after Sunday's elections.
According to Russian observers, this is explained by a country led by Alexander Lukashenko, which assures him prosperity and stability, while according to the Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe (OSCE), this can be explained by illegalities committed during the counting of votes.
The OSCE, having sent 450 observers from 43 countries, does not recognize the elections.
Although the legislature composed of 110 people in this country of 10 million inhabitants has gone very well from opposition for many decades, before yesterday's elections, the opposition uniting its forces under the name United Democratic Forces (ODSZ) seriously hoped that Lukashenko, renewing his mandate as president for the fourth time in questionable elections, in the footsteps of the amendment of the constitution in 2006,
In this way, the ODSZ has renounced its originally planned boycott.
The fact that Belarus, increasingly dependent on Russia, would like to improve its relations with the EU, where because of the Belarusian political order described as anti-democratic, Lukashenko and several high-ranking politicians do not receive an entry visa, was one of the expectations of the opposition. Bielarus had to take a credit because of the decrease in imports of Russian natural gas
The fact that the OSCE, during the evaluation of the campaign, focused on positive changes rather than failures, such as the ban on running for one-third of opposition candidates, was referring to prior appeasement.
However, Monday's OSCE statement already disputed the campaign, during which most opposition candidates could not appear in the media, and in particular the fact that the opposition could not be present at the counting of votes.
Despite all this, the opposition still feels a palpable improvement in the political regime: political detainees have regained freedom, and Minsk is ready for small confrontations with Moscow, always refusing to recognize South Ossetia and Abazzia separated from Georgia. However, Russia with which Belarus forms a confederation on paper, considers the two independent territories for a month already.
"We have taken two steps forward, and one step backwards, but the most important thing is that the dialogue has begun between Belarus and the EU" - Alexander Milinkevich told our newspaper.
According to the opposition candidate of the 2006 presidential elections, it is desirable that this remain so, despite the fact that "the power was afraid in this parliament which has no weight, to bring in even a single opponent".
Instead, the opposition does not foresee protest actions in view of the results; rather, it is trying to send its message about the need for economic reforms among voters.
"Of course, we have a lot of worries on this side: we have to print our opposing newspapers in Russia, the local authorities are putting sticks in our wheels as for the broadcast, but I can also see that they are more and more in support of the elections, even in the circle of the elderly," said Milinkevich, referring to the fact that in the current Lukashenko regime, which considers it more and more
"Because of the indebtedness of the country and the rise in the trade deficit, the collapse of social security, 'evoking the silence of a cemetery', is only a matter of time," Milinkevitch said.
In contrast to the opposition politician, Russian observers called the elections democratic.
Serguei Lebedev, leader of the mission of the Commonwealth of Independent States, told Russian news agency Ria Novosti: "Peoples don't believe in words, but in deeds." In this way, the absence of the opposition is only proof of the maturity of Belarusian voters.
Brunetta: "Starting today, breakfast before work".
Never, in the history of the Chigi Palace, did not have revolving gates for the entry and exit of employees.
But starting today, is in force this new measure against the clever, against which points and will then drink coffee at the bar during working hours.
The unlazy novelty was presented today by the Minister of Public Administration, Renato Brunetta, and the Secretary General of the Presidency of the Council, Mauro Masi.
"Revolving doors - says Brunetta- are an instrument to better work, with more productivity, more presence and more services," says the minister.
Now begins a new season of accountability and transparency.
"This is reformism," Brunetta said.
The installation in all establishments of the Presidency of the Council is completed with the Palais Chigi.
According to Masi, "the choice of revolving doors is important even from a symbolic point of view.
No resistance on the part of the employees has been registered, assures the Secretary General: "It is a process shared by the employees, and we had the collaboration of all".
In short, more breakfast during working hours; more coffee or crescent.
Promise of Brunetta, which suggests smiling, "a good cappuccino at 7am is an excellent and healthy resolution".
During a conference at the Higher School of Public Administration
from Rome, the minister communicates that "in three months, absences of illness of public employees have been reduced by 50%, i.e
And he added that it was essential to make the Public Administration more efficient.
Italian, "whose wage dynamics - explains the minister - over the last 8-10 years have doubled compared to the private sector, and in addition to inflation rates.
At the same time, thousands of public employees joined together to challenge the standards of the Brunetta decree using the Codacons.
"While the Minister of the Public Service today spreads the data concerning the reduction of absenteeism in the workplace because of disease - writes the association of consumers - the people of "active lazy" continues to increase.
Indeed, in Italy, thousands of correct and productive employees of the Public Administration met to declare war on the illegitimate norms contained in the decree.
In particulature, in Tar, the exclusion of additional allowances in the event of infirmity because of service is contesteded; the extension of the schedules of the housing permanence schedules of the sick worker, during which control visits are carried out, as well as other aspects contained in the new provisions required by the Minister.
Accidents in the workplace, Napolitano: "Crucial prevention"
Every day in Italy there are 2,500 accidents at work, 3 dead and 27 people who remain permanently disabled.
In 2007, Inail data confirm that the white deaths were about 1,200.
Figures that testify to the seriousness of the phenomenon which remains one of the main causes of death, and causes almost twice as many deaths as homicides.
To have the attention of the institutions, the social forces and the means of information on this dramatic aspect, the Anmil celebrates today, in all the Italian provinces, the 58th National Day of Victims of Accidents at Work, institutionalized on the second Sunday of October.
"The disturbing data disseminated by Anmil, as well as the tragic events of recent days, confirm the importance of the subject of prevention in the workplace," said the President of the Republic, Giorgio Napolitano, during a message sent to the president of Anmil, National Association of Mutilated and Invalids of Labour, Pietro Mercandelli.
The Head of State expresses his "sincere appreciation for the continuing associative effort in favor of prevention in the workplace, the protection of injured workers, assistance to the families of the victims and the awareness of public opinion"
"Naturally indignantly, everyone hit their fist to put an end to such tragedies which, because of their size, arouse the clamour of the media and the participation of public opinion, even if daily reality presents us with dramatic cases, even repetitive in their dynamics.
Napolitano does not hide that "there is certainly a problem of resources: it is essential to invest in training and information"
The many initiatives promoted in the schooling sector are of particular importance for a more direct awareness on the part of young people entering the world of work.
It is our duty, to maintain attention towards this phenomenon, not to underestimate the alarm about social gravity, to apply and improve legislative standards.
For his part, the President of the Chamber Gianfranco Fini stressed how "this phenomenon of white deaths constitutes an absolute social emergency, which insults the consciences of each of us, and imposes on all institutions, to make a concrete effort to put an end to it quickly."
The data of the injured people on work seem to signal a slight positive trend, but this does not prevent that the number of white deaths is still impressive, "said the president of the Anmil Pietro Mercandelli.
"we do not hope that this "trend at a discount" is actually translated into a minor attention, because it is precisely when attention relaxes, that tragedy occurs.
"we believe that, in general, we do not do enough to contrast the phenomenon of accidents and the contrast can be achieved through multiple actions."
"In our opinion- stressed Mercandelli - more controls and inspections are needed, as well as a more important intervention on the training of workers, and this, from school."
- He added - "that we still have to make an effort with regard to communication and public awareness.
"But above all, a greater sense of responsibilities on the part of employers in the face of a phenomenon that represents a social emergency."
"Mercandelli then denounced "the gradual deterioration of the conditions for safeguarding the compensation provided for the victims of work" and the "drift of assistance to which the system is moving in recent years".
"The objective, therefore, is to give back to the worker the guarantee of insurance - he added - so that the destiny of the one who has an accident, must not depend on spontaneism or the enlargement of the legislator"
Player Mp3 pointed, they put at risk the hearing.
Listening to full-volume music for an extended period of time using portable musical devices such as Mp3 drives can cause permanent hearing damage.
This is announced by the European Commission, which, in a statement in Brussels, cites the opinion of the Scientific Committee, on the unforeseen and recently identified health risks (Csrseri) of the EU, published today.
"From this scientific report - let's read in the note - it appears that 5-10% of those who, for a period of at least 5 years, listen to full-volume music using portable musical devices, for more than an hour a day and a week, risk a permanent loss of hearing capacity."
"I am concerned that such a large number of young people who frequently use portable music devices and full-volume mobile phones, can subconsciously damage their hearing irreparably," Kuneva said.
"According to the scientific data - continues the Ue - this is a clear risk and we must react quickly.
The most important thing is to raise awareness among consumers and make this information public.
We also need to re-examine the existing control systems given the scientific opinion, so that they are fully effective and able to track new technologies
Brussels asked the Independent Scientific Committee to examine the problem, given the intensive use of portable musical devices and an increase in the number of young people exposed to the noise they generate.
"Scientist-concluding the EU- executive confirms that there are good reasons to worry, and that the European Commission will now discuss with Member States and other interested parties, what measures could possibly be taken to better protect children and adolescents, against exposure to noise from wearable musical devices and similar devices.
There is already a European safety standard that limits the noise level of wearable musical devices to 100Db, but there is a growing concern about hearing damage that could derive from excessive exposure to such sources of noise.
It is possible to properly limit these data using solutions such as the reduction of levels and the duration of exposure to noise.
Scientific Committee Ue points out that users of portable musical devices, who listen to full-volume music (more than 89 decibels) for 5 hours a week, exceed the current limits on the maximum noise level at the workplace.
Those who listen to music in such conditions for extended periods of time, risk a permanent loss of hearing ability after 5 years.
That’s 5-10% of users of these devices, and their number can be quantified at around 2.5 and 10 million people in the EU.
Rome, Alemanno gave the order to have the Commission leave for the future of the capital.
Today we begin, in March we end, in June we will draw the sums.
Here is the calendar set by the Capitol for the work of the "Commission for the Future of Rome Capital", which took place this morning, at the new headquarters, behind the Baths of Caracalla.
In six months, President Antonio Marzano and the Commission's 49 other components will finalise the guidelines for Rome.
In March when they have finished, the Committee and the Municipal Council will evaluate the analyses and projects, so as to present in June, the Strategic Plan for Rome with the "official stamp" of the Capitol and its mayor Gianni Alemanno.
"The commission is absolutely independent; all political, social and cultural ideas are represented - wishes to underline Alemanno - it will have to propose ideas for the development of Rome from which political decisioners, who remain the Committee and the Communal Council, may use.
No superposition of roles, but only the desire to have a corner where to develop a great project for Rome.
In March, it will have finished its tasks and will then disappear, because this experience must have a precise temporal duration.
In June-announcing the mayor - we will present the strategic plan resulting from the work carried out by the Commission for the future of Rome Capital."
For those who accuse the Commission of being useless, since its opinions will not be binding, Alemanno replies: "It all depends on the work that the Commission will be able to do, as well as on the quality of its proposals.
If quality is there, as in France thanks to the Attali Commission, as indeed in Rome, I am sure, its contribution will be fundamental.
Too often politics is overwhelmed by the management of everyday life and emergencies that manifest themselves from time to time.
We, on the contrary, want to see forward, and beyond, towards a deeper foresight, working seriously for the development of Rome and its future."
In addition, the mayor of Rome would like to point out that "this Commission is not a useless body or a bureaucratic whole: it is absolutely free of charge, and will not cost a euro to Roman city dwellers, and will serve to study and involve the best intelligences that express the Capital.
For Alemanno, in fact, "we need a draft strategic reference, neither firm nor immobile, in order to allow our city, to improve, what it absolutely needs, while thinking of its future now a unitary spirit,".
A future which, specifies the mayor of Rome, "will not limit itself to the usual communal borders, but which will look further" not only from the perspective of the future metropolitan city and its legal status of Capital, but "also in relation to the role that Rome can and must have, as the Capital of Europe and the Mediterranean".
Obama? The First Anti-American President. That's why.
A famous U.S. politician who wants to pass as an independent, wrote: “Barack Obama will be the second anti-American president.
The first was Jimmy Carter, who, because of his decisions, destroyed the U.S. economy and had relations (as he still has today, as a private citizen) with the enemy Islam.
Attacks on Bamba increase as election date approaches.
A lady in Miami wrote on a website: “This person is full of ambition.”
Obama is really panty: he thinks he’ll earn the highest office of a nation that counts in everything, two governors and five black senators!”
In reality, these are attacks that don't say much; they're obviously racial attacks, and don't answer the question of all voters: will he be a good president? We also have the polls, all of which state that Obama will win and with a good advantage.
In this case, it's still someone talking with the heart and not with the brain.
A Mississipi newspaper, in the bowels of the southern United States, wrote an article: once in the voting booth, we know well to whom the American patriot will give his vote.
There emerges an aspect of the American mentality kept secret until the end.
“Say what you want, we’ll see ourselves putting the cross on Obama or McCain.
There are even people who swear: if Obama wins, we'll leave the United States.
Things never revealed until now, to such an extent that the success of the black candidate seems almost certain.
In any case, despite all these reasonings, the winning card for the Democrats is George Bush.
1500 people were asked: what were the mistakes Bush made during his tenure? 89 per cent responded: Iraq.
America's changing.
In Vietnam, this nation lost 50 thousand soldiers and was kicked to the buttocks.
In Iraq, 4500 soldiers lost their lives, and the Americans were not forced to pack.
In spite of this, Iraq, for the most part, has been Bush's weakness.
Which will take George W's place will have a huge stake to accomplish.
Usa, the economic debacle is felt even among journalists: in the press, it's time for deletions. And many accuse the Internet, this "murder" of newspaper.
Five hundred U.S. journalists have been questioned by the PEW Research Center about the status of their profession and mind control for the future.
Well, almost all journalists today are pessimistic about their future.
However, they did not openly state to the information attachés, feeling less worried about credibility.
They don’t feel isolated as before.
The problem with American journalists is money, the money.
The 2008 crisis for these journalists is an economic model crisis destroyed.
In addition, deletions carried out in the newsroom, in the room where the editors are working to assemble the newspapers, are a big problem.
It is a problem that must be solved, according to the journalists interviewed, if traditional journalism as the general public understands, to be significantly reduced.
An obvious but complex pessimism invades American journalism.
Six out of ten senior executives and newspaper journalists are convinced that journalism at the national level is following the wrong path.
Locally, the executive group is at 65%.
Printed publication against broadcast.
Journalists don’t have trouble seeing where the web goes.
The largest publishing houses make their way to the Internet, perceived by many as "the murderer" of the newsprint.
The "economic pressure" that journalists feel is increased by up to 79%.
The 500 journalists interviewed admitted to putting themselves on technology hoping it would “do something good” for their journalism.
Today, the biggest hitch in American journalism lies between who runs the newsrooms—i.e. the directors—and reporters who work for them.
56% of directors at the national level, are convinced that reporters share their values, while at the local level, this percentage drops to 23%.
For some, this is related to the "safety of work".
Directors who expect to lose their workstation in the space of 3 years, are 47% at the national level.
As far as journalists are concerned, we go to 30%.
That of "sure work" at the staff level (our editors, editors) in America is still on the agenda.
Only 13% of journalists work under contract.
In a huge nation like the USA, with 300 million inhabitants and thousands of journalists, magazines, radio, television and the Internet, employers often offer what they want, and it quickly becomes a matter of taking or leaving.
Managers almost always receive large salaries, while the writer can earn 10 thousand dollars a month, in addition to retiring and paying insurance.
As for the holidays, the logic becomes complicated: x days after the first year, x days after the third, up to 15 days in all, spent a certain number of years.
However, according to their revelations, U.S. journalists are often "sick" during the year.
It's the climate's fault.
UNIPOL:TRIBUNAL ROME, DANILO COPPOLA REMAINS IN GROTTAFERRATA.
The president of the second criminal section of the Rome court, Carmelo Rinaudo, decided to reject the body of the real estate agent, Danilo Coppola, who had asked to be able to leave his city, Grottaferrata, for a home in Rome.
. Coppola is being investigated for bankruptcy as part of the Unipol-Bnl escalation training.
At his trial last week, he was granted house release converted into a compulsory residence in Grottaferrata.
His lawyers, Michele Gentiloni and Gaetano Pecorella, had asked that the real estate agent be able to travel the route of the provincial city to a house in the capital.
According to the court, such movements (that the pm did not grant) would allow him to influence his company and to have access to the evidence that accuses him.
Elites at risk.
The phenomenon had already manifested itself a few days ago, with the vote of the House of Representatives, contrary to the 700-billion-dollar bailout approved by the White House: a significant number of Americans were, and still is, more interested in punishing Wall Street than in saving the U.S. economy.
And it is the New York Times of yesterday that confirms to us that the current crisis divides the Republican Party, even more divided between its so-called East Coast, moderate, favorable to federal institutions as well as the business community, and its new soul, on the contrary, conservative, hostile to "those of Washington" and to the world of finance, powerful especially in the central and southern states.
The soul, precisely, that was felt despotically with the vote I was talking about at the very beginning.
However, what has just been quoted is only an example of the changes, of the great changes that the ongoing economic earthquake prenanounces or already lets run: not only in the United States, but throughout the West, and perhaps not only here.
In the United States as in Europe, the crisis seems to accelerate the phenomena brooded for some time, so much so that, in the new climate, they solidify and reveal themselves.
The first of these phenomena is the updating, the extraordinary revival, of the dual state-sovereign category, in relation to a kind of renationalization of the economy.
The crisis, indeed, is a crisis of banking-financial institutions that have, of course, close relations with foreign countries, but which, above all, are involved on a large scale, the balance sheets of individuals and families living in a single country, in a single state.
It immediately creates a political problem for those who govern it: that is to say, to respond to the difficulties and protests of individuals and families who are also an electorate.
In short, the crisis appears economically global but politically, it is almost only here national.
Political internationalisation seems really out of the game or, has little to say: the obvious proof is provided to us by the European Union which, shared as usual, between the various interests and between the various national strategies, cannot decide any common political line.
And so, it is from the government of each state that everyone expects interventions, rescue and recovery plans, new rules, and above all an affection of appropriations: when we go through a difficult period, there are only states that have economic resources, the mass of financial resources capable of trying to put things in place.
They also have the necessary means and legitimization to use them: two resources of inestimable value, elements which no market or international organization will ever be able to dispose of in a similar way.
This new emphasis on the state-nation crisis is moreover in perfect harmony with the ever-increasing importance that the more recent economic developments of the economy tend to attribute to a factor quite linked to this element: territoriality.
Paradoxically, indeed, when we were convinced to have now entered the kingdom of the network, ever more sophisticated technology, non-material, convinced that globalized finance was now destined to dominate the world, we realized at once, that on the contrary, our future will have to settle its accounts more and more with rather unmaterial things such as water, crops, oil.
Things which can certainly be transported from one place to another of the earth, but which are absolutely linked to a circumscribed space, to a territory.
Thus, who is sovereign, certainly possesses more cards than he who is not, that i.e., he who has the misfortune to live in a place without harvest, without oil and without water.
I now arrive at the second novelty which, however, provides for a fracture.
Who says territory, state, government, inevitably says Politics, and therefore Leadership.
In this respect, the economic crisis appears to produce two converging phenomena.
On the one hand, the awareness of the objective need for authoritarian leadership, the search for a person who knows how to handle the situation.
On the other hand, a wave of discredit for the existing leaderships, economic species that have proved inadequate and full of shade.
And in addition, something even deeper, in a certain sense, even worrying: discredit, suffering, an image of inadequacy, a sense of remoteness that tends to involve the entire class of directors in an increasing number of countries of the West.
Thus further increases the conviction of the various classes, before the actions are the same ideas of the social elites until now at their peak, their way of listening and being, their culture in the complex acceptance of the term, to have the same time, and to be ever more alien to the opinions of majorities.
in short, the demand for leadership, feeds in an underground way, a powerful and branched movement of delegitimizations of the ruling classes and traditional political orders, which manifest themselves in the improvised electoral "revolts" or else, in the changes of collective moods of the lately latter, starting from the conservative rise in Austria Bavaria, at the fall of Laborists in Great Britin, in the rebellion of the American
One has the impression that the traditional elites, their parties, their programs, but also their rites, their newspapers, their accredited intellectuals, their format, would say someone, always have more difficulty, which does not date from today, to understand and therefore represent the deep strata of Western societies, and that the economic crisis strengthens, increases and agitates potentially disproportionately.
In view of all this, talking about a current "mass rebellion" on the agenda, seems frankly exaggerated.
But it's said, keeping your eyes wide open, it's not without reason.
Supercolla to walk on the walls.
Defeat the laws of gravity thanks to a super glue and, in the future, walk the walls like Spiderman.
This is the goal of various American research centers, and today, a team of four institutes has almost succeeded.
With an adhesive measured barely 2.5 square centimeters, the researchers managed to keep a 100-kilogram body stuck on a vertical wall.
STUDY – The American journal Science has published the findings of some researchers at the University of Dayton, Akron, the Georgia Institute of Technology and the Air Force Research Laboratory.
The material, invented by the team of researchers, is an evolution of those we know who use a particular structure of carbon, called carbon nano tubes.
“The principle of glue can be imagined as a series of tubes arranged like a stack of spaghetti,” says Zhong Lin Wang of the Georgia Institute of Technology in Atlanta.
Scientists were inspired by the legs of the geckos, covered with microscopic hairs that make it possible to insure the animal, a perfect adherent.
Tests carried out on various materials, such as glass, Teflon, and sandpaper, have shown that the new adhesive is ten times more powerful than the legs of these reptiles, and at the same time, that it “easily detaches from the wall” explains Liming Dai, researcher of the Faculty of Engineering at the University of Dayton.
WALKING ON THE WALLS? – “This material could be used as a temporary adhesive” adds Liming Dai.
Maybe to walk on walls like Spiderman? Maybe yes, but researchers are thinking of a much less fascinating use such as the connection between electronic devices, since carbon nanotubes are conducting heat and electric current.
Other applications may concern aerospace engineering.
For now, no superheroes.
OTHER RESEARCH- The University of Berkeley, California, is also looking for a new adhesive.
In an article in the American Chemical Society, California researchers presented an adhesive made up of microfibers such as hair, which self-cleans dust during movement on an area to ensure perfect and constant adherence.
Medications: Never with fruit juices.
Beware of grapefruit, orange and apple juice.
These drinks, consumed mainly in the summer because it is desaltering and rich in vitamins, if associated with certain vital drugs, impairs the amplifying absorption or decreasing according to the case, their effectiveness, with even serious consequences for health.
David Nailey, a professor at the University of Western Ontario in London, recalls the risks of fruit juices, whether boxed or fresh, if associated with the ingestion of certain drugs; during the 236th National Meeting of the American Chemical Society underway in Philadelphia, he launches a new alarm: it only takes a glass of grapefruit, orange and apple juice, to reduce the absorbation of certain drugs
Grapefruit juice has already been heavily criticized because it is responsible for increasing the effects of certain anti-arrhythmic, antibiotic, anxiolytic, anti-staminal and neurological drugs, to toxic levels.
MEDICINATIONS A RISQUE- It is the Italian Medicines Agency itself, which had given the news in a two-year newsletter, alerting physicians and patients not to underestimate the danger deriving from this mix and to pay attention to what we drink, if we take drugs such as Diazepam or Methadone.
NOT JUST THE GRASSWEHROAD- According to experts, even orange and apple juice contains substances that inhibit drugs.
Longe is the list of active ingredients inhibited by grapefruit and other juices.
From there, the call of Michele Carruba, director of the Department of Pharmacology at the Public University of Milan: "These discoveries are in general, immediately reported to the various national and international drug agencies that will have to, if necessary, change the informative notes of the drugs.
It is always advisable to read the illustrative papers of the drugs that we take, and, if something is not clear, not to be impressed, but speak clearly to the doctor or the pharmacist, "said the expert at the Ansa agency.
The Nobel of Economics to Paul Krugman.
The Nobel Prize in Economics was awarded to the American Paul Krugman for his research on business models and the localization of economic activities.
Krugman, 55, is a neo-Keynesian who teaches at Princeton University and he is additionally a well-known New York Times columnist.
Krugman is one of the biggest critics of the Bush administration’s economic and foreign policy solutions.
A few months after the attacks of September 2001, during the days of Enron's bankruptcy, Krugman had written, in an article published on the New York Times, that the second event would have been more decisive than the first, in terms of consequences for the history of the United States.
A foresight that unleashed many polemics, and was blamed on the economist for years, but which has now proved accurate.
"I'm surprised and even a bit shocked at the speed with which the remembrance of scandals such as the collapse of Enron or Worldcom, disappeared from the attention of the public," Krugman said in late 2002.
According to Lorenzo Bini Smaghi, of the executive committee of the European Central Bank, Krugman is an "economist of great value", but also a divulger with a style" very aggressive, particularly critical of the excesses of liberalism of the Bush administration.
Open Office, this is the third edition of the "anti-Microsoft" package
It’s OpenOffice.org.3.0, the open source suite of personal productivity that aims to reach always and even more users, from home to office, from professionals to public administration, offering a free alternative to the most famous Microsoft Office.
Following an almost one-year work, carried out by volunteer developers, supervised by SUN Microsystem, the new Open Office package is presented to the general public with various novelties: from the welcome screen, and the revised and less austere interface, to others more important, such as the compatibility born for the Apple Mac OS X operating system, and the support for the new ODF (Open Document Format).
Open Office has paid particular attention to the interaction with the Office 2007 suite, and it is indeed possible to open the documents with the new extension of Microsoft (.docx, .xlsx, .pptx.)
Also interesting, certain functions specific to certain modules, such as the one that reduces the dimensions of PowerPoint presentations, useful for example, to send them by e-mail, or to import PDF files in the graphic design module, retaining the original appearance of the page.
Also on this front, it is possible to create "hybrid PDF" documents that unite the typical Adobe format graph to the elements of a spreadsheet or a text builder.
The collaboration between several people is an element that has had various improvements inside the applications: in the spreadsheet, various users can intervene, avoiding conflicts over the changes, in the elaboration of texts, because to each employee is assigned a comment color for better recognition in the revision phase.
Some companies are even working to bring some features of OpenOffice to the web (on the cloud), and in the next few weeks, they are expected to deliver the final result.
The Italian version can be discharged at http://it.openoffice.org/download/3.0.0/download300.htmlet and is in addition to the English version that has been on the mirror since Friday.
In addition to Windows and Mac OS, the sequel is usually available for the Linux operating system.
Upon request, the installation contract can be dispatched at home or at the workplace.
And if the theme of gratuitousness can be a way to disseminate it in homes, it is precisely in the business, that Open Office tries the big challenge, can already count on various projects carried out in the public administration and schools.
Not to mention the realities that offer training, consultation and assistance, ideal for small and medium-sized companies, which could also open up towards business prospects.
Open Office consists of five main modules: Writer, for the equivalent writing video of Word; Calc, is the spreadsheet that aims to replace Excel; Impress, for the rival presentations of PowerPoint ;Dbase, such as the famous Access, is the program that allows you to work on databases ; finally Drwa is the graphic module.
Goal for 2008, reaching 5 million downloads, and considering that today there are almost 3 million and 600 miles, it is likely that with the release of the new version, we can even exceed the expected figure
Kohl, too, is positive, this is the fourth case of doping.
New case of positivity in the last Tour de France: it is the Austrian of the Gerolsteiner team, Bernhard Kohl, qualified third, and winner of the jersey of the best climber.
It is the site of the daily newspaper L'Equipe which reports the news.
At the age of 26, Kohl is the fourth positive cyclist at Cera, the third-generation Epo, after Italy's Riccardo Riccò and Leonardo Piepoli, and Gerolsteiner's Stefan Schumacher.
The blood tests carried out on Kohl's specimens are among those carried out in recent weeks by the laboratories of Lausanne and Chatenay-Malabry.
If the counter analyses confirm the positivity at the Cera, the Austrian will lose his third place in the Tour and will necessarily have to expect two years of elimination.
The news of Kohl's positivity at the third-generation Epo, was confirmed by the head of the French anti-doping agency, Pierre Bordry, which told The Associated Press that he had notified positivity to the Austrian authorities.
The 26-year-old Austrian placed himself on the podium behind Spain's Carlos Sastre and American Cadel Evans, bloading the polka dot jersey of the best climber.
IMMIGRES:NEW YORK TIMES WONDERS IF ITALY IS RACIST?
Italy "as a country of emigrators has recently turned into a preferred destination for immigrants" reluctantly welcomed.
Is this turning the Beautiful Country into a nation of racists? That's what the New York Times is about, which has dedicated a lengthy article debating the theme of racism in Italy, starting from the case of young Abdul William Guibre, from Burkina Faso, killed last month in Milan, for stealing a box of biscuits.
This case highlights what the New York Times defines as the "paradox of the north": the northern regions are both those where there is "more integration and more work", but also "the strongest anti-immigrant sentiment in the country and the most important support for the North Lega"
To confirm the increasing tension between Italians and immigrants, the NYT also referred to episodes of intolerance in Milan, Parma, Rome, Ciampino and Castel Volturno.
Not only.
Even the new legislature wanted by the Lega to curb immigration and facilitate the expulsion of illegal immigrants, is cited as proof of climate change in Italy.
Tendency underlined by the Pope as well as by the President of the Republic: "Giorgio Napolitano during his meeting with Benedict XVI in the Quirinal, asked that the State and the Church work together to overcome racism."
Student victims of expensive rents, up to 900 euros for a room.
The student rental market has no crisis
According to Sunia's latest survey, the tenants' union, renting a single room can cost a student, up to 900 euros in Milan, 700 in Florence, 650 in Naples, 600 in Rome.
"The phenomenon has now reached very serious aspects to the point of excluding entire categories of young people, from the right to study because of the very high overall costs that families of provenance have to support - explains the head of Sunia's design office, Laura Mariani- in these cities, the strong demand on the part of students has so distorted the market that a process of widespread increase has begun even for residents.
In most cases, leases also involve a series of infringements: free, unregistered contracts, without rent limits, subleases.
For the Sunia, extraordinary interventions through the review of tax taxation are necessary to calm prices.
Reading in detail the survey, we see that in Milan, for a bed in the Brianza area, it takes about 450 euros; for a single room, we go from a minimum of 650 euros (Bande Nere zone), at an average value of 800 (Lambiate zone, Udine, and Fiera) to a maximum of 900 euros (Vittoria zone)
In Florence, an average bed, 350/400 euros, a room about 700 euros.
In Bologna, where students are concentrated in areas close to the University, it takes 250/280 euros for a bed in a double, and from 370 to 500 for a single room.
In Rome, in areas close to central universities (San Lorenzo, Piazza Bologna), we ask for about 600 euros for a single, 450 for a bed in a double.
Slightly lower values (550 euro for a single) for areas close to the other Universities (Ostiense and Cinecittà).
We can only save if we move away in the peripheral areas: 300 euro for a bed, and 450 for a single in the Predestina, Centocelle or similar area.
In Naples, for a bed it takes 300/400 euros, for a single, we spend from 400 to 600 euros, with higher prices in the Policlinico, Vomero, and Colli Aminei areas.
In Bari, for bed, it takes 250/350 euros, at least 350 for a single.
In smaller cities, prices are lower in absolute terms, but they are of major importance in the urban economy.
Finally, cities such as Perugia, in which rentals are often taken by extra-community students, there is a later anomaly: the rent asked of foreign students is increased by 25/30% compared to that applied to Italians.
Farewell old cinema Paradiso, cities eliment historical rooms.
Historical cinemas close in favor of the Multiplex.
"A city without cinemas is a blind city, it's like an eyeless face"
The image offered by director Giuseppe Tornatore, illustrates well what is happening in the major Italian urban centers, but also in provincial cities,
The process of marginalization of cinemas, with the birth on the outskirts of large multihalls, and the death of consumption of single rooms in the central areas of the cities, is one of the major events that seems inescapable and undeniable to us; it also has all the characteristics of a real cultural crime.
To disappear, they are often historical places and loaded with remembrance, "it's as if one of my friends disappeared, someone we know, with whom we have shared a piece of path and so many emotions" observes the director of the Nuovo cinema Paradiso, a film that offers Tornatore 20 years ago, the first Oscar as well as the Grand Juror's Prize of Cannes, and who told the poetry.
The figures provided by Anec, the main association of merchants, want to say everything: in the last five years, 316 cinemas of the traditional type have closed, and it is the cinemas of the main cities that have mainly paid the costs: 10 less in Florence, 9 in Milan, Naples, Bologna and Rome, 6 less in Turin, 5 in Palermo.
Among these regions most affected by the closure, Emilie Romaine is in the lead with 82 single cinemas lost, followed by Tuscany (44) and Lombardy (41).
The proof that confirms and that the first person responsible for their disappearance is the advent of the multiplex, because it is indeed the three Italian regions that have recorded the most new multi-sall cinemas: 195 more screens in Lombardy in the last five years, 81 in Tuscany as in Emilia-Romagna.
"Italian exploitation has developed irrationally," says Paolo Protti, president of Anec, "it is useful to build new structures, but it would still be necessary to guarantee the survival of those that already exist, because when a room closes in a city, these frequenters are not necessarily all willing to go to multiplexes.
Not to mention the missed deliberation of credit in favor of traders: the state's antecedent debt is about 10 million euros.
It is shameful that, from an ethical and moral point of view, the Government does not comply with existing laws.
To support the sector, it would take between 16 and 18 million euros per year.
Note the case of Bologna, the first Italian city which, worried about these closures, has decided to solve the situation thanks to the signing of a protocol between Mairie and Anec, the first of its kind in Italy, to defend and promote single cinemas of the historical centers and on the outskirts.
Among the planned regulations, there are measures of tax facilities in favour of managers and benefits for the public in terms of mobility: spectators will be able to enter the restricted traffic area, and will have special rates for parking and some car parks.
"It's an initiative that I like" observes Giuseppe Tornatore "and I hope that other cities will follow the example of Bologna putting in place other measures.
It is obvious, however, that in order to contrast the superpower of multiplexes, traditional rooms also need to improve the quality of sound and image, because today's audience is more demanding.
The defense of traditional cinema is a theme that for me, has always been important: the closure of the Supercinema of Bagheria, the place of my childhood and adolescence, was the spark that convinced me to make "New Paradiso cinema" that I was already thinking about for 10years.
I have lived in Rome for 25 years, and when I pass in front of the Rialto, or the Quirinal of Via Nazionale, now closed, where I have experienced so many shared emotions, I turn on the other side not to see the panels blackened by the dust.
Links between politics and ‘ndranghetá, arrested mayor of Gioia Tauro.
“The mayor of Gioia Tauro was willing to standardize the choices of the Town Hall to the interests of the Piromalli clan.”
Heavily accusations are made by the magistrates of the Anti-Mafia Directorate of Calabria, against the mayor and vice-mayor of Gioia Tauro, Giorgio Dal Torrione, as well as Rosario Schiavione, and the mayor of Rosarno Carlo Martelli, arrested this morning for external competition for mafia association.
The controls also for Gioacchino Piromalli, the former mediator between the clan and the public administrations, and his grandson lawyer, homonymous of the boss, already convicted for association of criminals of mafia type, as well as to pay 10 million euros.
"an affront to legal and moral normatives, and above all common sense," write the prosecutors, the two local administrations had expressed the willingness to pay consultations to Master Piromalli.
"Thus we would have been granted to the clan - write the judges - to officially enter inside the town halls facilitating the already considerable possibilities of control and orientation of the public administration"
The president of the Calabrian regional committee Agazio Loiero, does not hide only "collusions between local committees and organized crime, there is more than one" in his region: "there are places where mafia intensity is stronger than in others, where collusion is almost inevitable.
But there are also a lot of politicians who are resisting the pressures of andrangheta, heroes who are honest
This morning's arrests follow those of last July, after which the police announced to have beheaded the Piromalli clan.
By the end of last April, the municipal council of Gioia Tauro had been dissolved; already in 1991, and still because of presumed conditionings between organized crime and the local administration, local politicians had been relieved of their function of administering the City.
Giorgio Dal Torrione, mayor of the UDC at the time of the dissolution, had been appointed leader of a center-right coalition, after the ballast in May 2006.
He shouted scandal when the prefecture ordered the dissolution of the municipal council.
He accused the Deputy Minister of the Interior at the time, Marco Minniti, accusing him of having engineered everything for a political question, but given the request for incarceration signed this morning, the judges explain that the Piromalli clan had even managed to ask for the variation of the project of the A3.
Starting from the interceptions ordered against Dal Torrione, the pm write, emerged "the readiness to marry the requirements of a businessman, representing a more well-known group, close to the Piromalli, standardizing the choices of the public administration to the interests of the clan.
When the energy is born of the waves, now the bigs get started.
This is an disproportionate deposit that occupies more than 70% of the area of the planet and now the world's major energy companies are discovering it.
Waves, currents, tides, these are the new renewable sources on which colossos like Chevron have put their eyes, and now are trying to get their hands on it.
The possibility of obtaining energy from the movements of the sea has now been studied for a hundred years, and in the 70's, in conjunction with the first oil crisis, research has intensified.
When the price of oil droped, around 1980, the idea of exploiting marine energy was set aside, but now the environmental authorities and the new increase in oil gave the sector great growth
The World Energy Council (WEC) of London estimated in 2007, that 15% of the global need for electricity could be covered by sea swell operating systems.
This quantity would be double that produced by nuclear power plants.
Besides, there would be currents and tides.
Colosses on attack.
The first company to focus on marine energy was the Enersis, which in 2005 signed an agreement to build a facility in northern Portugal.
When the agreement was announced, it would have been said a real revolutionary step.
Two years before that, more and more candidates among these companies always dedicated to traditional energy rely on marine energy.
“Chevron is currently studying ocean energy technologies – said the spokesman for a company associated with the U.S. oil colossus, the Wall Street Newspaper – and she’s evaluating opportunities to cooperate with us.”
The co-operation in question, concerns the obtaining of a permit deliberated by the U.S. Energy Commission, necessary to give rise to a plan for the exploitation of marine energy in Alaska.
This is a very interesting signal, especially considering that precisely in Alaska, the U.S. continues to extract oil, and recently President Bush has put in place a project for new excavations
In addition to Chevron, there is Verdant Power Inc. from New York and Ocean Power from New Jersey, which roam the path of energy from the sea, and the sums invested until today, although their details are not known, according to observers, an increasing interest.
Tecnology to develop.
Experts are talking about a technology still in its infancy, due to the fact that marine energy does not dispel fears about the environmental impact.
At present, systems such as those in Portugal, or some in Scotland, are small, and do not seem to create a major problem for the ecosystem.
To produce energy on a large scale, however, it is necessary to use technologies with a superior environmental impact.
“And the choices are diverse: Ocean Power, for example, uses a system of buoys connected to each other.
The thrust of the waves, which moves the sludge upwards and downwards, is converted into a hydraulic pressure of the pistons and cylinders inside the floats.
The pressure rotates a turbine which, in turn, feeds a generator.
Energy is sent to shore through submarine cables.
For now, we have four systems of this kind during the project phase, awaiting a single federal approval and some of the energy could be used for a military facility.
Verdant Power, on the other hand, already produces energy for a shopping mall and a parking lot using six underwater turbines in New York’s East River.
The movement of water during tides, rotates the blades of the turbines, creating a rotary movement that feeds a generator.
Verdant has announced that it has a long list of customers waiting for the necessary permissions to draw energy.
After the waves, the tides.
Of particular interest, they tell the Institute for the Research of Electrical Energy, deserves at this moment, the potential of the tides.
Although it is an intermittent phenomenon, the tide is more predictable from the wind, the sun, or waves, and therefore more reliable as a source of energy.
And it is precisely on tidal energy that states such as New York, Maine and Alaska, along with others, look to the coasts, have invested 7 and a half million dollars in 2008 and plans investments up to 35 million in 2009.
